window.NOTES_PROBLEMS=[{"id":"osn/cpu/practice-problems-lectures-01-to-07#p-c1","u":"osn/cpu/practice-problems-lectures-01-to-07.html#p-c1","l":"C1","s":"","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"cpu","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 01 to 07","q":"<p id=\"p-c1\"><strong>C1.</strong> How many processes exist in total (including the original) after this runs? Draw the process tree.</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-c\">for (int i = 0; i &lt; 3; i++)\n    fork();\n</code></pre>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em> <strong>8 processes</strong> (2³). Every process that exists when <code>fork()</code> runs is duplicated, and the child continues the loop from the same <code>i</code>. Call the original P.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>At i = 0, P creates C1.</li>\n<li>At i = 1, P creates C2, and C1 creates C1a.</li>\n<li>At i = 2, P creates C3, C1 creates C1b, C2 creates C2a, and C1a creates C1a-i.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>That gives 1 + 1 + 2 + 4 = 8.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/cpu/practice-problems-lectures-01-to-07#p-c2","u":"osn/cpu/practice-problems-lectures-01-to-07.html#p-c2","l":"C2","s":"","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"cpu","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 01 to 07","q":"<p id=\"p-c2\"><strong>C2.</strong> What does this print, and in what order? Could the order ever change?</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-c\">int x = 5;\nint rc = fork();\nif (rc == 0) {\n    x += 10;\n    printf(&quot;child %d\\n&quot;, x);\n} else {\n    wait(NULL);\n    x -= 1;\n    printf(&quot;parent %d\\n&quot;, x);\n}\n</code></pre>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em> It prints <code>child 15</code>, then <code>parent 4</code>, <strong>always in that order</strong>. The parent calls <code>wait(NULL)</code> before printing, so it cannot print until the child has exited. The child's change to <code>x</code> does not affect the parent's <code>x</code>, because after <code>fork</code> each process has its own copy of the address space. The parent's 5 becomes 4.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/cpu/practice-problems-lectures-01-to-07#p-c3","u":"osn/cpu/practice-problems-lectures-01-to-07.html#p-c3","l":"C3","s":"","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"cpu","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 01 to 07","q":"<p id=\"p-c3\"><strong>C3.</strong> A program calls <code>execvp(&quot;ls&quot;, args)</code> and the next line is <code>printf(&quot;done\\n&quot;)</code>. When does &quot;done&quot; print?</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em> <strong>Never, if <code>exec</code> succeeds.</strong> <code>exec</code> replaces the calling process's code, data, heap and stack with the new program, so the old program, including the <code>printf</code> line, no longer exists. <code>exec</code> only returns if it fails (for example, the file is not found), and then &quot;done&quot; prints.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/cpu/practice-problems-lectures-01-to-07#p-c4","u":"osn/cpu/practice-problems-lectures-01-to-07.html#p-c4","l":"C4","s":"","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"cpu","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 01 to 07","q":"<p id=\"p-c4\"><strong>C4.</strong> Explain, step by step, how a shell runs <code>ls &gt; out.txt</code> using <code>fork</code>, <code>close</code>, <code>open</code> and <code>exec</code>. Why is it important that fork and exec are separate calls?</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em> The steps:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>The shell calls <code>fork()</code>.</li>\n<li>The child calls <code>close(1)</code>, which frees file descriptor 1 (standard output).</li>\n<li>The child calls <code>open(&quot;out.txt&quot;, O_CREAT | O_WRONLY | O_TRUNC, ...)</code>. <code>open</code> always returns the <strong>lowest free descriptor</strong>, which is now 1.</li>\n<li>The child calls <code>exec(&quot;ls&quot;, ...)</code>. <code>ls</code> writes to descriptor 1 as usual, but descriptor 1 is now the file.</li>\n<li>The parent shell calls <code>wait()</code>, then prints the next prompt.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Because fork and exec are separate, the child can change its own environment (open files, redirections, pipes) in the gap <em>after</em> <code>fork</code> and <em>before</em> <code>exec</code>, without touching the shell itself and without <code>ls</code> knowing anything about it.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/cpu/practice-problems-lectures-01-to-07#p-c5","u":"osn/cpu/practice-problems-lectures-01-to-07.html#p-c5","l":"C5","s":"","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"cpu","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 01 to 07","q":"<p id=\"p-c5\"><strong>C5.</strong> In limited direct execution, how does the OS get the CPU back from a process stuck in <code>while (1);</code>? During the switch that follows, two different register saves happen. Who does each one, and where are the registers saved?</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em> Through the <strong>timer interrupt</strong>. At boot, the OS sets up a hardware timer (a privileged operation) that interrupts every few milliseconds. When it fires, the CPU jumps into the OS's interrupt handler, whatever the process was doing, and the scheduler can switch to another process. The two register saves are:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>By the hardware</strong>, at the moment of the interrupt: process A's <em>user</em> registers are saved onto A's <strong>kernel stack</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>By the OS</strong>, when it decides to switch: the switch routine (<code>swtch</code> in xv6) saves A's <em>kernel</em> registers into A's <strong>process structure</strong> (its PCB / <code>context</code>), and loads B's kernel registers from B's process structure.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The return-from-trap then restores B's user registers from B's kernel stack, and B resumes.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/cpu/practice-problems-lectures-01-to-07#p-c6","u":"osn/cpu/practice-problems-lectures-01-to-07.html#p-c6","l":"C6","s":"","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"cpu","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 01 to 07","q":"<p id=\"p-c6\"><strong>C6.</strong> Three jobs arrive as follows. Job A arrives at 0 and needs 30. Jobs B and C both arrive at 10; B needs 10 and C needs 25. For <strong>FIFO, SJF, STCF and RR</strong> (time slice 10), give the schedule, the average turnaround time and the average response time. For RR, assume jobs that arrive at the same moment a slice ends join the queue ahead of the job that was just preempted.</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em> A runs 0-30 if it is never preempted. B and C arrive at 10.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Policy</th>\n<th>Schedule</th>\n<th>Turnaround (A, B, C)</th>\n<th>Avg</th>\n<th>Response (A, B, C)</th>\n<th>Avg</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>FIFO</td>\n<td>A 0-30, B 30-40, C 40-65</td>\n<td>30, 30, 55</td>\n<td><strong>38.3</strong></td>\n<td>0, 20, 30</td>\n<td><strong>16.7</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>SJF</td>\n<td>Same as FIFO</td>\n<td>30, 30, 55</td>\n<td><strong>38.3</strong></td>\n<td>0, 20, 30</td>\n<td><strong>16.7</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>STCF</td>\n<td>A 0-10, B 10-20, A 20-40, C 40-65</td>\n<td>40, 10, 55</td>\n<td><strong>35</strong></td>\n<td>0, 0, 30</td>\n<td><strong>10</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>RR (10)</td>\n<td>A 0-10, B 10-20, C 20-30, A 30-40, C 40-50, A 50-60, C 60-65</td>\n<td>60, 10, 55</td>\n<td><strong>41.7</strong></td>\n<td>0, 0, 10</td>\n<td><strong>3.3</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>Points to notice:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>SJF is no better than FIFO here.</strong> When B and C arrive, A is already running, and non-preemptive SJF cannot stop it.</li>\n<li><strong>STCF</strong> preempts A at t = 10, because B has 10 left and A has 20. At t = 20, A (20 left) beats C (25 left).</li>\n<li><strong>RR</strong> has the best response time but the worst turnaround, which is the trade-off from 1.4.</li>\n</ul>\n"},{"id":"osn/cpu/practice-problems-lectures-01-to-07#p-c7","u":"osn/cpu/practice-problems-lectures-01-to-07.html#p-c7","l":"C7","s":"","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"cpu","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 01 to 07","q":"<p id=\"p-c7\"><strong>C7.</strong> (a) Which MLFQ rule stops a job from &quot;gaming&quot; the scheduler, and how did the old rules allow it? (b) What problem does the priority boost solve, and what goes wrong if the boost period S is too large or too small?</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em></p>\n<p>(a) <strong>Rule 4</strong>: a job's allotment at a level counts its <em>total</em> CPU time there, however many times it gave up the CPU. The old Rule 4b let a job keep its priority if it gave up the CPU before its slice ended. A job could issue a pointless I/O at 99% of every slice and stay in the top queue forever.</p>\n<p>(b) The <strong>priority boost</strong> (Rule 5) fixes <strong>starvation</strong> (long jobs at the bottom get to run at least every S) and <strong>behaviour change</strong> (a job that becomes interactive gets back to the top). If S is too large, long jobs still starve between boosts. If S is too small, jobs are moved to the top so often that interactive jobs lose their advantage, and MLFQ behaves like plain round robin.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/cpu/practice-problems-lectures-01-to-07#p-c8","u":"osn/cpu/practice-problems-lectures-01-to-07.html#p-c8","l":"C8","s":"","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"cpu","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 01 to 07","q":"<p id=\"p-c8\"><strong>C8.</strong> Two CPU-bound processes run under CFS with sched_latency = 48 ms. A has nice 0 (weight 1024) and B has nice 5 (weight 335). Compute each one's time slice, and how much each one's vruntime grows during its slice. What do you notice?</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em> The slice is in proportion to the weight:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>A: 48 × 1024 / (1024 + 335) = <strong>36.17 ms</strong></li>\n<li>B: 48 × 335 / 1359 = <strong>11.83 ms</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>vruntime grows by the time run × (1024 / weight):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>A: 36.17 × 1024/1024 = <strong>36.17</strong></li>\n<li>B: 11.83 × 1024/335 = <strong>36.17</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>They are equal.</strong> The weights make vruntime grow <em>more slowly</em> for a higher-priority process, so when CFS keeps all vruntimes level, the higher-weight process gets proportionally more real CPU time. A gets about 75% of the CPU and B about 25%.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/networking/practice-problems-lectures-08-and-09#p-n1","u":"osn/networking/practice-problems-lectures-08-and-09.html#p-n1","l":"N1","s":"L08","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"networking","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 08 and 09","q":"<p id=\"p-n1\"><strong>N1 (L08).</strong> A switch connects hosts H1, H2 and H3 within the network 192.168.10.x. Explain why the switch alone cannot let H2 communicate with a host on a completely different network, and name the device that makes this possible.</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em> A switch works at the <strong>data link layer (L2)</strong>. It forwards frames by <strong>MAC address</strong> between hosts on the same local network. It has no notion of IP networks or routes, so it cannot decide how to send a packet to a host outside 192.168.10.x. That needs a <strong>router</strong>, which works at the <strong>network layer (L3)</strong>: it reads the destination <strong>IP address</strong> and forwards the packet towards the destination network, hop by hop.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/networking/practice-problems-lectures-08-and-09#p-n2","u":"osn/networking/practice-problems-lectures-08-and-09.html#p-n2","l":"N2","s":"L08","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"networking","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 08 and 09","q":"<p id=\"p-n2\"><strong>N2 (L08).</strong> Using the OSI model, explain why data sent by an application on Host 1 must pass down through Layers 7 to 1 before reaching the physical medium, and then back up through Layers 1 to 7 on Host 2, instead of going directly from application to application.</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em> Two applications on different hosts have no direct connection: the only thing that physically links the two machines is the medium at <strong>Layer 1</strong>. Each layer provides a service to the layer above by using the layer below. Each layer also talks <em>logically</em> to its peer layer on the other host, through the header it adds. So the data must go down the stack to reach the wire, and back up the stack on the other side, where each layer reads and removes its peer's header. This layering keeps each layer independent: the application does not need to know about routing, MAC addresses or signalling, and a layer's technology (say, Wi-Fi instead of Ethernet) can change without affecting the others.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/networking/practice-problems-lectures-08-and-09#p-n3","u":"osn/networking/practice-problems-lectures-08-and-09.html#p-n3","l":"N3","s":"L08","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"networking","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 08 and 09","q":"<p id=\"p-n3\"><strong>N3 (L08).</strong> Describe encapsulation as data moves from the Application layer down to the Physical layer. Define a segment, a packet and a frame, and explain how decapsulation reverses the process at the receiving host.</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em> Going down the stack, each layer wraps what it receives in its own header:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>The <strong>application</strong> produces a message.</li>\n<li>The <strong>transport layer</strong> adds a header with ports, sequence numbers and a checksum. The result is a <strong>segment</strong>.</li>\n<li>The <strong>network layer</strong> adds an IP header with the source and destination IP addresses. The result is a <strong>packet</strong> (datagram).</li>\n<li>The <strong>data link layer</strong> adds a header with MAC addresses and a trailer with an error check. The result is a <strong>frame</strong>.</li>\n<li>The <strong>physical layer</strong> sends the frame as bits.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>At the receiver, <strong>decapsulation</strong> reverses this. Each layer checks and removes its own header and hands the payload up to the right protocol above (using a type, protocol or port field), until the application receives the original message.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/networking/practice-problems-lectures-08-and-09#p-n4","u":"osn/networking/practice-problems-lectures-08-and-09.html#p-n4","l":"N4","s":"L08","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"networking","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 08 and 09","q":"<p id=\"p-n4\"><strong>N4 (L08).</strong> Machine 234 sends data to Machine 345 across two routers. Explain the difference between hop-to-hop and end-to-end delivery here, and which layer is responsible for each.</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em> Per L08:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Hop-to-hop</strong> delivery moves a frame from one node to the <em>next directly connected</em> node: 234 to the first router, router to router, and the last router to 345. This is the <strong>data link layer (L2)</strong>, using MAC addresses, which change at every hop.</li>\n<li><strong>End-to-end</strong> delivery gets the data all the way from 234 to 345 across those hops, choosing the route. This is the <strong>network layer (L3)</strong>, using IP addresses, which stay the same for the whole trip.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The transport layer (L4) then extends delivery from host-to-host to process-to-process, using ports.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/networking/practice-problems-lectures-08-and-09#p-n5","u":"osn/networking/practice-problems-lectures-08-and-09.html#p-n5","l":"N5","s":"L08","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"networking","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 08 and 09","q":"<p id=\"p-n5\"><strong>N5 (L08).</strong> Using the example of sending one video-call frame from Host 1 to Host 2, describe two points in the journey where an <strong>OS mechanism</strong> (not a networking protocol) is essential, and what would go wrong at each without it.</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em> Any two of these, from L08's walk-through of sending one video frame:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>System call (trap) for <code>send()</code>.</strong> The application cannot touch the network card directly; it must trap into the kernel. Without this protection, any process could read or write the NIC and other processes' data.</li>\n<li><strong>Kernel buffers (memory management).</strong> The kernel copies the frame into OS-managed memory where TCP and IP headers are attached. Without them, the protocol stack would have nowhere to build packets or hold received data.</li>\n<li><strong>Interrupts from the NIC.</strong> Interrupts tell the OS that a transmission finished or a packet arrived. Without them, the OS would have to poll the NIC constantly, wasting CPU, or would never notice arriving data.</li>\n<li><strong>Process states and the scheduler.</strong> The receiving app is <strong>blocked</strong> on <code>recv()</code> and is moved to <strong>ready</strong> when data arrives. Without this, the app would have to spin while waiting, wasting the CPU.</li>\n<li><strong>Timers.</strong> A timer interrupt tells TCP when to retransmit a lost segment. Without it, a lost packet would never be noticed.</li>\n</ul>\n"},{"id":"osn/networking/practice-problems-lectures-08-and-09#p-n6","u":"osn/networking/practice-problems-lectures-08-and-09.html#p-n6","l":"N6","s":"sheet Q1","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"networking","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 08 and 09","q":"<p id=\"p-n6\"><strong>N6 (sheet Q1).</strong> A host sends a 5000-byte application message over TCP. TCP adds a 20-byte header; IP adds a 20-byte header to every packet or fragment; Ethernet adds a 14-byte header and a 4-byte trailer (18 bytes) to every frame. The Ethernet MTU limits each IP packet (header plus payload) to 1500 bytes.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>(a) Why does the 1500-byte MTU constrain the IP packet but not the Ethernet header and trailer? Why does a lower layer never need to understand the payload it is handed?</li>\n<li>(b) How big is the TCP segment handed to IP?</li>\n<li>(c) Does it fit in one IP packet? If not, give the minimum number of fragments and the exact size of each. (IP fragments the segment as plain bytes and knows nothing of the TCP header; every fragment needs its own 20-byte IP header.)</li>\n<li>(d) Each fragment goes in its own Ethernet frame. How many bytes are transmitted on the wire in total?</li>\n<li>(e) After IP reassembles the fragments, how does the transport layer demultiplex the segment to the right socket, and which header fields does it use? Why can this not happen fragment by fragment?</li>\n</ul>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em></p>\n<p>(a) The MTU limits <strong>what the link layer will carry as its payload</strong>, which is the IP packet. The Ethernet header and trailer are wrapped around that payload by the link layer itself, so they are outside the limit. Each layer treats what it is handed as an opaque string of bytes: it only needs <em>its own</em> header to do its job. This is what lets layers be designed and replaced independently.</p>\n<p>(b) 5000 + 20 = <strong>5020 bytes</strong>.</p>\n<p>(c) <strong>No.</strong> Each IP packet can carry at most 1500 − 20 = <strong>1480 bytes</strong> of payload. 5020 / 1480 = 3.39, so <strong>4 fragments</strong> are needed:</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Fragment</th>\n<th>Payload</th>\n<th>IP packet size</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>1480 (includes the 20-byte TCP header)</td>\n<td>1500</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>1480</td>\n<td>1500</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>1480</td>\n<td>1500</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>5020 − 3 × 1480 = 580</td>\n<td>600</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>(1480 is a multiple of 8, as IP fragment offsets require.) Note that only fragment 1 contains the TCP header.</p>\n<p>(d) Each frame adds 18 bytes: 3 × 1518 + 618 = <strong>5172 bytes</strong> on the wire. That is 172 bytes of overhead on 5000 bytes of data: 20 (TCP) + 4 × 20 (IP) + 4 × 18 (Ethernet).</p>\n<p>(e) The IP layer reassembles the fragments using the <strong>Identification</strong>, <strong>Flags</strong> (more-fragments) and <strong>Fragment Offset</strong> fields, and its <strong>Protocol</strong> field (6) says to pass the result to TCP. TCP then demultiplexes using the <strong>4-tuple</strong>: source and destination IP (from the IP header) and <strong>source and destination port</strong> (from the TCP header). This cannot happen fragment by fragment, because:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Only the first fragment contains the TCP header, and so the ports. Fragments 2-4 are just bytes, with nothing to say which socket they belong to.</li>\n<li>The TCP checksum covers the whole segment, so it cannot be verified until every fragment has arrived.</li>\n</ul>\n"},{"id":"osn/networking/practice-problems-lectures-08-and-09#p-n7","u":"osn/networking/practice-problems-lectures-08-and-09.html#p-n7","l":"N7","s":"sheet Q2","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"networking","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 08 and 09","q":"<p id=\"p-n7\"><strong>N7 (sheet Q2).</strong> A TCP sender starts with EstimatedRTT = 100 ms, DevRTT = 10 ms, α = 0.125 and β = 0.25. It then measures SampleRTT = 140, 90 and 115 ms.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>(a) Compute EstimatedRTT, DevRTT and TimeoutInterval after each sample, to 3 decimal places.</li>\n<li>(b) Right after sample 3, a 4th segment is sent and its ACK arrives 172 ms later. Would the timer already have fired, causing an unnecessary retransmission?</li>\n<li>(c) In one sentence: why does TCP use 4 × DevRTT as the margin, rather than a fixed constant such as 20 ms?</li>\n</ul>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em> The formulas are EstimatedRTT = 0.875 · EstimatedRTT + 0.125 · Sample, then DevRTT = 0.75 · DevRTT + 0.25 · |Sample − EstimatedRTT|, and Timeout = EstimatedRTT + 4 · DevRTT. Here DevRTT uses the <em>just-updated</em> EstimatedRTT, as in the Unit 2 worked example. (RFC 6298 updates DevRTT first, using the old estimate, which gives slightly different numbers; follow whichever order your exam uses.)</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Sample</th>\n<th>SampleRTT</th>\n<th>EstimatedRTT</th>\n<th>DevRTT</th>\n<th>TimeoutInterval</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>140</td>\n<td>0.875·100 + 0.125·140 = <strong>105.000</strong></td>\n<td>0.75·10 + 0.25·35 = <strong>16.250</strong></td>\n<td>105 + 65 = <strong>170.000</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>90</td>\n<td>0.875·105 + 0.125·90 = <strong>103.125</strong></td>\n<td>0.75·16.25 + 0.25·13.125 = <strong>15.469</strong></td>\n<td>103.125 + 61.875 = <strong>165.000</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>115</td>\n<td>0.875·103.125 + 0.125·115 = <strong>104.609</strong></td>\n<td>0.75·15.469 + 0.25·10.391 = <strong>14.199</strong></td>\n<td>104.609 + 56.797 = <strong>161.406</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>(b) <strong>Yes.</strong> The timeout is 161.406 ms, and the ACK arrives at 172 ms. The timer fires about 10.6 ms before the ACK arrives, so the sender retransmits a segment that was not actually lost.</p>\n<p>(c) DevRTT measures how much the RTT actually varies on this path, so the safety margin grows on jittery paths (avoiding spurious retransmissions) and shrinks on stable ones (so real losses are detected quickly), which no single fixed constant can do for every path.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/networking/practice-problems-lectures-08-and-09#p-n8","u":"osn/networking/practice-problems-lectures-08-and-09.html#p-n8","l":"N8","s":"sheet Q3","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"networking","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 08 and 09","q":"<p id=\"p-n8\"><strong>N8 (sheet Q3).</strong> A TCP connection has MSS = 1000 bytes, a fixed RTT of 80 ms, cwnd = 1 MSS and ssthresh = 16 MSS at the start. One full window is sent and acknowledged every RTT.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>(a) Tabulate cwnd for rounds 1-9, marking slow start and congestion avoidance.</li>\n<li>(b) At the end of round 9, a loss is detected by three duplicate ACKs. Give the new ssthresh and cwnd.</li>\n<li>(c) Give cwnd for rounds 10, 11 and 12.</li>\n<li>(d) Approximate the throughput (cwnd / RTT) in Mbps at round 4 and at round 11.</li>\n<li>(e) If the loss at round 9 had been detected by a <strong>timeout</strong> instead, what would cwnd and ssthresh become, and which phase does TCP re-enter? Why does TCP treat the two loss signals differently?</li>\n</ul>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em></p>\n<p>(a)</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Round</th>\n<th>1</th>\n<th>2</th>\n<th>3</th>\n<th>4</th>\n<th>5</th>\n<th>6</th>\n<th>7</th>\n<th>8</th>\n<th>9</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>cwnd (MSS)</td>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>8</td>\n<td>16</td>\n<td>17</td>\n<td>18</td>\n<td>19</td>\n<td>20</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Phase</td>\n<td>SS</td>\n<td>SS</td>\n<td>SS</td>\n<td>SS</td>\n<td>reaches ssthresh</td>\n<td>CA</td>\n<td>CA</td>\n<td>CA</td>\n<td>CA</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>Slow start doubles cwnd every RTT until it reaches ssthresh = 16 in round 5. From then on, congestion avoidance adds 1 MSS per RTT.</p>\n<p>(b) Three duplicate ACKs trigger multiplicative decrease: <strong>ssthresh = 20 / 2 = 10</strong>, and <strong>cwnd = 10</strong> (fast recovery: no return to slow start).</p>\n<p>(c) Rounds 10, 11, 12: <strong>10, 11, 12</strong> (congestion avoidance).</p>\n<p>(d) Throughput ≈ cwnd × MSS / RTT.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Round 4: 8 × 1000 bytes × 8 bits / 0.08 s = 800,000 bit/s = <strong>0.8 Mbps</strong>.</li>\n<li>Round 11: 11 × 8000 bits / 0.08 s = <strong>1.1 Mbps</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>(e) On a timeout: <strong>ssthresh = 10</strong>, <strong>cwnd = 1 MSS</strong>, and TCP re-enters <strong>slow start</strong>. The reason for the difference: three duplicate ACKs prove that later segments are still getting through, so the network is only mildly congested. A timeout means nothing is coming back at all, which suggests severe congestion, so TCP backs off much harder.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/networking/practice-problems-lectures-08-and-09#p-n9","u":"osn/networking/practice-problems-lectures-08-and-09.html#p-n9","l":"N9","s":"sheet Q4","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"networking","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 08 and 09","q":"<p id=\"p-n9\"><strong>N9 (sheet Q4).</strong> Flow control limits a sender to at most Window bytes in flight (LastByteSent − LastByteAcked), and TCP's ACKs are cumulative.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>(a) State the exact condition under which a sender with plenty of data must stop sending.</li>\n<li>(b) A sender has exactly W bytes in flight. The <strong>first</strong> of these segments is lost, but all the later ones arrive and are buffered. What ACK value does the receiver send back, and why does the sender's usable window not grow at all?</li>\n<li>(c) The lost segment is then retransmitted and arrives. What happens to the next ACK, and why can this one retransmission release a large burst of new data?</li>\n<li>(d) In one sentence: why can one early loss have an outsized effect on a flow-control-limited sender's throughput?</li>\n</ul>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em></p>\n<p>(a) The sender must stop when <strong>LastByteSent − LastByteAcked ≥ W</strong>, that is, when a full window of unacknowledged data is in flight.</p>\n<p>(b) Every segment that arrives after the gap produces an ACK carrying the <strong>same</strong> number: the first byte of the lost segment (the next byte the receiver expects in order). These are <strong>duplicate ACKs</strong>. Because ACKs are cumulative, the receiver cannot acknowledge any byte beyond the gap, even though it has buffered them. LastByteAcked therefore does not move, the sender still has W bytes in flight, and it cannot send anything new. (Three duplicate ACKs will trigger fast retransmit.)</p>\n<p>(c) Once the missing segment arrives, the gap is filled, and the receiver's next ACK <strong>jumps</strong> to the byte after the last buffered segment, acknowledging all W bytes at once. LastByteAcked advances by W in one step, the in-flight amount drops to zero, and the sender may immediately send a <strong>whole new window</strong> of data as a burst.</p>\n<p>(d) Because cumulative ACKs cannot move past a hole, one early loss freezes the entire window until the retransmission arrives (a timeout, or three duplicate ACKs plus a round trip), so throughput drops to almost zero for that time even though nearly all the data was delivered.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/networking/practice-problems-lectures-08-and-09#p-n10","u":"osn/networking/practice-problems-lectures-08-and-09.html#p-n10","l":"N10","s":"","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"networking","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 08 and 09","q":"<p id=\"p-n10\"><strong>N10.</strong> Compute the 16-bit Internet checksum of the three words 0xF0F0, 0x1234 and 0x8001. Show how the receiver checks it. Give an example of a two-bit error the checksum would miss.</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em> Add the words with 16-bit ones'-complement addition (any carry out of the top bit is added back in at the bottom):</p>\n<ol>\n<li>0xF0F0 + 0x1234 = 0x10324. Wrap the carry around: 0x0324 + 1 = <strong>0x0325</strong>.</li>\n<li>0x0325 + 0x8001 = <strong>0x8326</strong> (no carry).</li>\n<li>The checksum is the bitwise complement: ~0x8326 = <strong>0x7CD9</strong>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The receiver adds all four words, including the checksum: 0x8326 + 0x7CD9 = <strong>0xFFFF</strong>. All ones means no error was detected; any 0 bit means an error.</p>\n<p><strong>A missed error:</strong> if the lowest bit of 0xF0F0 flips from 0 to 1 (giving 0xF0F1) and the lowest bit of 0x8001 flips from 1 to 0 (giving 0x8000), the sum is unchanged, so the checksum still passes. Two errors in the same bit position, in opposite directions, cancel out.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/networking/practice-problems-lectures-08-and-09#p-n11","u":"osn/networking/practice-problems-lectures-08-and-09.html#p-n11","l":"N11","s":"","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"networking","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 08 and 09","q":"<p id=\"p-n11\"><strong>N11.</strong> Two browsers on different machines both connect to a web server on port 80. (a) How does the server's OS deliver each arriving segment to the right connection socket? (b) How would this differ for a UDP server on port 53? (c) List the system calls a TCP server makes, in order, up to the point where it can read a client's request, and say what <code>accept</code> returns.</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em></p>\n<p>(a) A TCP connection socket is identified by the <strong>4-tuple</strong> (source IP, source port, destination IP, destination port). Both clients' segments have destination port 80, but their source IPs and ports differ, so the OS finds a different connection socket for each. The listening socket on port 80 only receives new connection requests (SYNs).</p>\n<p>(b) A UDP socket is identified only by <strong>(destination IP, destination port)</strong>, so datagrams from all clients arrive at the <strong>same</strong> socket. The server uses the source address returned by <code>recvfrom</code> to know where to send each reply.</p>\n<p>(c) <code>socket()</code> → <code>bind()</code> (attach to port 80) → <code>listen()</code> → <code>accept()</code> → <code>read()</code>/<code>recv()</code>. <code>accept</code> blocks until a client connects, then returns a <strong>new file descriptor</strong>: a connected socket for that one client. The listening socket stays open so the server can accept more clients.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/memory/practice-problems-lectures-10-to-13#p-m1","u":"osn/memory/practice-problems-lectures-10-to-13.html#p-m1","l":"M1","s":"","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"memory","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 10 to 13","q":"<p id=\"p-m1\"><strong>M1.</strong> A process has base = 16384 and bounds = 4096 (bounds is the size). Translate the virtual addresses 0, 1000, 4096 and 5000, or say which ones fault.</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em> <strong>Rule:</strong> the hardware first checks VA &lt; bounds, then computes PA = VA + base. If VA ≥ bounds, it raises an exception and the OS handles it (usually by killing the process).</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>VA</th>\n<th>Check (VA &lt; 4096?)</th>\n<th>Working</th>\n<th>Result</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>0</td>\n<td>yes</td>\n<td>0 + 16384</td>\n<td><strong>16384</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1000</td>\n<td>yes</td>\n<td>1000 + 16384</td>\n<td><strong>17384</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4096</td>\n<td><strong>no</strong> (4096 is not &lt; 4096)</td>\n<td>the last legal address is 4095</td>\n<td><strong>Fault</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5000</td>\n<td><strong>no</strong> (5000 ≥ 4096)</td>\n<td></td>\n<td><strong>Fault</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><em>Watch out:</em> an address equal to the bounds is already illegal when bounds holds the <em>size</em>.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/memory/practice-problems-lectures-10-to-13#p-m2","u":"osn/memory/practice-problems-lectures-10-to-13.html#p-m2","l":"M2","s":"","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"memory","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 10 to 13","q":"<p id=\"p-m2\"><strong>M2.</strong> A system uses a 14-bit virtual address. The top 2 bits pick the segment: 00 = code, 01 = heap, 11 = stack. The maximum segment size is therefore 4KB.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Segment</th>\n<th>Base</th>\n<th>Size</th>\n<th>Grows</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Code (00)</td>\n<td>32K</td>\n<td>2K</td>\n<td>Positive</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Heap (01)</td>\n<td>34K</td>\n<td>3K</td>\n<td>Positive</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Stack (11)</td>\n<td>28K</td>\n<td>2K</td>\n<td>Negative</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>Translate the virtual addresses 1500, 5000, 7500, 16000 and 13000, or say which ones fault.</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em> <strong>Method:</strong> the top 2 bits give the segment, so each segment covers 4096 addresses: segment 00 is 0–4095, 01 is 4096–8191, 10 is 8192–12287 (unused) and 11 is 12288–16383. The offset is VA minus the segment's start. For a forward segment: check offset &lt; size, then PA = base + offset. For the stack: negative offset = offset − 4096, check |negative offset| ≤ size, then PA = base + negative offset.</p>\n<p>Bases in bytes: code 32768, heap 34816, stack 28672.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>VA</th>\n<th>Segment</th>\n<th>Offset</th>\n<th>Check</th>\n<th>PA</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1500</td>\n<td>code</td>\n<td>1500</td>\n<td>1500 &lt; 2048, ok</td>\n<td>32768 + 1500 = <strong>34268</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5000</td>\n<td>heap</td>\n<td>5000 − 4096 = 904</td>\n<td>904 &lt; 3072, ok</td>\n<td>34816 + 904 = <strong>35720</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>7500</td>\n<td>heap</td>\n<td>7500 − 4096 = 3404</td>\n<td>3404 ≥ 3072</td>\n<td><strong>Fault</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>16000</td>\n<td>stack</td>\n<td>16000 − 12288 = 3712</td>\n<td>3712 − 4096 = −384, and 384 ≤ 2048, ok</td>\n<td>28672 − 384 = <strong>28288</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>13000</td>\n<td>stack</td>\n<td>13000 − 12288 = 712</td>\n<td>712 − 4096 = −3384, and 3384 &gt; 2048</td>\n<td><strong>Fault</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n"},{"id":"osn/memory/practice-problems-lectures-10-to-13#p-m3","u":"osn/memory/practice-problems-lectures-10-to-13.html#p-m3","l":"M3","s":"","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"memory","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 10 to 13","q":"<p id=\"p-m3\"><strong>M3.</strong> A free list holds chunks of 20, 10, 40 and 15 bytes, in that order. A request for 12 bytes arrives. Which chunk does each of best fit, worst fit and first fit choose, and how big is the leftover piece? Which strategy tends to leave many tiny unusable pieces, and why?</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em> <strong>Rules:</strong> best fit = the smallest chunk that is large enough; worst fit = the largest chunk; first fit = the first chunk in the list that is large enough.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Strategy</th>\n<th>Candidates that fit (≥ 12)</th>\n<th>Chooses</th>\n<th>Leftover</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>First fit</td>\n<td>20 is first</td>\n<td><strong>20</strong></td>\n<td>8</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Best fit</td>\n<td>20, 40, 15 → smallest is 15</td>\n<td><strong>15</strong></td>\n<td>3</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Worst fit</td>\n<td>20, 40, 15 → largest is 40</td>\n<td><strong>40</strong></td>\n<td>28</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Best fit</strong> tends to leave many tiny pieces: by choosing the closest fit it makes each leftover as small as possible, and these slivers (such as the 3 here) are too small for any later request, so they accumulate as external fragmentation. Worst fit tries to keep leftovers large, but in practice also fragments badly, and both need a full search of the list (first fit stops early).</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/memory/practice-problems-lectures-10-to-13#p-m4","u":"osn/memory/practice-problems-lectures-10-to-13.html#p-m4","l":"M4","s":"","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"memory","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 10 to 13","q":"<p id=\"p-m4\"><strong>M4.</strong> A buddy allocator manages 128KB. (a) Show the splits for a 10KB request. How much internal fragmentation is there? (b) A 30KB request follows. Where does it go? (c) Why is coalescing cheap in a buddy system?</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(a)</strong> Round 10 KB up to the next power of two: <strong>16 KB</strong>. Split until a 16 KB block exists: 128 → 64 + 64; 64 → 32 + 32; 32 → 16 + 16. One 16 KB block is allocated; the free blocks left are 16 KB, 32 KB and 64 KB. Internal fragmentation = 16 − 10 = <strong>6 KB</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>(b)</strong> Round 30 KB up to <strong>32 KB</strong>. The free 32 KB block (left over from the second split) fits exactly, so it is used with no new split. Internal fragmentation = 2 KB. The free blocks are now one 16 KB and one 64 KB.</p>\n<p><strong>(c)</strong> A block's <strong>buddy</strong> is found by flipping one bit of its address (the bit that equals the block size), so the check is a single address calculation. When a block is freed, the allocator checks whether its buddy is entirely free; if so the two merge into a block of twice the size, and the check repeats one level up. No searching of a list is needed.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/memory/practice-problems-lectures-10-to-13#p-m5","u":"osn/memory/practice-problems-lectures-10-to-13.html#p-m5","l":"M5","s":"","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"memory","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 10 to 13","q":"<p id=\"p-m5\"><strong>M5.</strong> (a) A 32-bit address space with 8KB pages and 4-byte PTEs: how many bits are in the VPN and offset, and how big is a linear page table? (b) What would 100 such processes cost?</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(a)</strong> 8 KB = 2^13, so the <strong>offset is 13 bits</strong> and the <strong>VPN is 32 − 13 = 19 bits</strong>. A linear table has 2^19 = 524,288 entries × 4 bytes = 2^21 bytes = <strong>2 MB per process</strong>.</p>\n<div class=\"math-d\" data-tex=\"\\text{table size} = 2^{\\text{VPN bits}} \\times \\text{PTE size} = 2^{19} \\times 4 = 2\\text{ MB}\"><span class=\"katex-display\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.6944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord text\"><span class=\"mord\">table size</span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.9824em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord\">2</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.8991em;\"><span style=\"top:-3.113em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"katex-sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord text mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">VPN bits</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">×</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord text\"><span class=\"mord\">PTE size</span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.9474em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord\">2</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.8641em;\"><span style=\"top:-3.113em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"katex-sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">19</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">×</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">4</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">2</span><span class=\"mord text\"><span class=\"mord\"> MB</span></span></span></span></span></span></div>\n<p><strong>(b)</strong> 100 processes × 2 MB = <strong>200 MB</strong> of page tables, even if each process uses very little memory. This is why smaller page-table techniques (bigger pages, hybrid, multi-level) are needed.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/memory/practice-problems-lectures-10-to-13#p-m6","u":"osn/memory/practice-problems-lectures-10-to-13.html#p-m6","l":"M6","s":"","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"memory","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 10 to 13","q":"<p id=\"p-m6\"><strong>M6.</strong> A 1KB address space uses 64-byte pages. The page table is: VPN 0 → PFN 5, VPN 1 → invalid, VPN 2 → PFN 9, VPN 3 → PFN 1. Translate virtual addresses 165, 112 and 200.</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em> <strong>Method:</strong> 1 KB = 2^10, so addresses have 10 bits. 64-byte pages = 2^6, so the offset is 6 bits and the VPN is 4 bits. Then VPN = VA ÷ 64 (integer division), offset = VA mod 64, and PA = PFN × 64 + offset.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>VA</th>\n<th>VPN = VA ÷ 64</th>\n<th>Offset = VA mod 64</th>\n<th>PTE</th>\n<th>PA</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>165</td>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>165 − 128 = 37</td>\n<td>PFN 9</td>\n<td>9 × 64 + 37 = 576 + 37 = <strong>613</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>112</td>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>112 − 64 = 48</td>\n<td><strong>invalid</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Fault</strong> (the OS usually kills the process)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>200</td>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>200 − 192 = 8</td>\n<td>PFN 1</td>\n<td>1 × 64 + 8 = <strong>72</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n"},{"id":"osn/memory/practice-problems-lectures-10-to-13#p-m7","u":"osn/memory/practice-problems-lectures-10-to-13.html#p-m7","l":"M7","s":"","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"memory","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 10 to 13","q":"<p id=\"p-m7\"><strong>M7.</strong> An array of 12 four-byte ints starts at virtual address 120, and the page size is 32 bytes. A loop reads a[0] to a[11] once, starting with an empty TLB. How many TLB hits and misses are there, and what is the hit rate? What would happen if the loop ran a second time straight away? Which kind of locality does each pass show?</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em> <strong>Method:</strong> element a[i] is at 120 + 4i, and its page is address ÷ 32. The array occupies bytes 120 to 167.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Page</th>\n<th>Bytes</th>\n<th>Elements</th>\n<th>Result</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>96–127</td>\n<td>a[0] (120), a[1] (124)</td>\n<td>1 miss, 1 hit</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>128–159</td>\n<td>a[2] to a[9] (128 to 156)</td>\n<td>1 miss, 7 hits</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>160–191</td>\n<td>a[10] (160), a[11] (164)</td>\n<td>1 miss, 1 hit</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>3 misses and 9 hits: hit rate = 9 / 12 = 75%.</strong> The hits come from <strong>spatial locality</strong> (neighbouring elements share a page).</p>\n<p><strong>Second pass straight away:</strong> the three translations are still in the TLB (if it holds at least 3 entries), so <strong>all 12 accesses hit: 100%</strong>. That is <strong>temporal locality</strong> (the same pages are reused soon).</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/memory/practice-problems-lectures-10-to-13#p-m8","u":"osn/memory/practice-problems-lectures-10-to-13.html#p-m8","l":"M8","s":"","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"memory","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 10 to 13","q":"<p id=\"p-m8\"><strong>M8.</strong> A TLB lookup takes 2 ns and a memory access takes 100 ns. The page table is a single-level linear table, and the TLB hit rate is 95%. What is the effective memory-access time? What would it be with no TLB at all?</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Hit:</strong> TLB lookup + one memory access = 2 + 100 = <strong>102 ns</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Miss:</strong> TLB lookup + PTE fetch + data fetch = 2 + 100 + 100 = <strong>202 ns</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"math-d\" data-tex=\"EAT = 0.95 \\times 102 + 0.05 \\times 202 = 96.9 + 10.1 = \\textbf{107 ns}\"><span class=\"katex-display\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0576em;\">E</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">A</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.1389em;\">T</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">0.95</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">×</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">102</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">0.05</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">×</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">202</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">96.9</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">10.1</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord text\"><span class=\"mord textbf\">107 ns</span></span></span></span></span></span></div>\n<p><strong>With no TLB:</strong> every access needs the PTE and then the data = <strong>200 ns</strong>, almost twice as slow. The TLB cuts the cost to just 7% above a single memory access.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/memory/practice-problems-lectures-10-to-13#p-m9","u":"osn/memory/practice-problems-lectures-10-to-13.html#p-m9","l":"M9","s":"","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"memory","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 10 to 13","q":"<p id=\"p-m9\"><strong>M9.</strong> Use the multi-level setup from 3.7: a 14-bit address space, 64-byte pages and 4-byte PTEs, so one page holds 16 PTEs.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>(a) Split VA 0x1234 into page-directory index, page-table index and offset.</li>\n<li>(b) Suppose PDE[4] is valid with PFN 0x20, and entry 8 of that page of the page table is valid with PFN 0x31. What is the physical address?</li>\n<li>(c) How many memory accesses does this take on a TLB miss?</li>\n<li>(d) For a 48-bit address space with 4KB pages and 8-byte PTEs, how many levels are needed if every piece of the page table must fit in one page?</li>\n</ul>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(a) Layout:</strong> 14-bit VA = | 4-bit PD index | 4-bit PT index | 6-bit offset |. 0x1234 in 14 bits is <code>01 0010 0011 0100</code> (0x1234 is 0001 0010 0011 0100 in 16 bits; drop the top 2 zero bits). Split: <code>0100 | 1000 | 110100</code>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Page-directory index = <code>0100</code> = <strong>4</strong>.</li>\n<li>Page-table index = <code>1000</code> = <strong>8</strong>.</li>\n<li>Offset = <code>110100</code> = <strong>52</strong>. (The VPN is <code>01001000</code> = 72.)</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>(b)</strong> PDE[4] gives the page of the page table (PFN 0x20); entry 8 of it gives PFN 0x31. <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.1389em;\">P</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">A</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.1389em;\">P</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.1389em;\">F</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.109em;\">N</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">≪</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">6</span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">∣</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.8889em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">o</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.1076em;\">f</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.1076em;\">f</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">se</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">t</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">0</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">x</span><span class=\"mord\">31</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">×</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">64</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">52</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">3136</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">52</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">3188</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.6861em;\"></span><span class=\"mord text\"><span class=\"mord textbf\">0xC74</span></span></span></span></span>.</p>\n<p><strong>(c)</strong> <strong>3 memory accesses</strong>: the page-directory entry, the page-table entry, then the data itself.</p>\n<p><strong>(d)</strong> One 4 KB page holds 4096 / 8 = 512 PTEs = 2^9, so each level indexes 9 bits. VPN = 48 − 12 = 36 bits, and 36 / 9 = <strong>4 levels</strong> (this is x86-64).</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/memory/practice-problems-lectures-10-to-13#p-m10","u":"osn/memory/practice-problems-lectures-10-to-13.html#p-m10","l":"M10","s":"","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"memory","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 10 to 13","q":"<p id=\"p-m10\"><strong>M10.</strong> With 3 frames, and starting empty, run the reference string <strong>7, 0, 1, 2, 0, 3, 0, 4, 2, 3</strong> under OPT, FIFO and LRU. Give the number of hits for each. How many of the misses are compulsory (cold-start) misses?</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em> <strong>Rules:</strong> FIFO evicts the page that has been in memory longest. LRU evicts the page used least recently. OPT evicts the page whose next use is furthest in the future (never = furthest). All three start with 3 compulsory misses (7, 0, 1).</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Ref</th>\n<th>FIFO</th>\n<th>Frames</th>\n<th>LRU</th>\n<th>Frames (least recent first)</th>\n<th>OPT</th>\n<th>Frames</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>7</td>\n<td>M</td>\n<td>7</td>\n<td>M</td>\n<td>7</td>\n<td>M</td>\n<td>7</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>0</td>\n<td>M</td>\n<td>7 0</td>\n<td>M</td>\n<td>7 0</td>\n<td>M</td>\n<td>7 0</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>M</td>\n<td>7 0 1</td>\n<td>M</td>\n<td>7 0 1</td>\n<td>M</td>\n<td>7 0 1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>M, evict 7</td>\n<td>0 1 2</td>\n<td>M, evict 7</td>\n<td>0 1 2</td>\n<td>M, evict 7 (never used again)</td>\n<td>0 1 2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>0</td>\n<td><strong>H</strong></td>\n<td>0 1 2</td>\n<td><strong>H</strong></td>\n<td>1 2 0</td>\n<td><strong>H</strong></td>\n<td>0 1 2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>M, evict 0</td>\n<td>1 2 3</td>\n<td>M, evict 1</td>\n<td>2 0 3</td>\n<td>M, evict 1 (never used again)</td>\n<td>0 2 3</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>0</td>\n<td>M, evict 1</td>\n<td>2 3 0</td>\n<td><strong>H</strong></td>\n<td>2 3 0</td>\n<td><strong>H</strong></td>\n<td>0 2 3</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>M, evict 2</td>\n<td>3 0 4</td>\n<td>M, evict 2</td>\n<td>3 0 4</td>\n<td>M, evict 0 (never used again)</td>\n<td>2 3 4</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>M, evict 3</td>\n<td>0 4 2</td>\n<td>M, evict 3</td>\n<td>0 4 2</td>\n<td><strong>H</strong></td>\n<td>2 3 4</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>M, evict 0</td>\n<td>4 2 3</td>\n<td>M, evict 0</td>\n<td>4 2 3</td>\n<td><strong>H</strong></td>\n<td>2 3 4</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Hits: OPT 4, LRU 2, FIFO 1.</strong> There are 6 distinct pages (7, 0, 1, 2, 3, 4), so <strong>6 misses are compulsory</strong>. OPT's 6 misses are <em>all</em> compulsory, so no policy can do better on this string; FIFO has 9 misses and LRU 8.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/memory/practice-problems-lectures-10-to-13#p-m11","u":"osn/memory/practice-problems-lectures-10-to-13.html#p-m11","l":"M11","s":"","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"memory","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 10 to 13","q":"<p id=\"p-m11\"><strong>M11.</strong> Four frames hold pages A, B, C and D, with use bits 1, 0, 1, 1. The clock hand points at A. Page E is requested, and then page F. Which page is evicted each time, and what are the use bits and hand position afterwards?</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em> <strong>Clock rule:</strong> look at the page under the hand. If its use bit is 1, clear it to 0 and advance. If it is 0, evict it, load the new page there with use bit 1, and advance the hand past it.</p>\n<p><strong>Request E</strong> (hand at A): A has use 1, so clear it (A = 0) and advance. B has use 0: <strong>evict B</strong>. E goes in B's frame with use bit 1, and the hand moves to C. State: A 0, E 1, C 1, D 1; hand at C.</p>\n<p><strong>Request F</strong> (hand at C): C has use 1, so clear it and advance. D has use 1, so clear it and advance. A has use 0: <strong>evict A</strong>. F goes into A's frame with use bit 1, and the hand moves to E. State: F 1, E 1, C 0, D 0; hand at E.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/memory/practice-problems-lectures-10-to-13#p-m12","u":"osn/memory/practice-problems-lectures-10-to-13.html#p-m12","l":"M12","s":"","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"memory","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 10 to 13","q":"<p id=\"p-m12\"><strong>M12.</strong> Memory takes 100 ns and a disk access takes 10 ms. What is the AMAT with a hit rate of 99.9%, and with 99.99%? What does this say about how often a system can afford to page?</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em> <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">A</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.109em;\">M</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">A</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.1389em;\">T</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.8333em;vertical-align:-0.15em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.1389em;\">T</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.3283em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:-0.1389em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"katex-sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\" style=\"margin-right:0.109em;\">M</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.8333em;vertical-align:-0.15em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.1389em;\">P</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.3117em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:-0.1389em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"katex-sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\">mi</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\">ss</span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">×</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.8333em;vertical-align:-0.15em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.1389em;\">T</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.3283em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:-0.1389em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"katex-sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\" style=\"margin-right:0.0278em;\">D</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span> (10 ms = 10,000,000 ns).</p>\n<ul>\n<li>At 99.9% (P_miss = 0.001): 100 + 0.001 × 10,000,000 = 100 + 10,000 = 10,100 ns = <strong>10.1 µs</strong>, about 100 times slower than memory.</li>\n<li>At 99.99% (P_miss = 0.0001): 100 + 1,000 = 1,100 ns = <strong>1.1 µs</strong>, still 11 times slower.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> disk is about 100,000 times slower than memory, so even a tiny miss rate <strong>dominates</strong> the average. A system can only afford to page very rarely: to keep the AMAT within a few percent of memory speed, the miss rate must be below about 1 in 100,000, so each process's working set must fit in memory.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/memory/practice-problems-lectures-10-to-13#p-m13","u":"osn/memory/practice-problems-lectures-10-to-13.html#p-m13","l":"M13","s":"","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"memory","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 10 to 13","q":"<p id=\"p-m13\"><strong>M13.</strong> (a) What is thrashing, and what are two ways an OS can respond to it? (b) Why can LRU never suffer from Belady's anomaly while FIFO can? (c) Why does a clean page cost less to evict than a dirty one?</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(a)</strong> <strong>Thrashing</strong> happens when the working sets of the running processes add up to <strong>more than physical memory</strong>, so the system keeps faulting and evicting pages that are needed again soon, spending nearly all its time on paging I/O and doing little useful work. Two responses: <strong>(1) admission control</strong>: run fewer processes (suspend some) so the rest fit in memory; <strong>(2) an out-of-memory killer</strong> (as in Linux): kill a memory-hungry process.</p>\n<p><strong>(b)</strong> <strong>Belady's anomaly:</strong> with FIFO, giving a process more frames can cause more faults. LRU has the <strong>stack property</strong>: the set of pages in memory with N frames is always a subset of the set with N + 1 frames, so an extra frame can only turn misses into hits. FIFO ignores how recently a page was used, so it lacks this property.</p>\n<p><strong>(c)</strong> A clean page is identical to its copy on disk, so it can simply be <strong>dropped</strong> (one I/O for the new page only). A dirty page must first be <strong>written back to disk</strong>, which costs an <strong>extra I/O</strong> before the frame can be reused.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/memory/practice-problems-lectures-10-to-13#p-m14","u":"osn/memory/practice-problems-lectures-10-to-13.html#p-m14","l":"M14","s":"","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"memory","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 10 to 13","q":"<p id=\"p-m14\"><strong>M14.</strong> In xv6 (Sv39): (a) why are text pages mapped without the W bit? (b) What is the guard page below the user stack for, and why does it lack the U bit? (c) What does <code>satp</code> hold, and when does it change?</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(a)</strong> So that a program <strong>cannot overwrite its own code</strong>, by a bug or by an attack. A write to a text page causes a <strong>page fault</strong> (a protection fault), and the kernel kills the process. Read-only code can also be shared safely.</p>\n<p><strong>(b)</strong> The guard page sits just below the stack. It has <strong>no U bit</strong>, so user-mode code cannot access it. If the stack <strong>overflows</strong>, the process runs into the guard page and takes a <strong>page fault</strong>, instead of silently overwriting whatever lies below the stack (such as the heap or data).</p>\n<p><strong>(c)</strong> <code>satp</code> holds the <strong>physical page number of the root page table</strong> of the current address space (plus the mode, Sv39). It changes whenever the CPU <strong>switches address space</strong>: in xv6, on every trap between user and kernel (in the trampoline code, which is mapped in both page tables) and when the scheduler switches to a different process.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/concurrency/practice-problems-lectures-14-to-17#p-s1","u":"osn/concurrency/practice-problems-lectures-14-to-17.html#p-s1","l":"S1","s":"","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"concurrency","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 14 to 17","q":"<p id=\"p-s1\"><strong>S1.</strong> Two threads each run <code>counter++</code> three times, with no lock. counter starts at 0. What are the largest and the smallest possible final values? Give the interleaving that produces the smallest one.</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em> Each <code>counter++</code> is three instructions (load, add, store). A lost update happens when a thread stores a value computed from a stale load.</p>\n<p><strong>Largest = 6:</strong> every increment runs without interruption. <strong>Smallest = 2.</strong> Here is how:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>T1 loads 0 into its register, then is interrupted.</li>\n<li>T2 runs two complete increments: counter = 2.</li>\n<li>T1 adds 1 to its stale 0 and stores 1. <strong>T2's two increments are wiped out.</strong></li>\n<li>T2 loads 1 for its third increment, then is interrupted.</li>\n<li>T1 runs its last two increments in full: counter = 3.</li>\n<li>T2 adds 1 to its stale 1 and stores <strong>2</strong>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Why it cannot be 1 (or 0):</strong> every store writes (value loaded) + 1, so once any store has happened, memory holds at least 1. The final value comes from the last store, which is some thread's <em>last</em> increment. That increment loaded its value after the same thread's first increment had already stored, so it loaded at least 1 and stored at least 2.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/concurrency/practice-problems-lectures-14-to-17#p-s2","u":"osn/concurrency/practice-problems-lectures-14-to-17.html#p-s2","l":"S2","s":"","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"concurrency","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 14 to 17","q":"<p id=\"p-s2\"><strong>S2.</strong> In the code below, which of <code>g</code>, <code>s</code>, <code>local</code>, <code>p</code> and <code>*p</code> are shared between two threads that both run <code>work()</code>?</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-c\">int g = 0;\nvoid *work(void *arg) {\n    static int s = 0;\n    int local = 0;\n    int *p = malloc(sizeof(int));\n    ...\n}\n</code></pre>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em></p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Variable</th>\n<th>Shared?</th>\n<th>Why</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code>g</code></td>\n<td><strong>Yes</strong></td>\n<td>Globals live in the data segment, and there is one copy</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>s</code></td>\n<td><strong>Yes</strong></td>\n<td>A <code>static</code> local also lives in the data segment, not on the stack</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>local</code></td>\n<td>No</td>\n<td>Each thread has it on its own stack</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>p</code></td>\n<td>No</td>\n<td>The pointer variable itself is on each thread's stack</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>*p</code></td>\n<td>Not in practice</td>\n<td>The heap is shared memory, but each thread mallocs its own block. It becomes shared only if one thread passes the pointer to another</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n"},{"id":"osn/concurrency/practice-problems-lectures-14-to-17#p-s3","u":"osn/concurrency/practice-problems-lectures-14-to-17.html#p-s3","l":"S3","s":"","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"concurrency","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 14 to 17","q":"<p id=\"p-s3\"><strong>S3.</strong> Show an interleaving in which the plain-flag lock from 4.2 (test with a load, then set with a store) lets two threads into the critical section. Then explain exactly why <code>TestAndSet</code> closes that gap.</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em> The bad interleaving, starting with flag = 0:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>T1 tests the flag and sees 0.</li>\n<li>An interrupt switches to T2. T2 tests, sees 0, and sets the flag to 1, entering the critical section.</li>\n<li>An interrupt switches back to T1, which sets the flag to 1 and also enters.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The problem is the gap between the test and the set. <code>TestAndSet</code> does both in <strong>one atomic instruction</strong>, so there is no gap. Whichever thread executes it first gets back 0 and leaves 1 behind; every other thread gets back 1 and keeps spinning.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/concurrency/practice-problems-lectures-14-to-17#p-s4","u":"osn/concurrency/practice-problems-lectures-14-to-17.html#p-s4","l":"S4","s":"","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"concurrency","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 14 to 17","q":"<p id=\"p-s4\"><strong>S4.</strong> Four threads share one CPU and a test-and-set spin lock. The holder is preempted just after taking the lock. Roughly how much CPU time is wasted before the holder runs again, and why? How do <code>yield</code> and the queue lock each improve this?</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em> <strong>Reasoning:</strong> on one CPU only one thread runs at a time, and the holder is not running. Each of the other three threads gets scheduled in turn, finds the lock held, and <strong>spins for its whole time slice</strong>, because the lock cannot be released until the holder runs again. That is roughly <strong>3 time slices wasted</strong> (N − 1 for N threads) each time the holder is preempted, and more if the scheduler is unlucky.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>With <strong>yield</strong>, each waiter gives up the CPU as soon as it finds the lock held. That costs 3 context switches instead of 3 slices, but it still has overhead and starvation is still possible.</li>\n<li>With the <strong>queue lock</strong>, waiters sleep and are not scheduled at all until the holder unparks them, so no time is wasted.</li>\n</ul>\n"},{"id":"osn/concurrency/practice-problems-lectures-14-to-17#p-s5","u":"osn/concurrency/practice-problems-lectures-14-to-17.html#p-s5","l":"S5","s":"","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"concurrency","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 14 to 17","q":"<p id=\"p-s5\"><strong>S5.</strong> A ticket lock starts with ticket = 0 and turn = 0. T1, T2 and T3 call <code>lock()</code> in that order, then T1 calls <code>unlock()</code>, then T4 calls <code>lock()</code>, then T2 calls <code>unlock()</code>. After each step, give ticket, turn, each thread's <code>myturn</code>, and who holds the lock.</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em></p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Step</th>\n<th>ticket</th>\n<th>turn</th>\n<th>myturn</th>\n<th>Holder</th>\n<th>Spinning</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Start</td>\n<td>0</td>\n<td>0</td>\n<td></td>\n<td>none</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>T1 lock</td>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>0</td>\n<td>T1 = 0</td>\n<td>T1</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>T2 lock</td>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>0</td>\n<td>T2 = 1</td>\n<td>T1</td>\n<td>T2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>T3 lock</td>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>0</td>\n<td>T3 = 2</td>\n<td>T1</td>\n<td>T2, T3</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>T1 unlock</td>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>1</td>\n<td></td>\n<td><strong>T2</strong></td>\n<td>T3</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>T4 lock</td>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>T4 = 3</td>\n<td>T2</td>\n<td>T3, T4</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>T2 unlock</td>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>2</td>\n<td></td>\n<td><strong>T3</strong></td>\n<td>T4</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Rules used:</strong> <code>lock()</code> takes <code>myturn = FetchAndAdd(&amp;ticket)</code> (old value, then ticket++) and spins until <code>turn == myturn</code>; <code>unlock()</code> does <code>turn++</code>. Threads are served strictly in ticket order (T1, T2, T3, T4). That FIFO order is what makes the ticket lock <strong>fair</strong>: bounded waiting, no starvation.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/concurrency/practice-problems-lectures-14-to-17#p-s6","u":"osn/concurrency/practice-problems-lectures-14-to-17.html#p-s6","l":"S6","s":"","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"concurrency","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 14 to 17","q":"<p id=\"p-s6\"><strong>S6.</strong> In the queue lock with <code>park</code>/<code>unpark</code>: (a) What would go wrong if a thread called <code>park()</code> <em>before</em> releasing the guard? (b) Describe the wakeup/waiting race that remains, and how <code>setpark()</code> fixes it.</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em></p>\n<p>(a) The thread would go to sleep <strong>holding the guard</strong>. When the lock holder calls <code>unlock()</code>, it spins forever trying to take the guard, so it can never unpark anyone. Everything stops.</p>\n<p>(b) The race:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>A thread adds itself to the queue and releases the guard.</li>\n<li>It is preempted just <em>before</em> calling <code>park()</code>.</li>\n<li>The lock holder runs <code>unlock()</code> and calls <code>unpark()</code> on it. This has no effect, because the thread is not asleep yet.</li>\n<li>The thread runs again, calls <code>park()</code>, and <strong>sleeps forever</strong>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>With <strong><code>setpark()</code></strong>, the thread announces &quot;I am about to park&quot; <em>before</em> releasing the guard. If an <code>unpark()</code> arrives between that announcement and the <code>park()</code>, the <code>park()</code> returns immediately instead of sleeping.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/concurrency/practice-problems-lectures-14-to-17#p-s7","u":"osn/concurrency/practice-problems-lectures-14-to-17.html#p-s7","l":"S7","s":"","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"concurrency","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 14 to 17","q":"<p id=\"p-s7\"><strong>S7.</strong> Low-priority thread L holds a spin lock. High-priority thread H wakes up and tries to take it. Explain what happens on one CPU, and how priority inheritance fixes it.</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em> <strong>Priority inversion.</strong> H has the higher priority, so the scheduler always runs H, and H spins on the lock. L never gets the CPU, so it never finishes its critical section and never releases the lock. The system hangs, even though nothing is technically deadlocked. With <strong>priority inheritance</strong>, while H waits for a lock that L holds, L temporarily runs at H's priority. L gets the CPU, finishes, releases the lock, and drops back to its own priority, and then H proceeds.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/concurrency/practice-problems-lectures-14-to-17#p-s8","u":"osn/concurrency/practice-problems-lectures-14-to-17.html#p-s8","l":"S8","s":"","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"concurrency","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 14 to 17","q":"<p id=\"p-s8\"><strong>S8.</strong> Is this code correct? If not, give the interleaving that breaks it and fix it.</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-c\">// waiting thread                      // signalling thread\npthread_mutex_lock(&amp;m);                ready = 1;\nwhile (!ready)                         pthread_cond_signal(&amp;c);\n    pthread_cond_wait(&amp;c, &amp;m);\npthread_mutex_unlock(&amp;m);\n</code></pre>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em> <strong>Not correct: there is a lost wakeup</strong>, because the signaller does not take the lock.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>The waiter locks m and sees <code>ready == 0</code>. Just before it calls <code>wait</code>, it is interrupted.</li>\n<li>The signaller sets <code>ready = 1</code> and signals. Nobody is waiting yet, so the signal is lost.</li>\n<li>The waiter calls <code>wait</code> and <strong>sleeps forever</strong>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Fix:</strong> the signaller must hold the same mutex:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-c\">pthread_mutex_lock(&amp;m);\nready = 1;\npthread_cond_signal(&amp;c);\npthread_mutex_unlock(&amp;m);\n</code></pre>\n<p>Now the signaller cannot change <code>ready</code> between the waiter's check and its sleep, because the waiter holds m until <code>wait</code> has put it to sleep.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/concurrency/practice-problems-lectures-14-to-17#p-s9","u":"osn/concurrency/practice-problems-lectures-14-to-17.html#p-s9","l":"S9","s":"","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"concurrency","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 14 to 17","q":"<p id=\"p-s9\"><strong>S9.</strong> Producer/consumer already uses two CVs (<code>empty</code> and <code>fill</code>). Do the waits still need <code>while</code>, or would <code>if</code> now be safe? Give a scenario to justify your answer.</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em> <strong><code>while</code> is still needed.</strong> Two CVs make sure a signal wakes the right <em>kind</em> of thread, but not that the condition still holds when the woken thread runs. A scenario:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>The buffer is empty, and Tc1 is asleep on <code>fill</code>.</li>\n<li>The producer puts an item and signals <code>fill</code>, so Tc1 becomes ready.</li>\n<li>Before Tc1 runs, a newly arriving consumer Tc2 gets the mutex, sees count == 1, and takes the item.</li>\n<li>Tc1 finally returns from <code>wait</code>. With <code>if</code>, it calls <code>get()</code> on an empty buffer.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Spurious wakeups are a second reason. Under Mesa semantics, always use <code>while</code>.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/concurrency/practice-problems-lectures-14-to-17#p-s10","u":"osn/concurrency/practice-problems-lectures-14-to-17.html#p-s10","l":"S10","s":"","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"concurrency","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 14 to 17","q":"<p id=\"p-s10\"><strong>S10.</strong> For each, give the semaphore's initial value: (a) thread B's line <code>b1</code> must run after thread A's line <code>a1</code>; (b) at most 5 threads may hold a database connection at once; (c) a plain mutex; (d) the <code>full</code> semaphore of a bounded buffer with 8 slots; (e) its <code>empty</code> semaphore.</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em> <strong>Rule:</strong> the initial value is the number of threads that may pass <code>sem_wait</code> before anyone has called <code>sem_post</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Case</th>\n<th>Initial value</th>\n<th>Reason</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>(a) ordering: b1 after a1</td>\n<td><strong>0</strong></td>\n<td>B must wait until A posts; no one may pass at the start</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>(b) at most 5 connections</td>\n<td><strong>5</strong></td>\n<td>five threads may pass at once; the sixth blocks (throttling)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>(c) mutex</td>\n<td><strong>1</strong></td>\n<td>exactly one thread may enter at a time</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>(d) <code>full</code> of an 8-slot buffer</td>\n<td><strong>0</strong></td>\n<td>no slot is full at the start, so consumers must wait</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>(e) <code>empty</code> of an 8-slot buffer</td>\n<td><strong>8</strong></td>\n<td>all 8 slots are free at the start, so producers can proceed</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n"},{"id":"osn/concurrency/practice-problems-lectures-14-to-17#p-s11","u":"osn/concurrency/practice-problems-lectures-14-to-17.html#p-s11","l":"S11","s":"","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"concurrency","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 14 to 17","q":"<p id=\"p-s11\"><strong>S11.</strong> A semaphore starts at 2. T1, T2, T3, T4 and T5 call <code>sem_wait</code> in that order, then T1 and T2 each call <code>sem_post</code>. After each call, give the value and the list of waiting threads.</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em></p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Call</th>\n<th>Value</th>\n<th>Waiting</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Start</td>\n<td>2</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>T1 wait</td>\n<td>1</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>T2 wait</td>\n<td>0</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>T3 wait</td>\n<td>−1</td>\n<td>T3</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>T4 wait</td>\n<td>−2</td>\n<td>T3, T4</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>T5 wait</td>\n<td>−3</td>\n<td>T3, T4, T5</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>T1 post</td>\n<td>−2</td>\n<td>T4, T5 (T3 woken)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>T2 post</td>\n<td>−1</td>\n<td>T5 (T4 woken)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Rule:</strong> <code>sem_wait</code> decrements the value and blocks if the new value is negative; <code>sem_post</code> increments it and wakes one waiter if any are waiting. At every step, a negative value equals <strong>minus the number of waiting threads</strong>. Check: after T5's wait, value −3 = three waiters (T3, T4, T5). After two posts, value −1 = one waiter (T5). Waiters are woken in FIFO order.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/concurrency/practice-problems-lectures-14-to-17#p-s12","u":"osn/concurrency/practice-problems-lectures-14-to-17.html#p-s12","l":"S12","s":"","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"concurrency","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 14 to 17","q":"<p id=\"p-s12\"><strong>S12.</strong> In the bounded buffer with semaphores: (a) does the order of the two <code>sem_post</code> calls in the producer matter? (b) Does the order of the two <code>sem_wait</code> calls matter? Explain each.</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em></p>\n<p>(a) <strong>No.</strong> <code>sem_post</code> never blocks, so both posts always complete, in either order. Posting the mutex first just ends the critical section slightly sooner.</p>\n<p>(b) <strong>Yes.</strong> If a thread takes the mutex first and then waits on <code>full</code> or <code>empty</code>, it can go to sleep while holding the mutex. The thread that would wake it then blocks on the mutex, and both are stuck: the deadlock from 4.4. Always wait on <code>full</code>/<code>empty</code> first, then take the mutex.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/concurrency/practice-problems-lectures-14-to-17#p-s13","u":"osn/concurrency/practice-problems-lectures-14-to-17.html#p-s13","l":"S13","s":"","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"concurrency","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 14 to 17","q":"<p id=\"p-s13\"><strong>S13.</strong> Using semaphores only, make three threads print A, B and C in exactly that order every time, whatever order they are scheduled in. Then make two threads print ABABAB... forever.</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em> For A, then B, then C: use two semaphores, <code>s1</code> and <code>s2</code>, both initialized to 0.</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-c\">// Thread A            // Thread B            // Thread C\nprintf(&quot;A&quot;);           sem_wait(&amp;s1);         sem_wait(&amp;s2);\nsem_post(&amp;s1);         printf(&quot;B&quot;);           printf(&quot;C&quot;);\n                       sem_post(&amp;s2);\n</code></pre>\n<p>B cannot print until A has posted s1, and C cannot print until B has posted s2, whatever order the threads start in.</p>\n<p>For ABAB...: <code>sA</code> starts at 1 (A goes first) and <code>sB</code> starts at 0.</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-c\">// Thread A                       // Thread B\nwhile (1) {                       while (1) {\n    sem_wait(&amp;sA);                    sem_wait(&amp;sB);\n    printf(&quot;A&quot;);                      printf(&quot;B&quot;);\n    sem_post(&amp;sB);                    sem_post(&amp;sA);\n}                                 }\n</code></pre>\n<p>Each thread hands the turn to the other.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/concurrency/practice-problems-lectures-14-to-17#p-s14","u":"osn/concurrency/practice-problems-lectures-14-to-17.html#p-s14","l":"S14","s":"","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"concurrency","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 14 to 17","q":"<p id=\"p-s14\"><strong>S14.</strong> For the semaphore reader-writer lock: R1 holds a read lock, W calls <code>acquire_writelock</code>, then R2 calls <code>acquire_readlock</code>. What happens to W and R2, and what are <code>readers</code> and the value of <code>writelock</code>? Why is this unfair, and what is the general idea of the fix?</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em> While R1 is reading, readers = 1 and writelock = 0, because R1, as the first reader, took it.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>W calls <code>sem_wait(&amp;writelock)</code>: the value goes to −1, and <strong>W sleeps</strong>.</li>\n<li>R2 takes <code>lock</code>, increments readers to 2, and, because it is not the first reader, does not touch writelock. <strong>R2 enters immediately.</strong></li>\n<li>Now readers = 2, writelock = −1, and W is still waiting.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>This is unfair: W arrived before R2, but R2 got in first. W must wait until readers drops all the way to 0, and if readers keep arriving, that may never happen. The fix is to make new readers queue behind a waiting writer, with a turnstile semaphore that the writer holds while it waits (LBoS 4.2.3).</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/concurrency/practice-problems-lectures-14-to-17#p-s15","u":"osn/concurrency/practice-problems-lectures-14-to-17.html#p-s15","l":"S15","s":"","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"concurrency","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 14 to 17","q":"<p id=\"p-s15\"><strong>S15.</strong> Dining philosophers: (a) Does it also prevent deadlock if every <em>even-numbered</em> philosopher picks up the right fork first? (b) A different fix lets at most four philosophers try to eat at once, using a semaphore initialized to 4. Why does that prevent deadlock?</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em></p>\n<p>(a) <strong>Yes.</strong> With even philosophers taking the right fork first, P0 and P1 both reach for f1 first, and P2 and P3 both reach for f3 first. In each pair, the one who loses holds <strong>nothing</strong>. So the five philosophers can never all be holding one fork each, and the circular wait cannot close. In general, any arrangement where not everyone reaches in the same direction breaks the cycle.</p>\n<p>(b) With at most 4 philosophers at the table and 5 forks, even if all 4 hold one fork, one fork is still free. One of the seated philosophers is next to it, so that philosopher can take it, eat, and release both forks. Someone can always make progress, so there is no deadlock. This is the multiplex pattern (LBoS 4.4 calls the semaphore the &quot;footman&quot;).</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/concurrency/practice-problems-lectures-14-to-17#p-s16","u":"osn/concurrency/practice-problems-lectures-14-to-17.html#p-s16","l":"S16","s":"","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"concurrency","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 14 to 17","q":"<p id=\"p-s16\"><strong>S16.</strong> For each technique, name the deadlock condition it removes: (a) always locking in address order; (b) taking a global lock around all lock acquisitions; (c) <code>trylock</code> that releases what it holds on failure; (d) a lock-free counter using compare-and-swap.</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em> The four conditions are mutual exclusion, hold and wait, no preemption and circular wait. Prevention removes one of them.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Technique</th>\n<th>Condition removed</th>\n<th>Why</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>(a) lock in address (global) order</td>\n<td><strong>Circular wait</strong></td>\n<td>a total order on locks means no cycle of waits can form</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>(b) a global lock around all acquisitions</td>\n<td><strong>Hold and wait</strong></td>\n<td>a thread acquires all its locks atomically, so it never waits while holding one</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>(c) <code>trylock</code>, release everything on failure</td>\n<td><strong>No preemption</strong> (OSTEP's view)</td>\n<td>a thread effectively gives up locks it holds instead of waiting (it may livelock; add a random delay). Tutorial 6 files this under hold and wait; both views are acceptable</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>(d) lock-free counter with compare-and-swap</td>\n<td><strong>Mutual exclusion</strong></td>\n<td>no lock is held at all</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n"},{"id":"osn/concurrency/practice-problems-lectures-14-to-17#p-s17","u":"osn/concurrency/practice-problems-lectures-14-to-17.html#p-s17","l":"S17","s":"","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"concurrency","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 14 to 17","q":"<p id=\"p-s17\"><strong>S17.</strong> T1 holds A and waits for B. T2 holds B and waits for C. T3 holds C and is running, and will not request anything else. Is the system deadlocked? What if T3 then requests A?</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em> <strong>Method:</strong> draw the wait-for graph (an edge from each waiting thread to the holder of what it wants) and look for a cycle. With one instance of each resource, a cycle means deadlock.</p>\n<p><strong>First situation:</strong> T1 → T2 (T1 wants B, held by T2), T2 → T3 (T2 wants C, held by T3), and T3 waits for nothing. This is a chain with <strong>no cycle</strong>, so <strong>not deadlocked</strong>: T3 finishes and releases C, then T2 gets C, finishes and releases B, then T1 gets B.</p>\n<p><strong>If T3 then requests A:</strong> T3 → T1 closes the cycle T1 → T2 → T3 → T1. All four conditions hold (mutual exclusion, hold and wait, no preemption, circular wait): <strong>deadlock</strong>. Fix by a global lock order.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/concurrency/practice-problems-lectures-14-to-17#p-s18","u":"osn/concurrency/practice-problems-lectures-14-to-17.html#p-s18","l":"S18","s":"","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"concurrency","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 14 to 17","q":"<p id=\"p-s18\"><strong>S18.</strong> There are 12 units of a resource. T0 has maximum 10 and holds 5; T1 has maximum 4 and holds 2; T2 has maximum 9 and holds 3. Is this state safe under the Banker's algorithm? Explain.</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em> The free units are 12 − (5 + 2 + 3) = <strong>2</strong>. The remaining needs are T0: 5, T1: 2, T2: 6.</p>\n<p><strong>Method (Banker's safety check):</strong> find a thread whose remaining need is at most the free units; assume it runs to completion and returns <strong>everything it holds</strong>; repeat. If all threads can finish in some order, the state is safe.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>T1 can finish: its need 2 ≤ free 2. After it gets its 2 it holds 4, finishes, and returns all 4, so <strong>4 units are free</strong>.</li>\n<li>Now T0 needs 5 and T2 needs 6, but only 4 are free. Neither can be guaranteed to finish, and no other thread can run.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The state is <strong>unsafe</strong>. Unsafe does not mean deadlocked yet: it means a deadlock <em>can</em> happen if the threads ask for their maximums. This is exactly the state in 4.5's example after granting T2 one more unit, which is why the Banker's algorithm refuses that request.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/concurrency/practice-problems-lectures-14-to-17#p-s19","u":"osn/concurrency/practice-problems-lectures-14-to-17.html#p-s19","l":"S19","s":"","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"concurrency","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 14 to 17","q":"<p id=\"p-s19\"><strong>S19.</strong> Classify each bug as an atomicity violation, an order violation or a deadlock, and give a fix: (a) Thread 1 checks <code>if (q-&gt;head != NULL)</code> and then reads <code>q-&gt;head-&gt;value</code>, while Thread 2 may remove the head. (b) The main thread creates a worker that immediately uses a global config pointer, and only afterwards does the main thread set that pointer. (c) Function <code>transfer(a, b)</code> locks account a, then account b; two threads call <code>transfer(x, y)</code> and <code>transfer(y, x)</code>.</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em></p>\n<p>(a) <strong>Atomicity violation.</strong> The check and the use are meant to be one atomic step, but nothing makes them one. <strong>Fix:</strong> hold the queue's lock across both the check and the read, and in the remover.</p>\n<p>(b) <strong>Order violation.</strong> The worker assumes the pointer was set before it runs. <strong>Fix:</strong> set the pointer <em>before</em> creating the thread, or make the worker wait (on a semaphore initialized to 0, or a flag plus a CV) until main has set it.</p>\n<p>(c) <strong>Deadlock</strong> (circular wait): one thread holds x and wants y; the other holds y and wants x. <strong>Fix:</strong> always lock the two accounts in a global order, for example by account number or by address, whatever the argument order.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/concurrency/practice-problems-lectures-14-to-17#p-s20","u":"osn/concurrency/practice-problems-lectures-14-to-17.html#p-s20","l":"S20","s":"","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"concurrency","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 14 to 17","q":"<p id=\"p-s20\"><strong>S20.</strong> A program sometimes hangs. <code>top -H</code> shows its threads at 0% CPU. What is the likely bug class, and how would you find exactly where each thread is stuck? What if they showed 100% CPU instead?</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em> <strong>0% CPU</strong> means the threads are asleep, blocked. The likely cause is a <strong>deadlock</strong>, or a <strong>lost wakeup</strong> (a thread sleeping in <code>cond_wait</code> that nobody will ever signal, like Tutorial 6's &quot;forgot to signal&quot; variant). To find it:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Attach with <code>gdb -p $(pgrep prog)</code>.</li>\n<li>Run <code>thread apply all bt</code>, which shows exactly which <code>pthread_mutex_lock</code> or <code>pthread_cond_wait</code> call, on which line, each thread is stuck in.</li>\n<li>Helgrind can also report lock-order inversions directly.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>100% CPU with no progress</strong> suggests a <strong>livelock</strong>, or threads spinning on a lock or flag. Use <code>perf</code> or gdb backtraces to see where they are looping.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/concurrency/practice-problems-lectures-14-to-17#p-s21","u":"osn/concurrency/practice-problems-lectures-14-to-17.html#p-s21","l":"S21","s":"theory","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"concurrency","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 14 to 17","q":"<p id=\"p-s21\"><strong>S21 (theory).</strong> State the three requirements for a solution to the critical-section problem. For each of these, say which requirements it meets: (a) the load/store flag lock, (b) the test-and-set spin lock, (c) the ticket lock, (d) disabling interrupts on one CPU.</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em> The requirements are <strong>mutual exclusion, progress and bounded waiting</strong>. (a) The flag lock fails <strong>mutual exclusion</strong>: two threads can both see 0. (b) Test-and-set gives mutual exclusion and progress but <strong>not bounded waiting</strong>: a thread can lose every race. (c) The ticket lock meets <strong>all three</strong>, because tickets are served in FIFO order. (d) Disabling interrupts meets all three on a single CPU, but only if the code can be trusted not to keep interrupts off forever, and it does not work on several CPUs.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/concurrency/practice-problems-lectures-14-to-17#p-s22","u":"osn/concurrency/practice-problems-lectures-14-to-17.html#p-s22","l":"S22","s":"theory","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"concurrency","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 14 to 17","q":"<p id=\"p-s22\"><strong>S22 (theory).</strong> Write Peterson's algorithm for two threads. Prove that it gives mutual exclusion and bounded waiting. Why does it fail on a modern multicore CPU without extra help?</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em> See the Peterson section above for the code: <code>flag[self] = 1; turn = other; while (flag[other] &amp;&amp; turn == other) ;</code>. <strong>Mutual exclusion:</strong> suppose both are inside. Then both flags are 1, so T0 could only pass if turn = 0, and T1 only if turn = 1. turn holds one value, so this is impossible: whichever thread wrote turn last sees <code>turn == other</code> and spins. <strong>Bounded waiting:</strong> if T0 waits, then when T1 leaves and tries again, T1 sets turn = 0 and so lets T0 in first. T0 waits for at most one entry by T1. <strong>Modern hardware:</strong> CPUs and compilers may reorder the stores to flag and turn with the loads after them, so both threads can read the other's flag as 0. It needs memory barriers.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/concurrency/practice-problems-lectures-14-to-17#p-s23","u":"osn/concurrency/practice-problems-lectures-14-to-17.html#p-s23","l":"S23","s":"theory","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"concurrency","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 14 to 17","q":"<p id=\"p-s23\"><strong>S23 (theory).</strong> A program spends 20% of its time in serial code. (a) What is the maximum speedup on 4 cores, and on 16? (b) What is the limit as the cores grow without bound? (c) Explain the difference between concurrency and parallelism.</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em> (a) On 4 cores: $1 / (0.2 + 0.8/4) = 1 / 0.4 = $ <strong>2.5</strong>. On 16 cores: $1 / (0.2 + 0.05) = $ <strong>4</strong>. (b) $1/0.2 = $ <strong>5</strong>, no matter how many cores. (c) <strong>Concurrency:</strong> several tasks are in progress at once, possibly by interleaving on one core. <strong>Parallelism:</strong> tasks run at the same instant on different cores. Parallelism needs several cores; concurrency does not.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/concurrency/practice-problems-lectures-14-to-17#p-s24","u":"osn/concurrency/practice-problems-lectures-14-to-17.html#p-s24","l":"S24","s":"theory","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"concurrency","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 14 to 17","q":"<p id=\"p-s24\"><strong>S24 (theory).</strong> Compare user-level and kernel-level threads, and the many-to-one, one-to-one and many-to-many models. Which model does Linux use, and why?</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em> User-level threads are fast to create and switch (no system call), but one blocking system call blocks every thread in the process, and they cannot use several cores. Kernel threads can run in parallel and block independently, but each operation costs a system call. <strong>Many-to-one:</strong> no parallelism, and one block stops everything. <strong>One-to-one:</strong> true parallelism, at the cost of a kernel thread per user thread. <strong>Many-to-many:</strong> flexible, but complex to implement. Linux (and Windows) use <strong>one-to-one</strong>, because kernel threads are cheap enough today and the model is simple.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/concurrency/practice-problems-lectures-14-to-17#p-s25","u":"osn/concurrency/practice-problems-lectures-14-to-17.html#p-s25","l":"S25","s":"theory","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"concurrency","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 14 to 17","q":"<p id=\"p-s25\"><strong>S25 (theory).</strong> An approximate counter has 4 CPUs and threshold S = 100. (a) Describe update and get. (b) How far can the global count lag behind the true count? (c) What happens if S = 1, and if S = 10⁶?</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em> (a) <strong>Update:</strong> lock the local (per-CPU) counter and increment it. If it reaches S, lock the global counter, add the local value to it, and reset the local one to 0. <strong>Get:</strong> lock the global counter and return it (an approximation). (b) Each CPU can hold up to S − 1 unflushed updates, so the lag is up to about <strong>4 × 99 = 396</strong>. (c) With S = 1, every update touches the global lock, so it is exact but does not scale, like one lock. With S = 10⁶, it scales almost perfectly, but the global value can be wrong by millions.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/concurrency/practice-problems-lectures-14-to-17#p-s26","u":"osn/concurrency/practice-problems-lectures-14-to-17.html#p-s26","l":"S26","s":"theory","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"concurrency","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 14 to 17","q":"<p id=\"p-s26\"><strong>S26 (theory).</strong> Explain Hoare and Mesa semantics for <code>signal</code>. Show a case where <code>if</code> is safe under Hoare semantics but unsafe under Mesa semantics. Which do real systems use?</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em> <strong>Hoare:</strong> the signaller hands the lock and the CPU straight to the woken thread, so the condition is still true when that thread runs. <strong>Mesa:</strong> the woken thread only becomes ready, and runs later. Example: a consumer waits because count == 0. A producer puts an item and signals. Under Hoare, the consumer runs at once and the item is still there, so <code>if</code> is enough. Under Mesa, a second consumer can run first and take the item, so the woken consumer must re-check (<code>while</code>). Real systems (pthreads, Java, Linux) use <strong>Mesa</strong>, and also allow spurious wake-ups.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/concurrency/practice-problems-lectures-14-to-17#p-s27","u":"osn/concurrency/practice-problems-lectures-14-to-17.html#p-s27","l":"S27","s":"theory","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"concurrency","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 14 to 17","q":"<p id=\"p-s27\"><strong>S27 (theory).</strong> Draw the resource-allocation graph for: T1 holds R1 and requests R2; T2 holds R2 and requests R3; T3 holds R3. (a) Is there a deadlock? (b) T3 now requests R1. Now? (c) If R2 had two instances, would a cycle always mean deadlock?</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em> The edges are R1 → T1, T1 → R2, R2 → T2, T2 → R3, R3 → T3. (a) <strong>No cycle, so no deadlock.</strong> T3 can finish and release R3, then T2, then T1. (b) Adding T3 → R1 creates the cycle T1 → R2 → T2 → R3 → T3 → R1 → T1. With one instance of each resource, a cycle <strong>is</strong> a deadlock. (c) <strong>No.</strong> With several instances, a cycle is necessary but not sufficient: another holder of R2 outside the cycle may finish and free an instance, which breaks the wait.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/concurrency/practice-problems-lectures-14-to-17#p-s28","u":"osn/concurrency/practice-problems-lectures-14-to-17.html#p-s28","l":"S28","s":"theory","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"concurrency","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 14 to 17","q":"<p id=\"p-s28\"><strong>S28 (theory).</strong> How does a kernel implement a blocking semaphore? Why does it still need a spinlock or disabled interrupts, and why is that acceptable? How can the waiting list cause starvation?</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em> The semaphore holds a value and a <strong>list of waiting processes</strong>. <code>wait</code>: decrement; if the value is negative, add the caller to the list and block it (taking it off the run queue). <code>signal</code>: increment; if the value is ≤ 0, remove a process from the list and wake it (make it ready). The updates to the value and the list must be atomic, so the kernel disables interrupts (one CPU) or takes a spinlock (many CPUs). This is acceptable because the critical sections are only a few instructions long, unlike the application's critical section, which may be long. If the list is <strong>LIFO</strong>, a waiter can be overtaken forever (starvation). A <strong>FIFO</strong> list gives bounded waiting.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/filesystems/practice-problems-lectures-18-and-19#p-f1","u":"osn/filesystems/practice-problems-lectures-18-and-19.html#p-f1","l":"F1","s":"","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"filesystems","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 18 and 19","q":"<p id=\"p-f1\"><strong>F1.</strong> A process runs the code below on a 300-byte file. What does each call return, and what is the offset after each?</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-c\">int fd = open(&quot;file&quot;, O_RDONLY);\nread(fd, buf, 120);\nlseek(fd, -50, SEEK_END);\nread(fd, buf, 100);\nread(fd, buf, 100);\n</code></pre>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em> <strong>Rules:</strong> <code>open</code> returns the lowest free fd; <code>read</code> returns the number of bytes actually read (fewer at the end of the file, 0 at EOF) and advances the offset by that amount; <code>lseek(fd, off, SEEK_END)</code> sets the offset to file size + off.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Call</th>\n<th>Returns</th>\n<th>Offset after</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code>open</code></td>\n<td><strong>3</strong> (fds 0, 1, 2 are taken)</td>\n<td>0</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>read(fd, buf, 120)</code></td>\n<td><strong>120</strong></td>\n<td>120</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>lseek(fd, -50, SEEK_END)</code></td>\n<td><strong>250</strong> (300 − 50)</td>\n<td>250</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>read(fd, buf, 100)</code></td>\n<td><strong>50</strong> (only 50 bytes remain)</td>\n<td>300</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>read(fd, buf, 100)</code></td>\n<td><strong>0</strong> (end of file)</td>\n<td>300</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n"},{"id":"osn/filesystems/practice-problems-lectures-18-and-19#p-f2","u":"osn/filesystems/practice-problems-lectures-18-and-19.html#p-f2","l":"F2","s":"","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"filesystems","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 18 and 19","q":"<p id=\"p-f2\"><strong>F2.</strong> A parent opens a file, then forks. The child reads 100 bytes, then exits. The parent then reads 100 bytes. Which bytes does the parent get? What changes if the child instead opens the file itself after the fork?</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em> Each process has an <strong>fd table</strong> whose entries point into the system-wide <strong>open-file table</strong>; each open-file entry holds the <strong>current offset</strong>, the mode and a reference count. After <code>fork()</code> the parent's and child's fds point to the <strong>same open-file-table entry</strong>, so they <strong>share one offset</strong> (the reference count becomes 2). The child's read moves the shared offset to 100, so the parent's read returns <strong>bytes 100 to 199</strong>. If the child calls <code>open()</code> itself, it gets a <strong>new table entry</strong> with its <strong>own offset</strong>, so the parent's offset is unaffected and it reads <strong>bytes 0 to 99</strong>.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/filesystems/practice-problems-lectures-18-and-19#p-f3","u":"osn/filesystems/practice-problems-lectures-18-and-19.html#p-f3","l":"F3","s":"","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"filesystems","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 18 and 19","q":"<p id=\"p-f3\"><strong>F3.</strong> You run <code>echo hi &gt; a; ln a b; ln -s a c; rm a</code>. What do <code>cat b</code> and <code>cat c</code> print, and what are the link counts?</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em> Step by step:</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Command</th>\n<th>Effect</th>\n<th>Link count of the file's inode</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code>echo hi &gt; a</code></td>\n<td>creates inode I with name <code>a</code></td>\n<td>1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>ln a b</code></td>\n<td><code>b</code> is a second name for the <strong>same inode</strong></td>\n<td>2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>ln -s a c</code></td>\n<td><code>c</code> is a <strong>new inode</strong> whose data is the path &quot;a&quot;</td>\n<td>2 (unchanged)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>rm a</code></td>\n<td><code>unlink(&quot;a&quot;)</code> removes the name <code>a</code></td>\n<td><strong>1</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><code>cat b</code> prints <strong>hi</strong>: the inode and data still exist, reachable through <code>b</code>. <code>cat c</code> follows the stored path &quot;a&quot;, which no longer exists: <strong>&quot;No such file or directory&quot;</strong> (a <strong>dangling symlink</strong>). A file is really deleted only when its link count reaches 0 and no process has it open.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/filesystems/practice-problems-lectures-18-and-19#p-f4","u":"osn/filesystems/practice-problems-lectures-18-and-19.html#p-f4","l":"F4","s":"","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"filesystems","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 18 and 19","q":"<p id=\"p-f4\"><strong>F4.</strong> Why are hard links to directories not allowed, and why can't a hard link cross file systems?</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em> <strong>Directories:</strong> a hard link to a directory could create a <strong>cycle</strong> in the tree (a directory that contains a link to one of its own ancestors). That breaks tools that walk the tree (<code>find</code>, <code>du</code> would loop forever), makes <code>..</code> ambiguous (a directory could have several parents), and breaks reference counting (a cycle never reaches link count 0, so it is never freed). <strong>Across file systems:</strong> a directory entry stores only an <strong>inode number</strong>, and inode numbers are unique only <strong>within one file system</strong>, so an entry in one file system cannot name an inode in another. <strong>Symbolic links</strong> store a <strong>path</strong> instead, so they have neither problem.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/filesystems/practice-problems-lectures-18-and-19#p-f5","u":"osn/filesystems/practice-problems-lectures-18-and-19.html#p-f5","l":"F5","s":"","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"filesystems","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 18 and 19","q":"<p id=\"p-f5\"><strong>F5.</strong> An editor wants a crash during save never to leave a half-written file. Give the sequence of calls and say why each is needed.</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em> <strong>Sequence and purpose:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li><code>open(&quot;foo.txt.tmp&quot;, O_CREAT | O_WRONLY | O_TRUNC)</code> and <code>write()</code> the new contents: the original file is untouched, so a crash now loses only the temp file.</li>\n<li><strong><code>fsync(fd)</code>:</strong> forces the temp file's data and inode to disk. Without it, the OS may still hold the data in memory when the rename reaches the disk.</li>\n<li><code>close(fd)</code>.</li>\n<li><strong><code>rename(&quot;foo.txt.tmp&quot;, &quot;foo.txt&quot;)</code>:</strong> this is <strong>atomic</strong>, so after a crash <code>foo.txt</code> is either the complete old file or the complete new file, never a mixture.</li>\n<li>(For full safety) <code>fsync</code> the <strong>directory</strong> so that the rename itself is durable.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Without step 2, the rename could be written before the data, and a crash would leave <code>foo.txt</code> pointing to empty or garbage blocks.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/filesystems/practice-problems-lectures-18-and-19#p-f6","u":"osn/filesystems/practice-problems-lectures-18-and-19.html#p-f6","l":"F6","s":"","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"filesystems","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 18 and 19","q":"<p id=\"p-f6\"><strong>F6. (slides)</strong> vsfs: 4 KB blocks, 256-byte inodes, inode table starting at 12 KB, 512-byte sectors. Find the inode block, the sector and the offset inside the block for inode 70. Then do inode 45.</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em> <strong>Formulas (slides):</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-d\" data-tex=\"\\text{blk} = \\left\\lfloor\\frac{i \\times 256}{4096}\\right\\rfloor \\qquad \\text{address} = 12\\text{ KB} + \\text{blk} \\times 4\\text{ KB} \\qquad \\text{sector} = \\frac{\\text{address}}{512} \\qquad \\text{offset in block} = (i \\bmod 16) \\times 256\"><span class=\"katex-display\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.6944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord text\"><span class=\"mord\">blk</span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:2.4em;vertical-align:-0.95em;\"></span><span class=\"minner\"><span class=\"mopen delimcenter\" style=\"top:0em;\"><span class=\"delimsizing size3\">⌊</span></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mopen nulldelimiter\"></span><span class=\"mfrac\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:1.3365em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.314em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord\">4096</span></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.23em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"frac-line\" style=\"border-bottom-width:0.04em;\"></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.677em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">i</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">×</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">256</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.686em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mclose nulldelimiter\"></span></span><span class=\"mclose delimcenter\" style=\"top:0em;\"><span class=\"delimsizing size3\">⌋</span></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:2em;\"></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.1667em;\"></span><span class=\"mord text\"><span class=\"mord\">address</span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.7667em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">12</span><span class=\"mord text\"><span class=\"mord\"> KB</span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.7778em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord text\"><span class=\"mord\">blk</span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">×</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">4</span><span class=\"mord text\"><span class=\"mord\"> KB</span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:2em;\"></span><span class=\"mord text\"><span class=\"mord\">sector</span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:2.0574em;vertical-align:-0.686em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mopen nulldelimiter\"></span><span class=\"mfrac\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:1.3714em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.314em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord\">512</span></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.23em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"frac-line\" style=\"border-bottom-width:0.04em;\"></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.677em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord text\"><span class=\"mord\">address</span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.686em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mclose nulldelimiter\"></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:2em;\"></span><span class=\"mord text\"><span class=\"mord\">offset in block</span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">i</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.0556em;\"></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\"><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathrm\">mod</span></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.0556em;\"></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">16</span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">×</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">256</span></span></span></span></span></div>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>Inode 70</th>\n<th>Inode 45</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Offset in the table = i × 256</td>\n<td>17,920 B</td>\n<td>11,520 B</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Inode block = offset ÷ 4096</td>\n<td><strong>4</strong></td>\n<td><strong>2</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Block address = 12 KB + blk × 4 KB</td>\n<td>28 KB = 28,672</td>\n<td>20 KB = 20,480</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Sector = address ÷ 512</td>\n<td><strong>56</strong></td>\n<td><strong>40</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Offset within block = (i mod 16) × 256</td>\n<td>6 × 256 = <strong>1536 B</strong></td>\n<td>13 × 256 = <strong>3328 B</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>(16 inodes fit in a 4 KB block.)</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/filesystems/practice-problems-lectures-18-and-19#p-f7","u":"osn/filesystems/practice-problems-lectures-18-and-19.html#p-f7","l":"F7","s":"","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"filesystems","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 18 and 19","q":"<p id=\"p-f7\"><strong>F7.</strong> A file system has 4 KB blocks, 4-byte block pointers, 12 direct pointers, and one single, one double and one triple indirect pointer. (a) What is the maximum file size? (b) Which pointer holds byte offset 100,000? (c) Byte offset 10,000,000?</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em> <strong>(a)</strong> A block holds 4096 / 4 = 1024 pointers. <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.4306em;\"></span><span class=\"mord text\"><span class=\"mord\">max</span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord\">12</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">1024</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.8974em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">102</span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord\">4</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.8141em;\"><span style=\"top:-3.063em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"katex-sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">2</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:1.0641em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">102</span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord\">4</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.8141em;\"><span style=\"top:-3.063em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"katex-sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">3</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">×</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">4</span><span class=\"mord text\"><span class=\"mord\"> KB</span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">≈</span></span></span></span> <strong>4 TB</strong> (just over <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.8141em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord\">2</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.8141em;\"><span style=\"top:-3.063em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"katex-sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">42</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span> bytes; the triple-indirect term dominates).</p>\n<p><strong>Method for (b) and (c):</strong> block index = offset ÷ 4096. If it is &lt; 12, a direct pointer serves it. Otherwise subtract 12; if the result is &lt; 1024, it is in the single-indirect block at that slot. Otherwise subtract 1024; if the result is &lt; 1024², it is in the double-indirect tree (outer slot = result ÷ 1024, inner slot = result mod 1024).</p>\n<p><strong>(b)</strong> 100,000 ÷ 4096 = 24.4, so block <strong>24</strong>. 24 ≥ 12, and 24 − 12 = 12 &lt; 1024: <strong>single-indirect pointer, slot 12</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>(c)</strong> 10,000,000 ÷ 4096 = 2441.4, so block <strong>2441</strong>. 2441 − 12 = 2429 ≥ 1024, so subtract again: 2429 − 1024 = 1405 &lt; 1024². <strong>Double-indirect: outer slot 1405 ÷ 1024 = 1, inner slot 1405 mod 1024 = 381.</strong> (Disk reads to get the data: inode, double-indirect block, indirect block, data block.)</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/filesystems/practice-problems-lectures-18-and-19#p-f8","u":"osn/filesystems/practice-problems-lectures-18-and-19.html#p-f8","l":"F8","s":"","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"filesystems","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 18 and 19","q":"<p id=\"p-f8\"><strong>F8.</strong> Redo F7(a) for 1 KB blocks with the same pointers. Then compute xv6's maximum file size.</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em> A 1 KB block holds 1024 / 4 = <strong>256 pointers</strong>. <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.4306em;\"></span><span class=\"mord text\"><span class=\"mord\">max</span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord\">12</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">256</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.8974em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">25</span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord\">6</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.8141em;\"><span style=\"top:-3.063em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"katex-sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">2</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:1.0641em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">25</span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord\">6</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.8141em;\"><span style=\"top:-3.063em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"katex-sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">3</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">×</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">1</span><span class=\"mord text\"><span class=\"mord\"> KB</span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.8778em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">16</span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mpunct\">,</span></span><span class=\"mord\">843</span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mpunct\">,</span></span><span class=\"mord\">020</span><span class=\"mord text\"><span class=\"mord\"> KB</span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">≈</span></span></span></span> <strong>16 GB</strong>. <strong>xv6</strong> has only 12 direct pointers and one single-indirect pointer (NDIRECT = 12, NINDIRECT = 256): (12 + 256) blocks × 1 KB = <strong>268 KB</strong>.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/filesystems/practice-problems-lectures-18-and-19#p-f9","u":"osn/filesystems/practice-problems-lectures-18-and-19.html#p-f9","l":"F9","s":"","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"filesystems","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 18 and 19","q":"<p id=\"p-f9\"><strong>F9.</strong> How many disk reads does <code>open(&quot;/usr/lib/libc.so&quot;)</code> need with a cold cache, assuming each directory fits in one block?</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em> <strong>Rule:</strong> 2 reads for each directory in the path (its inode, then its data block), plus 1 read for the target's inode: 2 × depth + 1. Here the directories are <code>/</code>, <code>usr</code> and <code>lib</code> (depth 3): root inode, root data, <code>usr</code> inode, <code>usr</code> data, <code>lib</code> inode, <code>lib</code> data, then <code>libc.so</code>'s inode = <strong>7 reads</strong>. The superblock is already in memory after mount, and the root inode's location is known.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/filesystems/practice-problems-lectures-18-and-19#p-f10","u":"osn/filesystems/practice-problems-lectures-18-and-19.html#p-f10","l":"F10","s":"","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"filesystems","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 18 and 19","q":"<p id=\"p-f10\"><strong>F10. (slides)</strong> How many I/Os does it take to create <code>/foo/bar</code> and write 12 KB to it in vsfs (4 KB blocks, cold cache)? Which are user data?</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em> <strong>Create = 10 I/Os:</strong> read the root inode, read the root data, read foo's inode, read foo's data (4 reads to walk the path); read the inode bitmap, write the inode bitmap; write foo's data (the new entry); read bar's new inode, write bar's inode; write foo's inode. <strong>Each allocating write = 5 I/Os:</strong> read bar's inode, read the data bitmap, write the data bitmap, write the data block, write bar's inode. 12 KB = 3 blocks of 4 KB, so 3 × 5 = 15. <strong>Total = 10 + 15 = 25 I/Os</strong>, and <strong>only 3 of them</strong> write user data; the rest is metadata overhead.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/filesystems/practice-problems-lectures-18-and-19#p-f11","u":"osn/filesystems/practice-problems-lectures-18-and-19.html#p-f11","l":"F11","s":"","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"filesystems","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 18 and 19","q":"<p id=\"p-f11\"><strong>F11.</strong> Why does <code>read()</code> write the inode, and why does it never touch a bitmap? How could you avoid that write?</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em> A read must update the inode's <strong>last-access time</strong> (<code>atime</code>), so after reading the data it <strong>writes the inode back</strong> (read inode, read data, write inode = 3 I/Os per block). It touches <strong>no bitmap</strong> because reading never allocates or frees an inode or a block. To avoid the write, mount with <strong><code>noatime</code></strong> (never update atime) or <strong><code>relatime</code></strong> (update only if atime is older than mtime, or about once a day).</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/filesystems/practice-problems-lectures-18-and-19#p-f12","u":"osn/filesystems/practice-problems-lectures-18-and-19.html#p-f12","l":"F12","s":"","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"filesystems","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 18 and 19","q":"<p id=\"p-f12\"><strong>F12. (slides)</strong> In <code>vsfs.py</code> the state is <code>inodes [d a:0 r:2] [] [] []</code>, <code>data [(.,0) (..,0)] [] [] []</code>. Show the state after <code>mkdir(&quot;/g&quot;)</code>, then <code>creat(&quot;/g/q&quot;)</code>, then <code>link(&quot;/g/q&quot;, &quot;/w&quot;)</code>.</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em> <code>mkdir(&quot;/g&quot;)</code>: inodes <code>[d a:0 r:3] [d a:1 r:2] [] []</code> (root's count +1 for g's <code>..</code>), data <code>[(.,0) (..,0) (g,1)] [(.,1) (..,0)] [] []</code>. <code>creat(&quot;/g/q&quot;)</code>: inode 2 = <code>[f a:-1 r:1]</code> (no data block yet), g's block gets <code>(q,2)</code>. <code>link</code>: root's block gets <code>(w,2)</code> and inode 2 becomes <code>[f a:-1 r:2]</code>. No new inode or data block.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/filesystems/practice-problems-lectures-18-and-19#p-f13","u":"osn/filesystems/practice-problems-lectures-18-and-19.html#p-f13","l":"F13","s":"","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"filesystems","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 18 and 19","q":"<p id=\"p-f13\"><strong>F13.</strong> Compare pointer-based allocation, extents and FAT on (a) metadata for a 1 GB contiguous file, (b) random access, (c) hard links.</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em></p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>Pointer-based (inode)</th>\n<th>Extents</th>\n<th>FAT</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>(a) Metadata for a 1 GB contiguous file (262,144 blocks of 4 KB)</td>\n<td>One pointer per block (about 262,144 pointers plus indirect blocks)</td>\n<td><strong>One extent</strong> (start, length)</td>\n<td>One table entry per block (262,144 entries)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>(b) Random access</td>\n<td>Up to 3 extra indirect reads</td>\n<td>A short search over the extent list</td>\n<td><strong>Walk the chain</strong> (only fast because the table is cached in memory)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>(c) Hard links</td>\n<td>Yes (the link count is in the inode)</td>\n<td>Yes (inode-based)</td>\n<td><strong>No</strong>: there are no inodes, so no place for a link count</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n"},{"id":"osn/filesystems/practice-problems-lectures-18-and-19#p-f14","u":"osn/filesystems/practice-problems-lectures-18-and-19.html#p-f14","l":"F14","s":"","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"filesystems","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 18 and 19","q":"<p id=\"p-f14\"><strong>F14.</strong> Give one example each of batching, scheduling and avoidance by write buffering, and say what an application that cannot lose data should do.</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em> Batching: two files created in the same inode-bitmap block → one bitmap write instead of two. Scheduling: 100 scattered dirty blocks written in disk order instead of arrival order. Avoidance: a temp file created and deleted within 30 s → no I/O at all. An application that cannot lose data calls <strong><code>fsync()</code></strong> at commit points (or uses <code>O_DIRECT</code>).</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/filesystems/practice-problems-lectures-18-and-19#p-f15","u":"osn/filesystems/practice-problems-lectures-18-and-19.html#p-f15","l":"F15","s":"","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"filesystems","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 18 and 19","q":"<p id=\"p-f15\"><strong>F15.</strong> In xv6, why does <code>filewrite</code> split a large write into several <code>begin_op</code>/<code>end_op</code> transactions? Why does <code>writei</code> call <code>iupdate</code> even when the size did not change?</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em> <strong>Splitting:</strong> every file-system operation runs inside a transaction (<code>begin_op</code> ... <code>end_op</code>), and the transaction's modified blocks are first written to the <strong>log</strong>, which has limited room. One transaction may write at most <code>MAXOPBLOCKS</code> = 10 blocks. A single large write could touch far more: data blocks, bitmap blocks, the inode, the indirect block, and extra blocks if the start and end are not block-aligned. So <code>filewrite</code> writes in <strong>chunks of at most 3 data blocks</strong>, each in its own <code>begin_op</code>/<code>end_op</code>, so that every chunk fits in the log. Each chunk is atomic (all or nothing), but a crash between chunks leaves a partial write. <strong><code>iupdate</code>:</strong> <code>writei</code> always calls <code>iupdate(ip)</code> because <code>bmap</code> may have <strong>allocated a new block</strong> and stored its address in <code>ip-&gt;addrs[]</code> (and the size may have grown), and those changes in the in-memory inode must be written to the on-disk inode.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/filesystems/practice-problems-lectures-18-and-19#p-f16","u":"osn/filesystems/practice-problems-lectures-18-and-19.html#p-f16","l":"F16","s":"theory","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"filesystems","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 18 and 19","q":"<p id=\"p-f16\"><strong>F16 (theory).</strong> Compare contiguous, linked (including FAT) and indexed allocation on sequential access, random access, fragmentation and file growth. Which one is the UNIX inode, and why?</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em> <strong>Contiguous:</strong> sequential and random access are both fast (block i is at start + i), but it causes <strong>external fragmentation</strong> and files are hard to grow. <strong>Linked:</strong> no external fragmentation and growth is easy, but random access must walk the chain (slow), and each block loses space to a pointer. <strong>FAT</strong> keeps the chain in a table in memory, which makes random access fast, but there are no inodes, so no hard links. <strong>Indexed:</strong> fast random access with no external fragmentation, but small files waste an index block, and large files need several levels. The UNIX inode is a <strong>combined indexed scheme</strong>: direct pointers for the many small files, and single, double and triple indirect blocks for the few large ones.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/filesystems/practice-problems-lectures-18-and-19#p-f17","u":"osn/filesystems/practice-problems-lectures-18-and-19.html#p-f17","l":"F17","s":"theory","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"filesystems","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 18 and 19","q":"<p id=\"p-f17\"><strong>F17 (theory).</strong> A disk has 2 TB and 4 KB blocks. (a) How big is a free-space bitmap? (b) Compare the bitmap with a free list, grouping and counting.</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em> (a) 2 TB / 4 KB = <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:1.0641em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord\">2</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.8141em;\"><span style=\"top:-3.063em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"katex-sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">41</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mord\">/</span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord\">2</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.8141em;\"><span style=\"top:-3.063em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"katex-sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">12</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.8141em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord\">2</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.8141em;\"><span style=\"top:-3.063em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"katex-sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">29</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span> blocks, so <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.8141em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord\">2</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.8141em;\"><span style=\"top:-3.063em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"katex-sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">29</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span> bits = <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.8141em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord\">2</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.8141em;\"><span style=\"top:-3.063em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"katex-sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">26</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span> bytes = <strong>64 MB</strong>. (b) The <strong>bitmap</strong> makes runs of free blocks easy to find, but must be cached in memory to be fast. A <strong>free list</strong> costs no extra space, but finding n free blocks takes n disk reads. <strong>Grouping</strong> stores n free addresses per block, so one read finds many. <strong>Counting</strong> stores (start, length) runs, which is very compact when free space comes in contiguous runs.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/filesystems/practice-problems-lectures-18-and-19#p-f18","u":"osn/filesystems/practice-problems-lectures-18-and-19.html#p-f18","l":"F18","s":"theory","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"filesystems","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 18 and 19","q":"<p id=\"p-f18\"><strong>F18 (theory).</strong> Describe single-level, two-level, tree, acyclic-graph and general-graph directories. Why does UNIX forbid hard links to directories?</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em> <strong>Single-level:</strong> one directory for everyone, so names clash. <strong>Two-level:</strong> one directory per user, which makes sharing hard. <strong>Tree:</strong> arbitrary depth, with absolute and relative paths, but no sharing. <strong>Acyclic graph:</strong> files can appear in several directories through links, so deletion needs reference counts (hard links) or leaves dangling links (soft links). <strong>General graph:</strong> cycles are allowed, so traversal can loop, and a file can be unreachable but still counted, which needs garbage collection. UNIX forbids hard links to directories so the directory structure stays <strong>acyclic</strong>: <code>..</code> stays well defined, tools like <code>find</code> cannot loop, and link counts reach 0 correctly.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/filesystems/practice-problems-lectures-18-and-19#p-f19","u":"osn/filesystems/practice-problems-lectures-18-and-19.html#p-f19","l":"F19","s":"theory","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"filesystems","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 18 and 19","q":"<p id=\"p-f19\"><strong>F19 (theory).</strong> An append writes a data block, the inode and the data bitmap. For each case where exactly one or exactly two of these writes reach disk before a crash, say whether the file system is consistent and what the user sees.</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em> <strong>Data only:</strong> consistent; the append is simply lost. <strong>Inode only:</strong> inconsistent (the inode points to a block the bitmap says is free), and the file reads garbage. <strong>Bitmap only:</strong> inconsistent, with a <strong>space leak</strong> (the block is used but nothing points to it). <strong>Inode and bitmap:</strong> metadata consistent, but the file contains <strong>garbage</strong>. <strong>Inode and data:</strong> inconsistent with the bitmap (the block could later be given to another file). <strong>Bitmap and data:</strong> space leak, and the data is unreachable.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/filesystems/practice-problems-lectures-18-and-19#p-f20","u":"osn/filesystems/practice-problems-lectures-18-and-19.html#p-f20","l":"F20","s":"theory","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"filesystems","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 18 and 19","q":"<p id=\"p-f20\"><strong>F20 (theory).</strong> Explain how journaling recovers from a crash. What is the commit point? How does metadata (ordered) journaling differ from data journaling, and why must the data block be written first?</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em> (1) <strong>Journal write:</strong> write TxBegin plus the new inode, bitmap (and data) blocks to the log. (2) <strong>Commit:</strong> write TxEnd. Once TxEnd is on disk, the transaction is <strong>committed</strong>: this is the commit point. (3) <strong>Checkpoint:</strong> write the blocks to their real locations. (4) <strong>Free</strong> the journal space. On recovery, replay every committed transaction and discard incomplete ones, so each update happens fully or not at all. <strong>Data journaling</strong> logs the user data too, so data is written twice. <strong>Metadata journaling</strong> logs only the inode and bitmap, and writes the data block to its final location <strong>before</strong> the commit. Otherwise a committed inode could point to a block still holding old garbage. Compared with fsck, recovery only reads the journal, not the whole disk.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/filesystems/practice-problems-lectures-18-and-19#p-f21","u":"osn/filesystems/practice-problems-lectures-18-and-19.html#p-f21","l":"F21","s":"theory","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"filesystems","c":"Practice problems, Lectures 18 and 19","q":"<p id=\"p-f21\"><strong>F21 (theory).</strong> Give the three access methods, with one example where each is natural, and show how direct access is done with UNIX system calls.</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em> <strong>Sequential:</strong> read the next record in order, for example an editor loading a file or a compiler reading source. <strong>Direct (random):</strong> jump to block or record n, for example a database looking up a row. <strong>Indexed:</strong> look up a key in an index, then access the block directly, for example a sorted product file. In UNIX, direct access is <code>lseek(fd, n * recsize, SEEK_SET)</code> followed by <code>read(fd, buf, recsize)</code>.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/endsem/endsem-practice-the-professor-s-sheet#p-e1","u":"osn/endsem/endsem-practice-the-professor-s-sheet.html#p-e1","l":"E1","s":"sheet Q1","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"endsem","c":"Endsem practice: the professor's sheet","q":"<p id=\"p-e1\"><strong>E1 (sheet Q1).</strong> A process has a 16 KB address space, loaded with base = 32 KB and bounds = 16 KB (bounds holds the size). (a) Translate 0, 1024, 3000, 4400, 16384 and 18000, or say which fault. [5] (b) Who sets base and bounds, when, and why must those instructions be privileged? [3] (c) Why does base and bounds waste memory? [2]</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(a) Rule.</strong> The hardware (MMU) first checks the <strong>bounds</strong>, then adds the <strong>base</strong>:</p>\n<div class=\"math-d\" data-tex=\"0 \\le VA &lt; \\text{bounds} \\;\\Rightarrow\\; PA = VA + \\text{base}; \\quad \\text{otherwise raise an exception (fault)}\"><span class=\"katex-display\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.7804em;vertical-align:-0.136em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">0</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">≤</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.7224em;vertical-align:-0.0391em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\">V</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">A</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">&lt;</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.6944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord text\"><span class=\"mord\">bounds</span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">⇒</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.1389em;\">P</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">A</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.7667em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\">V</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">A</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord text\"><span class=\"mord\">base</span></span><span class=\"mpunct\">;</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:1em;\"></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.1667em;\"></span><span class=\"mord text\"><span class=\"mord\">otherwise raise an exception (fault)</span></span></span></span></span></span></div>\n<p>Here base = 32 KB = 32768 and bounds = 16 KB = 16384. Legal virtual addresses are 0 to 16383.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>VA</th>\n<th>Bounds check (VA &lt; 16384?)</th>\n<th>Working</th>\n<th>Result</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>0</td>\n<td>yes</td>\n<td>0 + 32768</td>\n<td><strong>32768</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1024</td>\n<td>yes</td>\n<td>1024 + 32768</td>\n<td><strong>33792</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3000</td>\n<td>yes</td>\n<td>3000 + 32768</td>\n<td><strong>35768</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4400</td>\n<td>yes</td>\n<td>4400 + 32768</td>\n<td><strong>37168</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>16384</td>\n<td><strong>no</strong> (VA = bounds)</td>\n<td>the last legal address is 16383</td>\n<td><strong>Fault</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>18000</td>\n<td><strong>no</strong> (18000 &gt; 16383)</td>\n<td></td>\n<td><strong>Fault</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><em>Watch out:</em> with the textbook's 4 KB address space, 4400 would fault. Here the space is 16 KB, so it is valid. Always compare with <em>this</em> question's bounds. Also, the check is <code>VA &lt; bounds</code>, <strong>not</strong> <code>VA &lt;= bounds</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>(b)</strong> The <strong>OS</strong>, running in <strong>kernel mode</strong>, sets the registers. It does so <strong>when it creates the process</strong> (loads the base chosen from the free list and the bounds from the address-space size) and <strong>on every context switch</strong>, where it saves the old process's values in its PCB and restores the new process's. The instructions must be <strong>privileged</strong> because, if a user program could write base or bounds, it could set them to cover another process's memory or the kernel, which destroys <strong>protection and isolation</strong>. A user attempt to execute them traps to the OS.</p>\n<p><strong>(c)</strong> The whole address space (16 KB) is placed in one contiguous slot, including the large <strong>unused gap between the heap and the stack</strong>. That reserved but unused space inside the process's slot is <strong>internal fragmentation</strong>. A process with a small heap and a small stack still occupies its full slot. This motivates segmentation (a base and bounds pair per segment) and later paging.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/endsem/endsem-practice-the-professor-s-sheet#p-e2","u":"osn/endsem/endsem-practice-the-professor-s-sheet.html#p-e2","l":"E2","s":"sheet Q2","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"endsem","c":"Endsem practice: the professor's sheet","q":"<p id=\"p-e2\"><strong>E2 (sheet Q2).</strong> The OS moves a descheduled process's address space to another region of physical memory. List the steps, and explain why the process never notices. [10]</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Steps</strong> (the process is not running, so its memory is quiet):</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Deschedule</strong> the process (it is already in the ready or blocked state).</li>\n<li><strong>Choose a new region</strong> from the <strong>free list</strong> that is big enough for the whole address space.</li>\n<li><strong>Copy</strong> the address space (code, heap, stack) from the old region to the new one.</li>\n<li><strong>Update the saved base</strong> in the process's <strong>PCB</strong> to the new region's start address. The bounds are unchanged because the size is unchanged.</li>\n<li><strong>Return the old region</strong> to the free list.</li>\n<li>When the process is next scheduled, the OS <strong>loads the new base</strong> into the MMU's base register as part of the context switch.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Why the process never notices.</strong> The process only ever generates <strong>virtual addresses</strong> that start at 0. The hardware adds the base <strong>on every access</strong>, so the process's addresses, pointers and bounds are unchanged; only the hidden VA-to-PA mapping moved. This is exactly why <strong>dynamic relocation</strong> (base and bounds) can move a process after it has started, whereas <strong>static (software) relocation</strong>, where a loader rewrites addresses once, cannot: the program would then hold stale physical addresses. The cost is that the copy takes time proportional to the size of the address space.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/endsem/endsem-practice-the-professor-s-sheet#p-e3","u":"osn/endsem/endsem-practice-the-professor-s-sheet.html#p-e3","l":"E3","s":"sheet Q3","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"endsem","c":"Endsem practice: the professor's sheet","q":"<p id=\"p-e3\"><strong>E3 (sheet Q3).</strong> 16-bit VAs: the top 2 bits pick the segment and the low 14 bits are the offset (max segment size 16 KB). The stack grows downward.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Segment</th>\n<th>Bits</th>\n<th>Base</th>\n<th>Size</th>\n<th>Grows positive?</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Code</td>\n<td>00</td>\n<td>32 KB</td>\n<td>2 KB</td>\n<td>Yes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Heap</td>\n<td>01</td>\n<td>34 KB</td>\n<td>3 KB</td>\n<td>Yes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Stack</td>\n<td>11</td>\n<td>28 KB</td>\n<td>2 KB</td>\n<td>No</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>unused</td>\n<td>10</td>\n<td>–</td>\n<td>–</td>\n<td>–</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>(a) Translate 0x0100, 0x4200, 0x4C00, 0xFC00, 0xF000 and 0x8000. [7] (b) How does the hardware compute a stack address? [3]</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(a) Method.</strong> Write the VA in binary: the top 2 bits give the segment, the low 14 bits give the offset. Then, for a forward-growing segment, check offset &lt; size and compute PA = base + offset. For the stack (grows backwards): <strong>negative offset = offset − 16384</strong> (the maximum segment size), check that its magnitude is at most the size, then PA = base + negative offset.</p>\n<p>Base values in bytes: code 32768, heap 34816, stack 28672. Sizes: code 2048, heap 3072, stack 2048.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>VA</th>\n<th>Segment (top 2 bits)</th>\n<th>Offset (low 14 bits)</th>\n<th>Check</th>\n<th>PA</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>0x0100</td>\n<td>00 = code</td>\n<td>0x0100 = 256</td>\n<td>256 &lt; 2048, ok</td>\n<td>32768 + 256 = <strong>33024</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>0x4200</td>\n<td>01 = heap</td>\n<td>0x0200 = 512</td>\n<td>512 &lt; 3072, ok</td>\n<td>34816 + 512 = <strong>35328</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>0x4C00</td>\n<td>01 = heap</td>\n<td>0x0C00 = 3072</td>\n<td>3072 &lt; 3072? <strong>no</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Fault</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>0xFC00</td>\n<td>11 = stack</td>\n<td>0x3C00 = 15360</td>\n<td>15360 − 16384 = −1024, and 1024 ≤ 2048, ok</td>\n<td>28672 − 1024 = <strong>27648</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>0xF000</td>\n<td>11 = stack</td>\n<td>0x3000 = 12288</td>\n<td>12288 − 16384 = −4096, and 4096 &gt; 2048</td>\n<td><strong>Fault</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>0x8000</td>\n<td>10 = unused</td>\n<td>0</td>\n<td>segment 10 is not mapped</td>\n<td><strong>Fault</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><em>Watch out:</em> 0x4C00 is exactly one byte past the end of the heap (size 3072 means valid offsets 0 to 3071). 0xFC00 is the standard stack example: the offset must be converted to a <strong>negative</strong> one before adding to the base.</p>\n<p><strong>(b)</strong> A stack segment grows toward lower addresses, so offsets are measured <strong>backwards from the top</strong> of the virtual segment. The hardware knows the direction from a <strong>&quot;grows positive&quot; bit</strong> in the segment register. Steps: (1) extract the segment bits and the offset; (2) subtract the maximum segment size (16 KB) from the offset to get a <strong>negative offset</strong>; (3) check that the magnitude of the negative offset does not exceed the segment size; (4) add the (negative) offset to the base: <strong>PA = base + (offset − 16 KB)</strong>. Example: offset 15360 gives −1024, and 28 KB − 1 KB = 27 KB = 27648.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/endsem/endsem-practice-the-professor-s-sheet#p-e4","u":"osn/endsem/endsem-practice-the-professor-s-sheet.html#p-e4","l":"E4","s":"sheet Q4","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"endsem","c":"Endsem practice: the professor's sheet","q":"<p id=\"p-e4\"><strong>E4 (sheet Q4).</strong> (a) Explain external fragmentation with a small example, and why compaction is expensive. [6] (b) How can several processes share a code segment safely? [4]</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(a) External fragmentation</strong> is free memory that is split into <strong>small holes between allocated segments</strong>, so that a request fails even though the <em>total</em> free space is large enough. Segments have different sizes, so allocating and freeing them leaves holes of different sizes.</p>\n<p><em>Example:</em> three 4 KB segments A, B and C sit one after another (12 KB). A and C exit, leaving two 4 KB holes with B between them. Total free memory = 8 KB, but a new <strong>6 KB</strong> segment <strong>cannot be placed</strong>, because neither hole is big enough.</p>\n<p><strong>Compaction</strong> rearranges segments to make one large hole. It is <strong>expensive</strong> because the OS must: (1) <strong>stop</strong> the affected processes; (2) <strong>copy</strong> every moved segment (memory-intensive, so cost proportional to the amount of memory moved); (3) <strong>update</strong> the base registers in each PCB. All of that is done while the processes are paused. A growing segment can also make the work wasted soon after.</p>\n<p><strong>(b) Sharing</strong> works through <strong>protection bits</strong> kept for each segment. The code segment is marked <strong>read-execute only</strong>. Several processes then <strong>map the same physical code segment</strong> (each process's segment table points to the same physical base), and each keeps its own private data, heap and stack segments. Because the segment is read-only, no process can corrupt it, and the hardware checks the protection bits on every access (a write attempt traps). This saves physical memory: one copy of the code serves all processes.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/endsem/endsem-practice-the-professor-s-sheet#p-e5","u":"osn/endsem/endsem-practice-the-professor-s-sheet.html#p-e5","l":"E5","s":"sheet Q5","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"endsem","c":"Endsem practice: the professor's sheet","q":"<p id=\"p-e5\"><strong>E5 (sheet Q5).</strong> The free list holds chunks of 10, 30, 20 and 15 bytes, in that order. Requests arrive for 12, 18 and 9 bytes. Ignore headers; a chunk is split and the remainder stays in place. (a) Show the list after each request under first fit, best fit and worst fit. [7] (b) A request for 25 then arrives. Which policies succeed, and what does that suggest? [3]</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(a) Rules.</strong> <strong>First fit</strong>: the first chunk that is big enough. <strong>Best fit</strong>: the <em>smallest</em> chunk that is big enough. <strong>Worst fit</strong>: the <em>largest</em> chunk. After the choice, the chunk shrinks by the request size and stays where it is.</p>\n<p><em>First fit</em> (list 10, 30, 20, 15):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>12 → first chunk ≥ 12 is 30 → list <strong>10, 18, 20, 15</strong></li>\n<li>18 → first chunk ≥ 18 is 18 (the second one) → list <strong>10, 20, 15</strong> (that chunk is used up)</li>\n<li>9 → first chunk ≥ 9 is 10 → list <strong>1, 20, 15</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p><em>Best fit</em> (list 10, 30, 20, 15):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>12 → chunks ≥ 12 are 30, 20, 15; the smallest is 15 → list <strong>10, 30, 20, 3</strong></li>\n<li>18 → chunks ≥ 18 are 30, 20; the smallest is 20 → list <strong>10, 30, 2, 3</strong></li>\n<li>9 → chunks ≥ 9 are 10, 30; the smallest is 10 → list <strong>1, 30, 2, 3</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p><em>Worst fit</em> (list 10, 30, 20, 15):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>12 → largest is 30 → list <strong>10, 18, 20, 15</strong></li>\n<li>18 → largest is 20 → list <strong>10, 18, 2, 15</strong></li>\n<li>9 → largest is 18 (list is 10, 18, 2, 15) → list <strong>10, 9, 2, 15</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Policy</th>\n<th>After 12</th>\n<th>After 18</th>\n<th>After 9</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>First fit</td>\n<td>10, 18, 20, 15</td>\n<td>10, 20, 15</td>\n<td>1, 20, 15</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Best fit</td>\n<td>10, 30, 20, 3</td>\n<td>10, 30, 2, 3</td>\n<td>1, 30, 2, 3</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Worst fit</td>\n<td>10, 18, 20, 15</td>\n<td>10, 18, 2, 15</td>\n<td>10, 9, 2, 15</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>(b)</strong> A request for 25 bytes: the largest chunk in each final list is 20 (first fit), <strong>30 (best fit)</strong>, 15 (worst fit). So only <strong>best fit succeeds</strong>. <em>What it suggests:</em> <strong>no policy is best for every request sequence</strong>. Best fit preserved a large chunk here by using small chunks for small requests, but it leaves tiny <strong>splinters</strong> (1, 2 and 3 bytes) that are useless, and each request needs a full list search. Worst fit tries to leave big remainders but here chopped up the largest chunks. First fit is fast but fragments the front of the list.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/endsem/endsem-practice-the-professor-s-sheet#p-e6","u":"osn/endsem/endsem-practice-the-professor-s-sheet.html#p-e6","l":"E6","s":"sheet Q6","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"endsem","c":"Endsem practice: the professor's sheet","q":"<p id=\"p-e6\"><strong>E6 (sheet Q6).</strong> A binary buddy allocator manages a 64 KB heap starting at 0. Requests arrive in order: A = 7 KB, B = 3 KB, C = 20 KB. (a) Which block does each get, and which splits are needed? [5] (b) What is the total internal fragmentation? [2] (c) A is freed, then B. Describe the coalescing. [3]</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(a) Method.</strong> Round each request up to the next power of two. To satisfy it, split a free block in half repeatedly until a block of that size exists. The two halves of a split are <strong>buddies</strong>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A = 7 KB → 8 KB block.</strong> Split 64 → 32 + 32; split the first 32 → 16 + 16; split the first 16 → 8 + 8. <strong>A = [0, 8 KB).</strong></li>\n<li><strong>B = 3 KB → 4 KB block.</strong> Take the free 8 KB at [8, 16) and split it → 4 + 4. <strong>B = [8, 12 KB).</strong></li>\n<li><strong>C = 20 KB → 32 KB block.</strong> The free 32 KB block at [32, 64) fits exactly, no split needed. <strong>C = [32, 64 KB).</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>The free blocks now are [12, 16) (4 KB) and [16, 32) (16 KB).</p>\n<p><strong>(b) Internal fragmentation</strong> = block size − request size: A: 8 − 7 = 1 KB; B: 4 − 3 = 1 KB; C: 32 − 20 = 12 KB. Total = <strong>14 KB</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>(c) Coalescing rule:</strong> when a block is freed, check its <strong>buddy</strong> (the other half of the block it was split from). If the buddy is <strong>completely free</strong>, merge the two into the next size up, and repeat upward.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Free A [0, 8):</strong> its buddy is [8, 16), which is <strong>not free as a whole</strong> (it was split, and B holds [8, 12)). So <strong>no merge</strong>; [0, 8) just becomes a free 8 KB block.</li>\n<li><strong>Free B [8, 12):</strong> its buddy is [12, 16), which is free → merge into [8, 16) (8 KB). Now [8, 16)'s buddy is [0, 8), which is free → merge into [0, 16) (16 KB). Now [0, 16)'s buddy is [16, 32), which is free → merge into [0, 32) (32 KB). Now [0, 32)'s buddy is [32, 64), which holds C → <strong>stop</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Final free list: one 32 KB block at [0, 32). Address check: a block's buddy address = block address XOR block size.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/endsem/endsem-practice-the-professor-s-sheet#p-e7","u":"osn/endsem/endsem-practice-the-professor-s-sheet.html#p-e7","l":"E7","s":"sheet Q7","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"endsem","c":"Endsem practice: the professor's sheet","q":"<p id=\"p-e7\"><strong>E7 (sheet Q7).</strong> 32-bit VAs, 4 KB pages, 4-byte PTEs and a linear page table. (a) VPN and offset bits? [2] (b) Table size per process, and for 100 processes? [3] (c) VA 0x00403A2C maps to a page whose PTE holds PFN 0x01F2. What is the PA? [3] (d) Without a TLB, how many memory references does one load need? [2]</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(a)</strong> Page size 4 KB = 2^12, so the <strong>offset = 12 bits</strong>. <strong>VPN = 32 − 12 = 20 bits</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>(b)</strong> A linear table has one entry per virtual page: 2^20 entries × 4 bytes = 2^22 bytes = <strong>4 MB per process</strong>. For 100 processes: 100 × 4 MB = <strong>400 MB</strong>, just for page tables. This is why linear page tables are too big (next topic: multi-level tables).</p>\n<p><strong>(c)</strong> Split the address 0x00403A2C: the low 12 bits (3 hex digits) are the offset: <strong>offset = 0xA2C</strong>. The rest is the VPN: 0x00403 (VPN = 0x403). The PTE for this VPN gives PFN = 0x01F2. The offset is not translated:</p>\n<div class=\"math-d\" data-tex=\"PA = (\\text{PFN} \\ll 12) \\mid \\text{offset} = 0x01F2 \\cdot 0x1000 + 0xA2C = \\textbf{0x01F2A2C}\"><span class=\"katex-display\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.1389em;\">P</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">A</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord text\"><span class=\"mord\">PFN</span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">≪</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">12</span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">∣</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.6944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord text\"><span class=\"mord\">offset</span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">0</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">x</span><span class=\"mord\">01</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.1389em;\">F</span><span class=\"mord\">2</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">⋅</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">0</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">x</span><span class=\"mord\">1000</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">0</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">x</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">A</span><span class=\"mord\">2</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0715em;\">C</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.6861em;\"></span><span class=\"mord text\"><span class=\"mord textbf\">0x01F2A2C</span></span></span></span></span></span></div>\n<p>(PFN digits followed by the 3 offset digits.)</p>\n<p><strong>(d)</strong> <strong>2 memory references</strong>: one to read the <strong>PTE</strong> from the page table in memory, and one to access the <strong>data</strong> itself. (With a TLB hit, only 1.)</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/endsem/endsem-practice-the-professor-s-sheet#p-e8","u":"osn/endsem/endsem-practice-the-professor-s-sheet.html#p-e8","l":"E8","s":"sheet Q8","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"endsem","c":"Endsem practice: the professor's sheet","q":"<p id=\"p-e8\"><strong>E8 (sheet Q8).</strong> What happens, and who handles it? (a) A load to a page with valid = 0. (b) valid = 1, present = 0. (c) A store to a read-only page. (d) How are the dirty and reference bits used in eviction? [10]</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(a) valid = 0.</strong> The page is <strong>not part of the process's address space</strong> (never allocated). The <strong>hardware</strong> (MMU) raises an exception and traps to the <strong>OS</strong>, which treats it as an <strong>illegal memory access</strong> and normally <strong>kills the process</strong> (segmentation fault).</p>\n<p><strong>(b) valid = 1, present = 0.</strong> The page is in the address space but is <strong>swapped out to disk</strong>, so the access causes a <strong>page fault</strong>, handled by the <strong>OS page-fault handler</strong> (not the hardware). Steps: (1) the PTE holds the page's <strong>disk address</strong>; (2) the OS finds a free frame (if memory is full, it picks a victim page by the replacement policy and writes it back if dirty); (3) it issues a <strong>disk read</strong> and <strong>blocks</strong> the process (another process runs meanwhile); (4) on completion it updates the PTE (<strong>present = 1</strong>, new PFN); (5) it <strong>retries the faulting instruction</strong>, which now succeeds (after a TLB refill).</p>\n<p><strong>(c) Store to a read-only page</strong> (protection bit does not allow writes). The hardware detects the violation and raises a <strong>protection fault</strong> that traps to the <strong>OS</strong>. Normally the OS <strong>terminates the process</strong>. (If the page was deliberately marked read-only for <strong>copy-on-write</strong>, as in your MP2 xv6 fork, the OS handler instead allocates a new frame, copies the page, maps it writable and retries.)</p>\n<p><strong>(d) Dirty bit.</strong> Set by the hardware when the page is <strong>written</strong>. When choosing a victim, a <strong>clean</strong> page can simply be discarded (its copy on disk is up to date), but a <strong>dirty</strong> page must first be <strong>written back</strong> to disk, which costs an extra I/O. So the OS <strong>prefers to evict clean pages</strong>. <strong>Reference (use) bit.</strong> Set by the hardware whenever the page is <strong>accessed</strong>. The OS uses it to <strong>approximate LRU</strong>: in the <strong>clock algorithm</strong> the hand sweeps over the pages; a page with use = 1 is given a second chance (the bit is cleared), and the first page found with use = 0 is evicted.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/endsem/endsem-practice-the-professor-s-sheet#p-e9","u":"osn/endsem/endsem-practice-the-professor-s-sheet.html#p-e9","l":"E9","s":"sheet Q9","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"endsem","c":"Endsem practice: the professor's sheet","q":"<p id=\"p-e9\"><strong>E9 (sheet Q9).</strong> An array of ten 4-byte ints a[0..9] starts at VA 100. Pages are 16 bytes, and the TLB starts empty. (a) Which pages hold the array? Mark each access as a hit or miss, and give the hit rate. [5] (b) The loop runs again right away. What is the hit rate, and which locality explains it? [2] (c) What would the first-loop hit rate be with 4 KB pages? [3]</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(a)</strong> Page size 16 bytes, so VPN = VA / 16. The array occupies bytes 100 to 139 (a[i] is at 100 + 4i).</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Element</th>\n<th>VA</th>\n<th>VPN (VA ÷ 16)</th>\n<th>TLB result</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>a[0]</td>\n<td>100</td>\n<td>6 (bytes 96–111)</td>\n<td><strong>Miss</strong> (first touch of page 6)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>a[1]</td>\n<td>104</td>\n<td>6</td>\n<td>Hit</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>a[2]</td>\n<td>108</td>\n<td>6</td>\n<td>Hit</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>a[3]</td>\n<td>112</td>\n<td>7 (bytes 112–127)</td>\n<td><strong>Miss</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>a[4]</td>\n<td>116</td>\n<td>7</td>\n<td>Hit</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>a[5]</td>\n<td>120</td>\n<td>7</td>\n<td>Hit</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>a[6]</td>\n<td>124</td>\n<td>7</td>\n<td>Hit</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>a[7]</td>\n<td>128</td>\n<td>8 (bytes 128–143)</td>\n<td><strong>Miss</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>a[8]</td>\n<td>132</td>\n<td>8</td>\n<td>Hit</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>a[9]</td>\n<td>136</td>\n<td>8</td>\n<td>Hit</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>The array spans <strong>virtual pages 6, 7 and 8</strong>. Pattern: M H H M H H H M H H. <strong>3 misses, 7 hits</strong>, so the hit rate = 7 / 10 = <strong>70%</strong>. The misses are compulsory (cold) misses, one per new page.</p>\n<p><strong>(b)</strong> On the second run all three translations (VPN 6, 7 and 8) are already in the TLB, so every access hits: <strong>hit rate = 100%</strong>. This is <strong>temporal locality</strong>: the same pages are re-referenced soon after.</p>\n<p><strong>(c)</strong> With 4 KB pages, bytes 100 to 139 all lie in <strong>page 0</strong>, so the first access misses and the other nine hit: hit rate = <strong>90%</strong>. The page size exploits <strong>spatial locality</strong>: neighbouring addresses lie on the same page, so a larger page means more neighbours per TLB entry and fewer misses.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/endsem/endsem-practice-the-professor-s-sheet#p-e10","u":"osn/endsem/endsem-practice-the-professor-s-sheet.html#p-e10","l":"E10","s":"sheet Q10","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"endsem","c":"Endsem practice: the professor's sheet","q":"<p id=\"p-e10\"><strong>E10 (sheet Q10).</strong> A TLB lookup takes 1 ns and a memory access 100 ns. On a miss, the hardware reads one PTE from memory. (a) Find the effective access time for hit rates of 98% and 90%. [5] (b) P1 and P2 both use VPN 10, mapped to different frames. What problem does this cause on a context switch? Give two fixes and their trade-offs. [5]</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(a) Cases.</strong> A <strong>hit</strong> costs one TLB lookup plus one memory access (the data): 1 + 100 = <strong>101 ns</strong>. A <strong>miss</strong> costs the lookup, one memory access for the PTE, then the data access: 1 + 100 + 100 = <strong>201 ns</strong>.</p>\n<div class=\"math-d\" data-tex=\"EAT = h(t + m) + (1-h)(t + 2m)\"><span class=\"katex-display\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0576em;\">E</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">A</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.1389em;\">T</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">h</span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">t</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">m</span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord\">1</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">−</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">h</span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">t</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">2</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">m</span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span></span></span></span></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li>h = 0.98: EAT = 0.98 × 101 + 0.02 × 201 = 98.98 + 4.02 = <strong>103 ns</strong>.</li>\n<li>h = 0.90: EAT = 0.90 × 101 + 0.10 × 201 = 90.9 + 20.1 = <strong>111 ns</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A drop of 8 points in hit rate costs about 8 ns (8%) per access.</p>\n<p><strong>(b) The problem.</strong> The TLB holds <strong>VPN → PFN</strong> translations but does not know which process they belong to. After a context switch from P1 to P2, the entry &quot;VPN 10 → P1's frame&quot; is still in the TLB. If P2 accesses VPN 10, it <strong>hits the stale entry and reads P1's memory</strong>: wrong data and a protection violation.</p>\n<p><strong>Fix 1: flush the TLB</strong> (invalidate all entries) on every context switch. <em>Trade-off:</em> simple and needs no extra hardware, but the next process starts with an <strong>empty (cold) TLB</strong>, so every switch is followed by a burst of misses, which hurts performance if switches are frequent.</p>\n<p><strong>Fix 2: ASIDs (address-space identifiers).</strong> Add an ASID field (like a PID) to each TLB entry, and the hardware matches both the VPN <strong>and</strong> the current process's ASID. <em>Trade-off:</em> entries from several processes can coexist, so <strong>no flush</strong> and warm TLBs after a switch. But it needs <strong>extra hardware bits</strong> in every TLB entry, and the OS must manage ASID allocation (and recycle them). It also lets two processes share a physical page through two entries with different ASIDs.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/endsem/endsem-practice-the-professor-s-sheet#p-e11","u":"osn/endsem/endsem-practice-the-professor-s-sheet.html#p-e11","l":"E11","s":"sheet Q11","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"endsem","c":"Endsem practice: the professor's sheet","q":"<p id=\"p-e11\"><strong>E11 (sheet Q11).</strong> A 16 KB address space (14-bit VAs), 64-byte pages and 4-byte PTEs. The process uses only VPNs 0, 1 (code), 4, 5 (heap), 254 and 255 (stack). (a) VPN and offset bits? How large would a linear table be? [2] (b) With one page of PTEs per directory entry, how many bits index the directory and how many index a page of the table? [2] (c) How many pages does the two-level table need? [2] (d) Split 0x3F80 into directory index, table index and offset. Its PTE holds PFN 55. What is the PA? [4]</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(a)</strong> 64-byte pages = 2^6, so <strong>offset = 6 bits</strong>; <strong>VPN = 14 − 6 = 8 bits</strong> (256 virtual pages). A linear table: 256 entries × 4 B = <strong>1024 B = 1 KB = 16 pages</strong> (1024 / 64).</p>\n<p><strong>(b)</strong> One page holds 64 / 4 = <strong>16 PTEs</strong>, so a page of the table is indexed by <strong>4 bits</strong> (2^4 = 16). The directory needs one entry per page of the table: 256 PTEs / 16 = 16 pages, so <strong>4 bits</strong> (8 − 4) index the directory. The VA splits as <strong>| 4-bit PD index | 4-bit PT index | 6-bit offset |</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>(c)</strong> Needed pages: <strong>1 page for the page directory</strong>, plus only the table pages that contain a used VPN. VPNs 0, 1, 4 and 5 all lie in table page 0 (VPNs 0–15); VPNs 254 and 255 lie in table page 15 (VPNs 240–255). That is 2 table pages. <strong>Total = 1 + 2 = 3 pages</strong> (192 bytes) instead of 16 pages. The other 14 directory entries are marked invalid and cost nothing more.</p>\n<p><strong>(d)</strong> 0x3F80 in binary (14 bits): 11 1111 1000 0000 → split <strong>1111 | 1110 | 000000</strong>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>PD index = 1111 = <strong>15</strong> → the directory entry points to table page 15.</li>\n<li>PT index = 1110 = <strong>14</strong> → entry 14 of that page, which is VPN 15 × 16 + 14 = 254.</li>\n<li>Offset = 000000 = <strong>0</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The PTE holds PFN = 55, so PA = 55 × 64 + 0 = <strong>3520</strong> (= <strong>0xDC0</strong>).</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/endsem/endsem-practice-the-professor-s-sheet#p-e12","u":"osn/endsem/endsem-practice-the-professor-s-sheet.html#p-e12","l":"E12","s":"sheet Q12","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"endsem","c":"Endsem practice: the professor's sheet","q":"<p id=\"p-e12\"><strong>E12 (sheet Q12).</strong> 48-bit VAs, 4 KB pages, 8-byte PTEs, and every level must fit in one page. (a) How many levels are needed? [5] (b) How many memory references does a load need on a TLB miss, and what is the trade-off? [5]</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(a)</strong> Offset = 12 bits (4 KB), so VPN = 48 − 12 = <strong>36 bits</strong>. One page holds 4096 / 8 = <strong>512 PTEs = 2^9</strong>, so each level can index <strong>9 bits</strong>. Levels needed = ⌈36 / 9⌉ = <strong>4 levels</strong> (9 + 9 + 9 + 9 = 36).</p>\n<div class=\"math-d\" data-tex=\"\\text{levels} = \\left\\lceil \\frac{\\text{VPN bits}}{\\log_2(\\text{page size} / \\text{PTE size})} \\right\\rceil = \\left\\lceil \\frac{36}{9} \\right\\rceil = 4\"><span class=\"katex-display\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.6944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord text\"><span class=\"mord\">levels</span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:2.4em;vertical-align:-0.95em;\"></span><span class=\"minner\"><span class=\"mopen delimcenter\" style=\"top:0em;\"><span class=\"delimsizing size3\">⌈</span></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mopen nulldelimiter\"></span><span class=\"mfrac\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:1.3714em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.314em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mop\"><span class=\"mop\">lo<span style=\"margin-right:0.0139em;\">g</span></span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.207em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.4559em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"katex-sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">2</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.2441em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord text\"><span class=\"mord\">page size</span></span><span class=\"mord\">/</span><span class=\"mord text\"><span class=\"mord\">PTE size</span></span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.23em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"frac-line\" style=\"border-bottom-width:0.04em;\"></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.677em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord text\"><span class=\"mord\">VPN bits</span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.936em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mclose nulldelimiter\"></span></span><span class=\"mclose delimcenter\" style=\"top:0em;\"><span class=\"delimsizing size3\">⌉</span></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:2.4em;vertical-align:-0.95em;\"></span><span class=\"minner\"><span class=\"mopen delimcenter\" style=\"top:0em;\"><span class=\"delimsizing size3\">⌈</span></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mopen nulldelimiter\"></span><span class=\"mfrac\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:1.3214em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.314em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord\">9</span></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.23em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"frac-line\" style=\"border-bottom-width:0.04em;\"></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.677em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord\">36</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.686em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mclose nulldelimiter\"></span></span><span class=\"mclose delimcenter\" style=\"top:0em;\"><span class=\"delimsizing size3\">⌉</span></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">4</span></span></span></span></span></div>\n<p><strong>(b)</strong> On a TLB miss the hardware reads <strong>one entry per level</strong> (4 memory references to walk the table) and then the data itself: <strong>4 + 1 = 5 memory references</strong>. (On a TLB hit: 1.)</p>\n<p><strong>Trade-off:</strong> multi-level tables trade <strong>time for space</strong>. <em>Space saved:</em> page-table pages are allocated only for the parts of the address space actually in use, so a sparse address space needs very little memory (unlike a linear table of 2^36 × 8 B = 512 GB). <em>Time and complexity cost:</em> a TLB miss costs several memory references instead of one extra, and the lookup logic is more complex. This is acceptable because the TLB makes misses rare.</p>\n<p><em>Variant to expect:</em> RISC-V <strong>Sv39</strong> (the xv6 system you used): 39-bit VA, 4 KB pages, 8-byte PTEs → 27-bit VPN = 3 levels × 9 bits.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/endsem/endsem-practice-the-professor-s-sheet#p-e13","u":"osn/endsem/endsem-practice-the-professor-s-sheet.html#p-e13","l":"E13","s":"sheet Q13","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"endsem","c":"Endsem practice: the professor's sheet","q":"<p id=\"p-e13\"><strong>E13 (sheet Q13).</strong> <code>lock: while (m-&gt;flag == 1) ; m-&gt;flag = 1;</code> and <code>unlock: m-&gt;flag = 0;</code> (a) Give an interleaving with two threads in the critical section. [3] (b) Rewrite lock() with TestAndSet. [3] (c) Evaluate the spin lock for correctness, fairness and single-CPU performance. [4]</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(a)</strong> The bug is that <strong>testing the flag and setting it are two separate steps</strong>. Interleaving (flag = 0 initially):</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Step</th>\n<th>T1</th>\n<th>T2</th>\n<th>flag</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td><code>while (flag == 1)</code>: sees 0, exits the loop</td>\n<td></td>\n<td>0</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td><em>timer interrupt: switch to T2</em></td>\n<td></td>\n<td>0</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td></td>\n<td><code>while (flag == 1)</code>: sees 0, exits</td>\n<td>0</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td></td>\n<td><code>flag = 1</code>; <strong>enters the critical section</strong></td>\n<td>1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td><em>switch back to T1</em></td>\n<td></td>\n<td>1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6</td>\n<td><code>flag = 1</code>; <strong>enters the critical section</strong></td>\n<td></td>\n<td>1</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Both threads are now in the critical section</strong>: mutual exclusion is violated.</p>\n<p><strong>(b)</strong> <code>TestAndSet(ptr, new)</code> atomically returns the <strong>old</strong> value and stores the <strong>new</strong> one, so the test and the set cannot be separated:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-c\">void lock(lock_t *m) {\n    while (TestAndSet(&amp;m-&gt;flag, 1) == 1)\n        ;   // spin: old value 1 means someone else holds the lock\n}\nvoid unlock(lock_t *m) { m-&gt;flag = 0; }\n</code></pre>\n<p>If the old value was 0, the lock was free and this thread has just taken it (it left flag = 1); only one thread can ever see the old value 0.</p>\n<p><strong>(c)</strong> <strong>Correctness: yes.</strong> The atomic test-and-set guarantees mutual exclusion. <strong>Fairness: no.</strong> There is no queue and no ordering, so a thread can spin forever while others keep winning (starvation). <strong>Performance on a single CPU: very poor.</strong> If the lock holder is preempted inside the critical section, every other thread runs and <strong>spins for its whole time slice</strong> without being able to get the lock (the holder cannot release it until it runs again). With N threads, up to N − 1 slices are wasted. (On several CPUs, spinning is reasonable when critical sections are short, because the holder runs on another CPU.)</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/endsem/endsem-practice-the-professor-s-sheet#p-e14","u":"osn/endsem/endsem-practice-the-professor-s-sheet.html#p-e14","l":"E14","s":"sheet Q14","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"endsem","c":"Endsem practice: the professor's sheet","q":"<p id=\"p-e14\"><strong>E14 (sheet Q14).</strong> A ticket lock: <code>myturn = FetchAndAdd(&amp;ticket); while (turn != myturn) ;</code> and <code>unlock: turn++</code>, with ticket = turn = 0. (a) T1, T2 and T3 call lock in that order. Give each myturn and the order they enter. [3] (b) T1 calls unlock twice by mistake. What happens? [4] (c) Why is this fair when test-and-set is not? [3]</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(a)</strong> <code>FetchAndAdd</code> returns the old value of ticket and increments it atomically: <strong>T1: myturn = 0</strong> (ticket becomes 1), <strong>T2: myturn = 1</strong> (ticket becomes 2), <strong>T3: myturn = 2</strong> (ticket becomes 3). turn = 0, so only T1 passes the <code>while</code>. <strong>Entry order: T1, T2, T3.</strong> (T1's unlock makes turn = 1, letting T2 in; T2's unlock lets T3 in.)</p>\n<p><strong>(b)</strong> Double unlock by T1:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>First <code>unlock</code>: turn = 1, so <strong>T2</strong> enters (correct).</li>\n<li>Second <code>unlock</code> (by T1, mistaken): turn = 2, so <strong>T3</strong> now sees turn == myturn and <strong>enters while T2 is still inside</strong>. Two threads are in the critical section: <strong>mutual exclusion is broken</strong>.</li>\n<li>Worse, turn is now permanently one ahead of the &quot;correct&quot; count, so later threads keep overlapping. The ticket lock relies on <strong>exactly one unlock per lock</strong>; an extra unlock destroys its invariant.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>(c)</strong> In a ticket lock each thread takes a <strong>ticket number</strong> and is served in <strong>ticket order (FIFO)</strong>: a thread that asked earlier always goes before one that asked later, so every thread gets in after a bounded number of others (<strong>bounded waiting, no starvation</strong>). With a test-and-set spin lock, when the lock is released <strong>whichever thread happens to be running and executes its TestAndSet first wins</strong>, so an unlucky thread can lose every time (starvation).</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/endsem/endsem-practice-the-professor-s-sheet#p-e15","u":"osn/endsem/endsem-practice-the-professor-s-sheet.html#p-e15","l":"E15","s":"sheet Q15","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"endsem","c":"Endsem practice: the professor's sheet","q":"<p id=\"p-e15\"><strong>E15 (sheet Q15).</strong> Two broken joins. Version 1 (no state variable): exit = lock, signal, unlock; join = lock, wait, unlock. Version 2 (no lock): exit = <code>done = 1; signal</code>; join = <code>if (done == 0) wait</code>. (a) and (b) Give an interleaving in which each version makes the parent wait forever. [3 + 3] (c) Write the correct version. [4]</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(a) Version 1 (no state variable).</strong></p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Step</th>\n<th>Parent</th>\n<th>Child</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>creates the child</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td></td>\n<td>runs first: <code>thr_exit()</code>: lock, <code>signal(&amp;c)</code>, unlock</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td><code>thr_join()</code>: lock, <code>wait(&amp;c)</code></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>The child's <strong>signal found nobody waiting and was lost</strong> (a condition variable has no memory). The parent then waits <strong>forever</strong> for a signal that will never come. A state variable (<code>done</code>) is needed so the parent can see that the child has already finished.</p>\n<p><strong>(b) Version 2 (no lock).</strong></p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Step</th>\n<th>Parent</th>\n<th>Child</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td><code>thr_join()</code>: checks <code>done == 0</code>, true</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td><em>interrupt before it calls wait</em></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td></td>\n<td><code>thr_exit()</code>: <code>done = 1; signal(&amp;c)</code> (nobody is waiting yet: lost)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>calls <code>wait(&amp;c)</code></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>The parent checked the condition, was interrupted <strong>before it went to sleep</strong>, and so missed the signal: it sleeps <strong>forever</strong>. The check and the wait must be one atomic step, which is what holding the <strong>lock</strong> (released atomically by <code>wait</code>) provides.</p>\n<p><strong>(c) Correct version:</strong></p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-c\">int done = 0;\n\nvoid thr_exit() {\n    lock(&amp;m);\n    done = 1;          // record the state\n    signal(&amp;c);        // wake the parent if it is waiting\n    unlock(&amp;m);\n}\n\nvoid thr_join() {\n    lock(&amp;m);\n    while (done == 0)  // while, not if\n        wait(&amp;c, &amp;m);  // atomically releases m and sleeps; re-acquires m on wake-up\n    unlock(&amp;m);\n}\n</code></pre>\n<p><em>Rules to state:</em> (1) always keep a <strong>state variable</strong> that records the condition; (2) <strong>hold the lock</strong> when calling wait and signal; (3) always test the condition in a <strong>while</strong> loop.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/endsem/endsem-practice-the-professor-s-sheet#p-e16","u":"osn/endsem/endsem-practice-the-professor-s-sheet.html#p-e16","l":"E16","s":"sheet Q16","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"endsem","c":"Endsem practice: the professor's sheet","q":"<p id=\"p-e16\"><strong>E16 (sheet Q16).</strong> allocate(n) waits on one CV while bytesLeft &lt; n. free(n) adds n and calls signal. bytesLeft starts at 0. (a) Ta calls allocate(100) and Tb calls allocate(10), and both wait. Tc calls free(50). Show how a thread can stay asleep even though memory is available. [4] (b) Give the fix and its cost. [3] (c) Why must allocate use a while loop? [3]</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(a)</strong> Trace (one CV, one <code>signal</code>):</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Step</th>\n<th>Action</th>\n<th>bytesLeft</th>\n<th>Waiting on the CV</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>Ta: allocate(100): 0 &lt; 100, waits</td>\n<td>0</td>\n<td>Ta</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>Tb: allocate(10): 0 &lt; 10, waits</td>\n<td>0</td>\n<td>Ta, Tb</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Tc: free(50): bytesLeft = 50, <strong>signal wakes one waiter</strong>, suppose Ta</td>\n<td>50</td>\n<td>Tb</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>Ta rechecks: 50 &lt; 100, <strong>waits again</strong></td>\n<td>50</td>\n<td>Tb, Ta</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Tb needs only 10 bytes and 50 are free, but Tb is never woken</strong>, because the single signal woke the wrong thread (Ta) and was &quot;used up&quot;. Tb stays asleep although memory is available.</p>\n<p><strong>(b) Fix: <code>broadcast</code></strong> (wake <strong>all</strong> waiters) in free(). Each woken thread rechecks its own condition (Tb sees 50 ≥ 10 and proceeds; Ta sees 50 &lt; 100 and sleeps again). This is a <strong>covering condition</strong>: it covers all cases where a thread might be able to proceed. <strong>Cost:</strong> threads that cannot proceed are also woken, recheck and go back to sleep, which wastes <strong>context switches</strong>, a bigger cost as the number of waiters grows.</p>\n<p><strong>(c)</strong> With <strong>Mesa semantics</strong> (used by pthreads and almost every real system), <code>signal</code> only makes the waiter <strong>ready</strong>, it does not run it immediately. Between the signal and the moment the woken thread re-acquires the lock, <strong>another thread can run and change the state</strong> (for example, take the memory), so the condition may no longer hold. Also, <strong>spurious wake-ups</strong> can happen. A <code>while</code> loop makes the thread <strong>recheck the condition after every wake-up</strong> and sleep again if it is false, so it only proceeds when the condition really is true. <code>if</code> would proceed on a stale assumption.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/endsem/endsem-practice-the-professor-s-sheet#p-e17","u":"osn/endsem/endsem-practice-the-professor-s-sheet.html#p-e17","l":"E17","s":"sheet Q17","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"endsem","c":"Endsem practice: the professor's sheet","q":"<p id=\"p-e17\"><strong>E17 (sheet Q17).</strong> Thread A prints A1 then A2. Thread B prints B1 then B2. (a) Using semaphores, ensure A1 comes before B2 and B1 comes before A2. Give initial values. [6] (b) What goes wrong if both threads call sem_wait before sem_post? [4]</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(a)</strong> This is the <strong>rendezvous</strong> problem: each thread must finish its first step before the <em>other</em> thread starts its second step. Use <strong>two semaphores, both initialised to 0</strong> (an ordering semaphore starts at 0 because the event has not happened yet):</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-c\">sem_t aDone, bDone;\nsem_init(&amp;aDone, 0, 0);\nsem_init(&amp;bDone, 0, 0);\n\n// Thread A                    // Thread B\nprint(&quot;A1&quot;);                   print(&quot;B1&quot;);\nsem_post(&amp;aDone);              sem_post(&amp;bDone);   // signal: my first step is done\nsem_wait(&amp;bDone);              sem_wait(&amp;aDone);   // wait for the other's first step\nprint(&quot;A2&quot;);                   print(&quot;B2&quot;);\n</code></pre>\n<p><em>Why it works:</em> A2 cannot print until B has posted <code>bDone</code>, which happens after B1, so B1 &lt; A2. Symmetrically, A1 &lt; B2. Each thread posts <strong>before</strong> it waits, so neither can block forever.</p>\n<p><strong>(b) Deadlock.</strong> If both call <code>sem_wait</code> first, A waits for <code>bDone</code> (which only B's post can supply) and B waits for <code>aDone</code> (which only A's post can supply). Both are blocked in <code>sem_wait</code> and neither reaches its <code>sem_post</code>: a <strong>circular wait</strong>, so both sleep <strong>forever</strong>. The order post-then-wait is what makes the rendezvous safe.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/endsem/endsem-practice-the-professor-s-sheet#p-e18","u":"osn/endsem/endsem-practice-the-professor-s-sheet.html#p-e18","l":"E18","s":"sheet Q18","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"endsem","c":"Endsem practice: the professor's sheet","q":"<p id=\"p-e18\"><strong>E18 (sheet Q18).</strong> The semaphore reader-writer lock, where the first reader waits on writelock and the last reader posts it. (a) What are the initial values of lock and writelock? Why do only the first and last readers touch writelock? [4] (b) Give a scenario in which a writer waits forever. [3] (c) Suggest a fix. [3]</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(a)</strong> Both are <strong>1</strong>: <code>lock</code> is a binary semaphore (a mutex) that protects the shared <code>readers</code> counter, and <code>writelock</code> is a binary semaphore that gives a writer exclusive access (and that readers collectively hold). <strong>Why only the first and last readers touch <code>writelock</code>:</strong> many readers may read at the same time, so they need to block writers only once, as a group. The <strong>first reader</strong> (readers goes 0 → 1) acquires <code>writelock</code> on behalf of all readers, so any writer blocks. Readers that arrive later only increment the counter. The <strong>last reader</strong> to leave (readers goes 1 → 0) releases <code>writelock</code>, allowing a writer in. All the counter changes are protected by <code>lock</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>(b) Writer starvation.</strong> R1 holds the read lock (and so <code>writelock</code>). Writer W calls <code>acquire_write</code> and blocks on <code>writelock</code>. R2 arrives before R1 finishes, takes <code>lock</code>, sees readers = 2 (not the first reader), and reads. Then R3 arrives before R1 or R2 finishes, and so on. As long as there is always at least one reader inside, <code>readers</code> never reaches 0, so the last-reader release never happens and <strong>W waits forever</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>(c) Fix: give waiting writers priority.</strong> Add a semaphore <code>turnstile</code> (initial value 1). A <strong>writer</strong> does <code>sem_wait(&amp;turnstile)</code> before waiting on <code>writelock</code> (holding the turnstile while it waits) and <code>sem_post(&amp;turnstile)</code> after it releases. Every <strong>reader</strong> does <code>sem_wait(&amp;turnstile); sem_post(&amp;turnstile);</code> at the start of <code>acquire_read</code>, so once a writer is waiting, <strong>new readers queue behind it</strong> at the turnstile. Existing readers finish, <code>readers</code> reaches 0, the writer gets <code>writelock</code>. (Trade-off: this can now starve readers if writers keep arriving; fairness needs a more careful design. Your Homework 04 is this problem.)</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/endsem/endsem-practice-the-professor-s-sheet#p-e19","u":"osn/endsem/endsem-practice-the-professor-s-sheet.html#p-e19","l":"E19","s":"sheet Q19","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"endsem","c":"Endsem practice: the professor's sheet","q":"<p id=\"p-e19\"><strong>E19 (sheet Q19).</strong> VSFS: 4 KB blocks and 256-byte inodes. Block 0 is the superblock, block 1 the inode bitmap, block 2 the data bitmap, and the inode table starts at block 3. (a) In which block, and at what byte address, is inode 37? [4] (b) An inode has 12 direct pointers, 1 single-indirect and 1 double-indirect pointer, and pointers are 4 bytes. What is the maximum file size? [4] (c) Why is this design a good fit for real files? [2]</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(a) Formula.</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-d\" data-tex=\"\\text{offset in table} = i \\times \\text{sizeof(inode)} \\qquad \\text{address} = \\text{inodeStartAddr} + \\text{offset}\"><span class=\"katex-display\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.6944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord text\"><span class=\"mord\">offset in table</span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.7429em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">i</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">×</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord text\"><span class=\"mord\">sizeof(inode)</span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:2em;\"></span><span class=\"mord text\"><span class=\"mord\">address</span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.7778em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord text\"><span class=\"mord\">inodeStartAddr</span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.6944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord text\"><span class=\"mord\">offset</span></span></span></span></span></span></div>\n<p>The inode table starts at block 3, so inodeStartAddr = 3 × 4096 = <strong>12288</strong> (12 KB).</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Offset in the table = 37 × 256 = <strong>9472</strong> bytes.</li>\n<li>Block within the table = 9472 / 4096 = 2 (remainder 1280), so it is the <strong>third block of the table: disk block 3 + 2 = block 5</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Byte address = 12288 + 9472 = 21760.</strong> Check: block 5 starts at 5 × 4096 = 20480, and 21760 − 20480 = <strong>1280</strong> bytes into the block (= (37 mod 16) × 256, since 16 inodes fit per block).</li>\n<li><strong>Sector:</strong> the slide formula gives the sector of the inode's <em>block</em>: 5 × 4096 = 20480 = 20 KB, so sector 20480 / 512 = <strong>40</strong>, with the inode at offset 1280 within the block. (The inode's own first byte, 21760, lies in sector 42, so a disk read fetches sectors 40 to 47 and the inode starts 1280 bytes in.)</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>(b)</strong> A 4 KB block holds 4096 / 4 = <strong>1024 pointers</strong>. Reachable data blocks:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>12 direct pointers → 12 blocks = 48 KB</li>\n<li>1 single-indirect → 1024 blocks = 4 MB</li>\n<li>1 double-indirect → 1024 × 1024 = 1,048,576 blocks = 4 GB</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"math-d\" data-tex=\"\\text{max size} = (12 + 1024 + 1024^2) \\times 4\\text{ KB} = 1{,}048{,}612 \\times 4096 = \\textbf{4{,}299{,}210{,}752 bytes} \\approx 4\\text{ GB}\"><span class=\"katex-display\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.6679em;\"></span><span class=\"mord text\"><span class=\"mord\">max size</span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord\">12</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">1024</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:1.1141em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">102</span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord\">4</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.8641em;\"><span style=\"top:-3.113em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"katex-sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">2</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">×</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">4</span><span class=\"mord text\"><span class=\"mord\"> KB</span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.8389em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">1</span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mpunct\">,</span></span><span class=\"mord\">048</span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mpunct\">,</span></span><span class=\"mord\">612</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">×</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">4096</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.8889em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord text\"><span class=\"mord textbf\">4</span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord textbf\">,</span></span><span class=\"mord textbf\">299</span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord textbf\">,</span></span><span class=\"mord textbf\">210</span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord textbf\">,</span></span><span class=\"mord textbf\">752 bytes</span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">≈</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">4</span><span class=\"mord text\"><span class=\"mord\"> GB</span></span></span></span></span></span></div>\n<p><strong>(c)</strong> <strong>Most files are small</strong> (the file-size distribution is heavily skewed to small files), so the 12 direct pointers cover them with <strong>no extra disk reads</strong> and small inodes. The <strong>few very large files</strong> are reachable through indirect blocks, paying one or two extra reads. So the design is <strong>space-efficient and fast for the common case</strong> while still supporting huge files. This unbalanced tree is intentional.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/endsem/endsem-practice-the-professor-s-sheet#p-e20","u":"osn/endsem/endsem-practice-the-professor-s-sheet.html#p-e20","l":"E20","s":"sheet Q20","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"endsem","c":"Endsem practice: the professor's sheet","q":"<p id=\"p-e20\"><strong>E20 (sheet Q20).</strong> Nothing is cached, and each directory fits in one block. List the disk I/Os for (a) <code>open(&quot;/foo/bar&quot;, O_RDONLY)</code> [3], (b) the first 4 KB <code>read()</code> [3], and (c) creating <code>/foo/bar</code> [4].</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(a) open(&quot;/foo/bar&quot;): 5 reads.</strong> Path traversal starts at the <strong>root inode</strong>, whose location is known (inode 2 in vsfs). For each directory in the path, read its inode, then its data block (to find the next name). Finally read the target's inode.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Read the <strong>root inode</strong>.</li>\n<li>Read the <strong>root directory's data block</strong> (find &quot;foo&quot; → its inode number).</li>\n<li>Read <strong>foo's inode</strong>.</li>\n<li>Read <strong>foo's data block</strong> (find &quot;bar&quot; → its inode number).</li>\n<li>Read <strong>bar's inode</strong> (check permissions, allocate the file-table entry).</li>\n</ol>\n<p>General rule: <strong>2 × (depth) + 1 reads</strong> for a path of depth (number of directories) 2 → 5. No writes. The cost grows with the length of the pathname.</p>\n<p><strong>(b) First read() of 4 KB: 2 reads + 1 write = 3 I/Os.</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Read bar's inode</strong> (find the first data block's pointer).</li>\n<li><strong>Read the data block.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Write bar's inode</strong> (to update the last-accessed time, atime).</li>\n</ol>\n<p>No bitmap is read or written: a read does not allocate anything.</p>\n<p><strong>(c) Create /foo/bar: 10 I/Os (6 reads + 4 writes).</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Read the root inode.</li>\n<li>Read the root data (find &quot;foo&quot;).</li>\n<li>Read foo's inode.</li>\n<li>Read foo's data (check that &quot;bar&quot; does not already exist).</li>\n<li>Read the <strong>inode bitmap</strong> (find a free inode).</li>\n<li>Write the <strong>inode bitmap</strong> (mark it allocated).</li>\n<li>Write <strong>foo's data block</strong> (add the entry (bar, new inode number)).</li>\n<li>Read the <strong>new inode</strong> (it shares a 4 KB block with other inodes, so the block must be read before part of it is modified).</li>\n<li>Write the <strong>new inode</strong> (initialise it).</li>\n<li>Write <strong>foo's inode</strong> (its modification time changes, and its size if the directory grew).</li>\n</ol>\n<p><em>Likely follow-up from the slides:</em> each <strong>allocating write</strong> takes <strong>5 I/Os</strong> (read the inode, read the data bitmap, write the data bitmap, write the data block, write the inode). Creating a 12 KB file therefore takes 10 + 3 × 5 = <strong>25 I/Os</strong>.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/endsem/endsem-practice-post-midsem-extras#p-x1","u":"osn/endsem/endsem-practice-post-midsem-extras.html#p-x1","l":"X1","s":"","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"endsem","c":"Endsem practice: post-midsem extras","q":"<p id=\"p-x1\"><strong>X1.</strong> Page replacement trace (L13 s48-52). Reference string 1, 2, 3, 4, 1, 2, 5, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5. (a) Count the page faults for FIFO with 3 frames and with 4 frames. [4] (b) Count the faults for LRU with 3 and 4 frames, and for OPT with 3 frames. [4] (c) What is Belady's anomaly, and why can LRU never show it? [2]</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Method.</strong> For each reference, check whether the page is in memory (hit) or not (miss, a page fault). On a miss with full frames, evict by the policy: FIFO evicts the page that has been in memory longest; LRU evicts the page whose last use is oldest; OPT evicts the page whose next use is furthest in the future (or never).</p>\n<p><strong>(a) FIFO.</strong> Frames shown oldest first.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Ref</th>\n<th>1</th>\n<th>2</th>\n<th>3</th>\n<th>4</th>\n<th>1</th>\n<th>2</th>\n<th>5</th>\n<th>1</th>\n<th>2</th>\n<th>3</th>\n<th>4</th>\n<th>5</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>3 frames</td>\n<td>M [1]</td>\n<td>M [1 2]</td>\n<td>M [1 2 3]</td>\n<td>M [2 3 4]</td>\n<td>M [3 4 1]</td>\n<td>M [4 1 2]</td>\n<td>M [1 2 5]</td>\n<td>H</td>\n<td>H</td>\n<td>M [2 5 3]</td>\n<td>M [5 3 4]</td>\n<td>H</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4 frames</td>\n<td>M</td>\n<td>M</td>\n<td>M</td>\n<td>M [1 2 3 4]</td>\n<td>H</td>\n<td>H</td>\n<td>M [2 3 4 5]</td>\n<td>M [3 4 5 1]</td>\n<td>M [4 5 1 2]</td>\n<td>M [5 1 2 3]</td>\n<td>M [1 2 3 4]</td>\n<td>M [2 3 4 5]</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>FIFO with 3 frames: <strong>9 faults</strong>. FIFO with 4 frames: <strong>10 faults</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>(b) LRU</strong> (evict the least recently used).</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>3 frames:</strong> 1, 2, 3, 4 (evict 1), 1 (evict 2), 2 (evict 3), 5 (evict 4) are 7 misses; then 1 and 2 hit; 3 (evict 5), 4 (evict 1), 5 (evict 2) are 3 more misses. Total <strong>10 faults</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>4 frames:</strong> 1, 2, 3, 4 miss; 1 and 2 hit; 5 (evict 3) misses; 1 and 2 hit; 3 (evict 4), 4 (evict 5), 5 (evict 1) miss. Total <strong>8 faults</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>OPT, 3 frames:</strong> 1, 2, 3 miss; 4 misses (evict 3, used furthest ahead); 1 and 2 hit; 5 misses (evict 4); 1 and 2 hit; 3 misses (evict 1, which is never used again); 4 misses (evict 2); 5 hits. Total <strong>7 faults</strong>, the minimum possible.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Policy</th>\n<th>3 frames</th>\n<th>4 frames</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>FIFO</td>\n<td>9</td>\n<td><strong>10</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>LRU</td>\n<td>10</td>\n<td>8</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>OPT</td>\n<td>7</td>\n<td>–</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>(c) Belady's anomaly:</strong> giving a process <strong>more frames</strong> produces <strong>more</strong> page faults, as FIFO does here (9 → 10). It happens because FIFO ignores how recently a page was used. LRU and OPT have the <strong>stack property</strong>: the set of pages in memory with N frames is always a <strong>subset</strong> of the set with N + 1 frames, so adding a frame can only turn misses into hits, never the reverse.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/endsem/endsem-practice-post-midsem-extras#p-x2","u":"osn/endsem/endsem-practice-post-midsem-extras.html#p-x2","l":"X2","s":"","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"endsem","c":"Endsem practice: post-midsem extras","q":"<p id=\"p-x2\"><strong>X2.</strong> AMAT and the page-fault path (L13 s33-47). T_M = 100 ns and T_D = 10 ms. (a) Find the AMAT at a 99.9% hit rate. [3] (b) What hit rate keeps the AMAT under 200 ns? [3] (c) Describe step by step what happens on a TLB miss for a page that is valid but not present. [4]</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(a)</strong> <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">A</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.109em;\">M</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">A</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.1389em;\">T</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.8333em;vertical-align:-0.15em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.1389em;\">T</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.3283em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:-0.1389em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"katex-sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\" style=\"margin-right:0.109em;\">M</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.8333em;vertical-align:-0.15em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.1389em;\">P</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.3117em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:-0.1389em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"katex-sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\">mi</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\">ss</span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">⋅</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.8333em;vertical-align:-0.15em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.1389em;\">T</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.3283em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:-0.1389em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"katex-sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\" style=\"margin-right:0.0278em;\">D</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span>. With P_miss = 0.001 and T_D = 10 ms = 10,000,000 ns: AMAT = 100 + 0.001 × 10,000,000 = 100 + 10,000 = 10,100 ns = <strong>10.1 µs</strong>. (At 90% hits: 100 + 0.1 × 10^7 = about 1 ms.)</p>\n<p><strong>(b)</strong> Need 100 + P × 10^7 &lt; 200, so P &lt; 100 / 10^7 = 10^−5. The hit rate must be <strong>above 99.999%</strong>. The disk is about 100,000 times slower than memory, so even a tiny miss rate <strong>dominates</strong> the average.</p>\n<p><strong>(c) Steps:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>The TLB lookup <strong>misses</strong>, so the hardware walks the page table and reads the PTE.</li>\n<li>The PTE has <strong>valid = 1, present = 0</strong>, so the hardware raises a <strong>page fault</strong> (trap to the OS).</li>\n<li>The <strong>OS page-fault handler</strong> finds the page's disk address (stored in the PTE).</li>\n<li>It finds a <strong>free frame</strong>. If none is free, the replacement policy picks a <strong>victim</strong>, and if the victim is dirty it is written to disk first.</li>\n<li>The OS issues the <strong>disk read</strong> and <strong>blocks</strong> the process; the scheduler runs another process while the I/O completes.</li>\n<li>When the I/O finishes, the OS sets <strong>present = 1</strong> and the <strong>PFN</strong> in the PTE.</li>\n<li>The faulting instruction is <strong>retried</strong>. It misses in the TLB again, the hardware now finds a present PTE, installs it in the TLB, and the next retry <strong>hits</strong>.</li>\n</ol>\n"},{"id":"osn/endsem/endsem-practice-post-midsem-extras#p-x3","u":"osn/endsem/endsem-practice-post-midsem-extras.html#p-x3","l":"X3","s":"","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"endsem","c":"Endsem practice: post-midsem extras","q":"<p id=\"p-x3\"><strong>X3.</strong> Producer/consumer with condition variables (L16 s14-24). One producer and two consumers share a one-slot buffer.</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-c\">void *consumer() { lock(&amp;m); if (count == 0) wait(&amp;cv, &amp;m); get(); signal(&amp;cv); unlock(&amp;m); }\nvoid *producer() { lock(&amp;m); if (count == 1) wait(&amp;cv, &amp;m); put(); signal(&amp;cv); unlock(&amp;m); }\n</code></pre>\n<p>(a) Show how a consumer can call get() on an empty buffer. [4] (b) After changing <code>if</code> to <code>while</code>, show how all three threads can end up asleep. [4] (c) Give the final fix. [2]</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(a) Problem with <code>if</code> (Mesa semantics).</strong> Tc1 and Tc2 are consumers, P is the producer.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Step</th>\n<th>Event</th>\n<th>count</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>Tc1 runs: buffer empty, <code>wait</code> (sleeps)</td>\n<td>0</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>P runs: <code>put()</code> fills the buffer, <code>signal</code> wakes Tc1 (now <strong>ready</strong>, not running)</td>\n<td>1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td><strong>Tc2 runs first</strong>: count is 1, so it skips the wait, <code>get()</code> empties the buffer</td>\n<td>0</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>Tc1 runs: it was already past its <code>if</code>, so it does <strong>not recheck</strong>, and calls <code>get()</code> on an <strong>empty buffer</strong></td>\n<td>0 (error)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>(b) Problem with one CV.</strong> With <code>while</code> instead of <code>if</code>:</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Step</th>\n<th>Event</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>Tc1 and Tc2 both run first: count = 0, both wait on the CV</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>P: <code>put()</code>, <code>signal</code> wakes one waiter (Tc1); then P loops and finds the buffer full, so it <strong>waits</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Tc1: <code>get()</code>, then <code>signal</code>: with <strong>one</strong> CV, the signal can wake <strong>Tc2</strong> (a consumer) instead of P</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>Tc2: count = 0, waits again</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>All three threads are now asleep</strong> (P waits for room, Tc1 finished and Tc2 waits for data, nothing is left to wake them). The signal woke the wrong <strong>kind</strong> of thread.</p>\n<p><strong>(c) Fix.</strong> Use <strong><code>while</code></strong> (recheck after wake-up) <strong>and two CVs</strong>: a producer waits on <code>empty</code> and signals <code>fill</code>; a consumer waits on <code>fill</code> and signals <code>empty</code>. Then a consumer's signal can only wake a producer, and vice versa.</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-c\">producer: lock; while (count == MAX) wait(&amp;empty, &amp;m); put(); signal(&amp;fill);  unlock;\nconsumer: lock; while (count == 0)   wait(&amp;fill, &amp;m);  get(); signal(&amp;empty); unlock;\n</code></pre>\n"},{"id":"osn/endsem/endsem-practice-post-midsem-extras#p-x4","u":"osn/endsem/endsem-practice-post-midsem-extras.html#p-x4","l":"X4","s":"","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"endsem","c":"Endsem practice: post-midsem extras","q":"<p id=\"p-x4\"><strong>X4.</strong> Semaphore bounded buffer (L17 s13-19). empty = MAX, full = 0, mutex = 1. A first attempt takes <code>sem_wait(&amp;mutex)</code> <strong>before</strong> <code>sem_wait(&amp;empty)</code> in the producer, and <code>sem_wait(&amp;mutex)</code> before <code>sem_wait(&amp;full)</code> in the consumer. (a) Show the deadlock. [5] (b) Fix it, and say why the fix works. [5]</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(a) Deadlock.</strong> The buffer is empty (full = 0).</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Step</th>\n<th>Event</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>The consumer runs: <code>sem_wait(&amp;mutex)</code> succeeds (mutex = 0), then <code>sem_wait(&amp;full)</code> <strong>blocks</strong> (full = 0), <strong>while still holding the mutex</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>The producer runs: <code>sem_wait(&amp;mutex)</code> <strong>blocks</strong>, because the consumer holds it</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>The consumer waits for the producer to put an item (full), and the producer waits for the consumer to release the mutex: a <strong>circular wait</strong>, so both sleep forever. The cause is <strong>hold and wait</strong>: a thread blocks on <code>full</code> or <code>empty</code> while holding the mutex.</p>\n<p><strong>(b) Fix: shrink the mutex's scope</strong> so it covers only the buffer update:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-c\">producer:                       consumer:\n  sem_wait(&amp;empty);               sem_wait(&amp;full);\n  sem_wait(&amp;mutex);               sem_wait(&amp;mutex);\n  put(i);                         tmp = get();\n  sem_post(&amp;mutex);               sem_post(&amp;mutex);\n  sem_post(&amp;full);                sem_post(&amp;empty);\n</code></pre>\n<p>A thread now waits for <strong>empty/full first, without holding the mutex</strong>, so it cannot block anyone while it sleeps. It only takes the mutex once it knows there is room (or an item), so the critical section is short and always makes progress. (The mutex is still needed when MAX &gt; 1: without it, two producers could write the same slot.)</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/endsem/endsem-practice-post-midsem-extras#p-x5","u":"osn/endsem/endsem-practice-post-midsem-extras.html#p-x5","l":"X5","s":"","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"endsem","c":"Endsem practice: post-midsem extras","q":"<p id=\"p-x5\"><strong>X5.</strong> The counter race (L14 s14-18). <code>counter = counter + 1</code> compiles to <code>mov counter, %eax; add $1, %eax; mov %eax, counter</code>. counter starts at 50, and two threads each run it once. (a) Trace an interleaving that ends with 51. [5] (b) Define critical section, race condition, indeterminate program and mutual exclusion. [5]</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(a)</strong> Each thread has its own copy of the registers (saved and restored on a switch), but both share the memory location <code>counter</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Step</th>\n<th>Thread</th>\n<th>Instruction</th>\n<th>T1's eax</th>\n<th>T2's eax</th>\n<th>counter</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>T1</td>\n<td>load</td>\n<td>50</td>\n<td></td>\n<td>50</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>T1</td>\n<td>add</td>\n<td>51</td>\n<td></td>\n<td>50</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td></td>\n<td><em>timer interrupt, switch to T2 (T1's eax = 51 is saved)</em></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td>50</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>T2</td>\n<td>load</td>\n<td></td>\n<td>50</td>\n<td>50</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>T2</td>\n<td>add</td>\n<td></td>\n<td>51</td>\n<td>50</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6</td>\n<td>T2</td>\n<td>store</td>\n<td></td>\n<td>51</td>\n<td><strong>51</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>7</td>\n<td></td>\n<td><em>switch back to T1</em></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td>51</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>8</td>\n<td>T1</td>\n<td>store</td>\n<td>51</td>\n<td></td>\n<td><strong>51</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>Both increments ran, but the final value is <strong>51, not 52</strong>: one update was <strong>lost</strong>, because T2 read the old value (50) before T1 wrote its result.</p>\n<p><strong>(b)</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Critical section:</strong> a piece of code that accesses a shared resource (here, the three instructions that update <code>counter</code>) and must not be run by more than one thread at a time.</li>\n<li><strong>Race condition:</strong> several threads enter the critical section at about the same time, and the result <strong>depends on the exact timing</strong> of the scheduler.</li>\n<li><strong>Indeterminate program:</strong> a program with one or more race conditions, so its output <strong>varies from run to run</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Mutual exclusion:</strong> a guarantee that if one thread is in the critical section, <strong>all others are kept out</strong>. A lock provides it.</li>\n</ul>\n"},{"id":"osn/endsem/endsem-practice-post-midsem-extras#p-x6","u":"osn/endsem/endsem-practice-post-midsem-extras.html#p-x6","l":"X6","s":"","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"endsem","c":"Endsem practice: post-midsem extras","q":"<p id=\"p-x6\"><strong>X6.</strong> File descriptors and links (L18 s6-22). A 100-byte file. <code>fd = open(&quot;f&quot;, O_RDONLY); read(fd, buf, 10);</code> then <code>fork()</code>. The child calls <code>read(fd, buf, 20)</code> and exits. The parent waits, then calls <code>lseek(fd, 0, SEEK_CUR)</code>. (a) What does it return, and why? [3] (b) What if the child had opened the file again itself? [2] (c) Run <code>echo x &gt; a; ln a b; ln -s a s; rm a</code>. Give the link count of b, and say what <code>cat b</code> and <code>cat s</code> do. [3] (d) How do you update a file atomically? [2]</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(a) It returns 30.</strong> Each process has a <strong>file-descriptor table</strong>, whose entries point to a <strong>system-wide open-file table</strong>. After <code>fork</code>, the parent's and child's fd 3 point to the <strong>same open-file-table entry</strong>, which holds the <strong>current offset</strong>. The offset moves 0 → 10 (parent's read) → 30 (child's read of 20). <code>lseek(fd, 0, SEEK_CUR)</code> just reports the current offset, so it returns <strong>30</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>(b)</strong> A separate <code>open()</code> creates a <strong>new</strong> open-file-table entry with its <strong>own offset</strong> (starting at 0). The child's reads would then not move the parent's offset, and the parent would still report <strong>10</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>(c)</strong> <code>echo x &gt; a</code> creates inode with link count 1. <code>ln a b</code> adds a second name for the <strong>same inode</strong>: count = 2. <code>ln -s a s</code> creates a new file whose data is the path &quot;a&quot; (it does not change the count). <code>rm a</code> is <code>unlink(&quot;a&quot;)</code>: count drops to <strong>1</strong>. <code>cat b</code> prints <strong>x</strong>, because the inode and data still exist. <code>cat s</code> <strong>fails</strong> (No such file or directory): the symlink holds the <em>path</em> &quot;a&quot;, which no longer exists. It is a <strong>dangling symlink</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>(d) Atomic update:</strong> write the new contents to a temporary file <code>f.tmp</code>, call <strong><code>fsync()</code></strong> so the data is on disk, then <strong><code>rename(&quot;f.tmp&quot;, &quot;f&quot;)</code></strong>. <code>rename</code> is <strong>atomic</strong>, so after a crash the file is either completely the old version or completely the new one, never half-written.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/endsem/endsem-practice-post-midsem-extras#p-x7","u":"osn/endsem/endsem-practice-post-midsem-extras.html#p-x7","l":"X7","s":"","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"endsem","c":"Endsem practice: post-midsem extras","q":"<p id=\"p-x7\"><strong>X7.</strong> Dining philosophers (L17 s23-25). 5 philosophers and 5 forks. Each runs <code>sem_wait(left); sem_wait(right); eat; sem_post(right); sem_post(left);</code>. (a) Show the deadlock, and name the four deadlock conditions. [5] (b) Give the standard fix and the condition it breaks. [3] (c) Name one other fix. [2]</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(a)</strong> If all five philosophers pick up their <strong>left</strong> fork at the same moment, each holds one fork and waits for the <strong>right</strong> fork, which is held by the neighbour. Everyone waits for the next, in a cycle: <strong>deadlock</strong>. The four necessary conditions: <strong>(1) mutual exclusion</strong> (a fork is used by one philosopher at a time), <strong>(2) hold and wait</strong> (a philosopher holds one fork while waiting for another), <strong>(3) no preemption</strong> (a fork cannot be taken away), <strong>(4) circular wait</strong> (each waits for a fork held by the next in a cycle).</p>\n<p><strong>(b) Fix: break the dependency.</strong> Let <strong>one philosopher (the last one) pick up the right fork first</strong>, then the left. The forks are then acquired in a consistent global order, so the cycle cannot form. This breaks <strong>circular wait</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>(c) Another fix:</strong> <strong>admission control</strong>: a semaphore initialised to 4 lets at most 4 philosophers try to eat at once, so at least one can always get both forks. (Or pick up both forks atomically under one lock, which breaks hold and wait.)</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/endsem/endsem-practice-post-midsem-extras#p-x8","u":"osn/endsem/endsem-practice-post-midsem-extras.html#p-x8","l":"X8","s":"","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"endsem","c":"Endsem practice: post-midsem extras","q":"<p id=\"p-x8\"><strong>X8.</strong> Lock implementations (L15 s15-33). (a) Write CompareAndSwap and a spin lock using it. [3] (b) Write a lock using LoadLinked and StoreConditional. [3] (c) What is priority inversion, and how is it fixed? [4]</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(a)</strong> CompareAndSwap atomically compares <code>*ptr</code> with <code>expected</code> and, if equal, writes <code>new</code>; it returns the old value:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-c\">int CompareAndSwap(int *ptr, int expected, int new) {\n    int actual = *ptr;\n    if (actual == expected) *ptr = new;\n    return actual;          // all of this executes atomically\n}\nvoid lock(lock_t *l)   { while (CompareAndSwap(&amp;l-&gt;flag, 0, 1) == 1) ; }   // spin until we swapped 0 to 1\nvoid unlock(lock_t *l) { l-&gt;flag = 0; }\n</code></pre>\n<p><strong>(b)</strong> <code>LoadLinked</code> loads the value; <code>StoreConditional</code> stores only if <strong>no other write to that address has happened since the load-linked</strong>, returning 1 on success and 0 on failure:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-c\">void lock(lock_t *l) {\n    while (1) {\n        while (LoadLinked(&amp;l-&gt;flag) == 1) ;               // spin while held\n        if (StoreConditional(&amp;l-&gt;flag, 1) == 1) return;   // fails if another thread wrote in between; retry\n    }\n}\n</code></pre>\n<p><strong>(c) Priority inversion:</strong> a low-priority thread L holds a lock that a high-priority thread H needs. H waits for L, but a <strong>medium-priority</strong> thread M keeps preempting L, so L cannot finish and release the lock: H is effectively blocked by M. (On one CPU with a spin lock, H can spin forever.) <strong>Fixes:</strong> <strong>priority inheritance</strong> (L temporarily runs at H's priority until it releases the lock), or avoid spinning (use sleeping locks).</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/endsem/endsem-practice-post-midsem-extras#p-x9","u":"osn/endsem/endsem-practice-post-midsem-extras.html#p-x9","l":"X9","s":"","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"endsem","c":"Endsem practice: post-midsem extras","q":"<p id=\"p-x9\"><strong>X9.</strong> Hybrid paging and segmentation (L13 s8-11). 32-bit VAs and 4 KB pages. The top 2 bits pick the segment (01 code, 10 heap, 11 stack, 00 unused), then an 18-bit VPN, then a 12-bit offset. Each segment has a base register (physical address of its page table) and a bounds register (number of valid pages). Code bounds = 3, heap base = 0x2000 and heap bounds = 10, with 4-byte PTEs. (a) Where is the PTE for VA 0x80001234? [3] (b) What happens on VA 0x40003ABC? [2] (c) The process uses 3 code pages, 10 heap pages and 2 stack pages. Compare the page-table memory with a linear table. [3] (d) Give two drawbacks of the hybrid. [2]</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(a)</strong> 0x80001234 = 1000 0000 0000 0000 0001 0010 0011 0100. Top 2 bits = <strong>10 = heap</strong>. VPN = (VA &amp; VPN_MASK) &gt;&gt; 12 = <strong>1</strong>, offset = 0x234. Check: VPN 1 &lt; heap bounds 10, so valid. <strong>AddressOfPTE = Base[heap] + VPN × sizeof(PTE) = 0x2000 + 1 × 4 = 0x2004.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>(b)</strong> 0x40003ABC: top 2 bits = 01 = code; VPN = 3, offset = 0xABC. Code bounds = 3, so valid VPNs are 0, 1 and 2. VPN 3 is <strong>out of bounds</strong>, so the hardware raises an <strong>exception</strong> and the OS likely kills the process.</p>\n<p><strong>(c)</strong> A linear table has 2^20 entries × 4 B = <strong>4 MB</strong>. The hybrid keeps only the valid pages' PTEs: (3 + 10 + 2) × 4 B = <strong>60 B</strong> in total. The unused region between heap and stack costs <strong>nothing</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>(d)</strong> (1) It still <strong>needs segmentation</strong>, which assumes a particular pattern of address-space use (for example, a large sparse heap still wastes table space). (2) Page tables are now <strong>variable-sized</strong>, so finding memory for them brings <strong>external fragmentation</strong> back.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/endsem/endsem-practice-post-midsem-extras#p-x10","u":"osn/endsem/endsem-practice-post-midsem-extras.html#p-x10","l":"X10","s":"","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"endsem","c":"Endsem practice: post-midsem extras","q":"<p id=\"p-x10\"><strong>X10.</strong> Inverted tables, swapping, and the L13 mechanisms. (a) What is an inverted page table, and how big is it for 4 GB of RAM, 4 KB frames and 8-byte entries? [4] (b) Why does lookup need a hash? [2] (c) On a TLB miss, what are the three cases the OS or hardware can find? [4]</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(a)</strong> An inverted page table has <strong>one entry per physical frame</strong> for the <strong>whole system</strong> (not one table per process). Each entry records which <strong>(process, VPN)</strong> currently uses that frame. 4 GB / 4 KB = 2^20 frames × 8 B = <strong>8 MB in total</strong>, regardless of the number of processes. (A per-process linear table would need 4 MB for each process.)</p>\n<p><strong>(b)</strong> Translation starts from (PID, VPN), but the table is indexed by frame number. Searching 2^20 entries linearly on each TLB miss is far too slow, so a <strong>hash table on (PID, VPN)</strong> points to the right entry.</p>\n<p><strong>(c) Three cases on a TLB miss:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>The PTE is <strong>valid and present</strong>: install the translation in the TLB and retry (a hit now).</li>\n<li>The PTE is <strong>valid but not present</strong>: a <strong>page fault</strong>; the OS brings the page in from swap, updates the PTE and retries.</li>\n<li>The PTE is <strong>not valid</strong>: an illegal access, so a <strong>segmentation fault</strong>; the OS kills the process.</li>\n</ol>\n"},{"id":"osn/endsem/endsem-practice-pre-midsem-problems#p-p1","u":"osn/endsem/endsem-practice-pre-midsem-problems.html#p-p1","l":"P1","s":"","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"endsem","c":"Endsem practice: pre-midsem problems","q":"<p id=\"p-p1\"><strong>P1.</strong> (a) What does this print, and in what order? [4] (b) Define zombie and orphan. What does xv6's exit() do with a dying process's children? [3] (c) Why are fork and exec separate calls? [3]</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-c\">int main() {\n    int x = 1;\n    if (fork() == 0) {\n        x++;\n        if (fork() == 0) { x++; printf(&quot;%d\\n&quot;, x); exit(0); }\n        wait(NULL); printf(&quot;%d\\n&quot;, x);\n    } else { wait(NULL); printf(&quot;%d\\n&quot;, x); }\n}\n</code></pre>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(a) Trace.</strong> After the first fork there are the <strong>parent</strong> (x = 1) and the <strong>child</strong> (x = 1). The parent goes to the <code>else</code> branch and waits. The child increments x to 2, then forks a <strong>grandchild</strong> (x = 2). The grandchild increments x to 3, <strong>prints 3</strong> and exits. The child's <code>wait</code> returns and it <strong>prints 2</strong>. The parent's <code>wait</code> returns and it <strong>prints 1</strong>.</p>\n<p>Output: <strong>3, 2, 1</strong>, always in this order, because each <code>wait</code> forces the parent to print after its child. Each process has its <strong>own copy</strong> of x (fork copies the address space), so a change in a child never affects the parent.</p>\n<p><strong>(b)</strong> A <strong>zombie</strong> is a process that has <strong>exited</strong> but whose <strong>parent has not yet called wait()</strong>: its process-table entry and exit status remain until the parent collects them. An <strong>orphan</strong> is a process whose <strong>parent has exited first</strong>; the OS reparents it to <strong>init</strong>. In xv6, <code>exit()</code> closes the files, sets the process state to <strong>ZOMBIE</strong>, gives the dying process's <strong>children to init</strong> (which waits for them), and <strong>wakes up the parent</strong>. A zombie that is never waited for stays in the table forever (a leak).</p>\n<p><strong>(c)</strong> Keeping them separate lets the shell run code <strong>between fork and exec</strong>, in the child, to <strong>change the environment</strong> without the program knowing: for <code>ls &gt; out.txt</code>, the child does <code>close(1); open(&quot;out.txt&quot;, ...)</code> (the new file gets fd 1), then <code>exec(&quot;ls&quot;)</code>. The same pattern sets up <strong>pipes</strong> and changes the working directory. The program that is then exec'd inherits those fds, so <strong>I/O redirection</strong> needs no support inside the program.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/endsem/endsem-practice-pre-midsem-problems#p-p2","u":"osn/endsem/endsem-practice-pre-midsem-problems.html#p-p2","l":"P2","s":"","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"endsem","c":"Endsem practice: pre-midsem problems","q":"<p id=\"p-p2\"><strong>P2.</strong> Limited direct execution (L04). (a) List the hardware and OS steps when a timer interrupt switches from process A to process B. [6] (b) Which registers are saved twice, by whom, and where? [2] (c) Why does the trap table have to be set up at boot, and why is a system-call <em>number</em> used instead of an address? [2]</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(a) Steps:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Hardware:</strong> the timer interrupt fires. The hardware saves A's <strong>user registers</strong> (including the PC) onto A's <strong>kernel stack</strong>, switches to <strong>kernel mode</strong>, and jumps to the <strong>trap handler</strong> found in the trap table.</li>\n<li><strong>OS:</strong> the handler decides to switch to B and calls <code>switch()</code> (the scheduler).</li>\n<li><strong>OS:</strong> <code>switch()</code> saves A's <strong>kernel registers</strong> into A's process structure (its <code>struct context</code>).</li>\n<li><strong>OS:</strong> it restores B's kernel registers from B's process structure, which also <strong>switches to B's kernel stack</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>OS:</strong> the OS executes <strong>return-from-trap</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Hardware:</strong> it restores B's user registers from B's kernel stack, switches to <strong>user mode</strong> and jumps to B's PC. B runs as if it had never stopped.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>(b)</strong> The <strong>user registers</strong> are saved <strong>by the hardware</strong> onto the <strong>kernel stack</strong> (in xv6, the trapframe). The <strong>kernel registers</strong> are saved <strong>by the OS</strong> (in <code>swtch()</code>) into the <strong>process structure</strong> (xv6: ra, sp, s0 to s11 in <code>struct context</code>).</p>\n<p><strong>(c)</strong> The trap table is installed <strong>at boot, in kernel mode, with a privileged instruction</strong>, so the hardware knows where the kernel's handlers are and user code cannot redirect traps to its own code. User code gives a <strong>number</strong> (not an address) so that it can only reach <strong>kernel code the OS has chosen</strong>: the kernel validates the number and looks up the handler itself. An address would let a program jump into the middle of arbitrary kernel code. This is a <strong>protection</strong> mechanism.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/endsem/endsem-practice-pre-midsem-problems#p-p3","u":"osn/endsem/endsem-practice-pre-midsem-problems.html#p-p3","l":"P3","s":"","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"endsem","c":"Endsem practice: pre-midsem problems","q":"<p id=\"p-p3\"><strong>P3.</strong> Scheduling. Jobs: A arrives at 0 and needs 6; B arrives at 2 and needs 3; C at 4 needs 8; D at 6 needs 2. Find the average turnaround and response times for FIFO, SJF (non-preemptive), STCF and RR (q = 2; a new arrival joins the queue before the job that was just preempted). [10]</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Formulas.</strong> Turnaround = completion − arrival. Response = first run − arrival.</p>\n<p><strong>FIFO:</strong> A 0–6, B 6–9, C 9–17, D 17–19. Turnaround: A 6, B 7, C 13, D 13, average = 39 / 4 = <strong>9.75</strong>. Response: A 0, B 4, C 5, D 11, average = 20 / 4 = <strong>5</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>SJF (non-preemptive):</strong> at 0 only A is there, so A 0–6. At 6, B (3), C (8) and D (2) are waiting, so the shortest is D: D 6–8, then B 8–11, then C 11–19. Turnaround: A 6, B 9, C 15, D 2, average = 32 / 4 = <strong>8</strong>. Response: A 0, B 6, C 7, D 0, average = 13 / 4 = <strong>3.25</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>STCF (preemptive SJF):</strong> A runs 0–2. At 2, B (3) is shorter than A's remaining 4, so B runs 2–5 (C arrives at 4 with 8, not shorter than B's remaining 1). At 5, A (4) runs; at 6, D (2) is shorter than A's remaining 3, so D 6–8. Then A 8–11, then C 11–19. Completion: A 11, B 5, C 19, D 8. Turnaround: A 11, B 3, C 15, D 2, average = 31 / 4 = <strong>7.75</strong>. Response: A 0, B 0, C 7, D 0, average = 7 / 4 = <strong>1.75</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>RR (q = 2):</strong> the queue changes as follows (arrivals join ahead of the preempted job).</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Time</th>\n<th>Runs</th>\n<th>Queue after (front first)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>0–2</td>\n<td>A</td>\n<td>B arrives at 2, goes before A: [B, A]</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2–4</td>\n<td>B</td>\n<td>C arrives at 4, goes before B: [A, C, B]</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4–6</td>\n<td>A</td>\n<td>D arrives at 6, goes before A: [C, B, D, A]</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6–8</td>\n<td>C</td>\n<td>[B, D, A, C]</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>8–9</td>\n<td>B (finishes)</td>\n<td>[D, A, C]</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>9–11</td>\n<td>D (finishes)</td>\n<td>[A, C]</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>11–13</td>\n<td>A (finishes)</td>\n<td>[C]</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>13–19</td>\n<td>C (finishes)</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>Completion: A 13, B 9, C 19, D 11. Turnaround: A 13, B 7, C 15, D 5, average = 40 / 4 = <strong>10</strong>. Response: A 0, B 0, C 2, D 3, average = 5 / 4 = <strong>1.25</strong>.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Policy</th>\n<th>Avg turnaround</th>\n<th>Avg response</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>FIFO</td>\n<td>9.75</td>\n<td>5</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>SJF</td>\n<td>8</td>\n<td>3.25</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>STCF</td>\n<td><strong>7.75</strong></td>\n<td>1.75</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>RR</td>\n<td>10</td>\n<td><strong>1.25</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> STCF is best for turnaround, RR is best for response, and the two goals trade off.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/endsem/endsem-practice-pre-midsem-problems#p-p4","u":"osn/endsem/endsem-practice-pre-midsem-problems.html#p-p4","l":"P4","s":"","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"endsem","c":"Endsem practice: pre-midsem problems","q":"<p id=\"p-p4\"><strong>P4.</strong> MLFQ with three queues: Q2 (top, quantum 10 ms), Q1 (20 ms) and Q0 (round robin), with no boost. A is CPU-bound and needs 100 ms, arriving at 0. B needs 20 ms and arrives at 30. (a) Trace the schedule and give both turnaround times. [4] (b) Show how a job can game Rules 4a/4b, and give the fix. [3] (c) Why is a priority boost needed, and what goes wrong if it is too frequent? [3]</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(a) Rules used:</strong> a new job starts at the top queue; a job that uses its whole allotment at a level moves down; the highest-priority non-empty queue runs.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Time</th>\n<th>Event</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>0–10</td>\n<td>A runs in Q2, uses its 10 ms, moves to Q1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>10–30</td>\n<td>A runs in Q1, uses its 20 ms, moves to Q0</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>30</td>\n<td>B arrives (needs 20 ms) and enters Q2, which has higher priority than A's Q0</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>30–40</td>\n<td>B runs in Q2, uses 10 ms, moves to Q1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>40–50</td>\n<td>B runs in Q1, the remaining 10 ms: <strong>B finishes at 50</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>50–120</td>\n<td>A runs in Q0 with 100 − 10 − 20 = 70 ms left: <strong>A finishes at 120</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Turnaround: B = 50 − 30 = 20 ms; A = 120 ms.</strong> The short job B overtakes the long job A, so MLFQ approximates SJF without knowing job lengths.</p>\n<p><strong>(b) Gaming (old Rule 4b):</strong> the old rules let a job that <strong>gave up the CPU before its slice ended stay at the same priority</strong>. A job can issue a pointless I/O just before the end of each time slice (for example, at 9.9 ms of a 10 ms slice), so it is never demoted and monopolises the top queue. <strong>Fix (new Rule 4):</strong> account for the <strong>total CPU time used at a level</strong>, regardless of how many times the job gave up the CPU, and demote it once its allotment is used up.</p>\n<p><strong>(c)</strong> Without a boost: <strong>(1) starvation:</strong> if there are many interactive jobs, long jobs sink to the bottom and never run; <strong>(2) behaviour change:</strong> a CPU-bound job that later becomes interactive stays stuck at the bottom. <strong>Rule 5:</strong> after some period S, <strong>move all jobs to the top queue</strong> (this guarantees progress and re-evaluates behaviour). <strong>If S is too small,</strong> CPU-bound jobs keep returning to the top and take CPU time from interactive jobs, so MLFQ degrades towards plain round robin. If S is too large, long jobs still starve between boosts. S is a &quot;voo-doo constant&quot; that must be tuned.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/endsem/endsem-practice-pre-midsem-problems#p-p5","u":"osn/endsem/endsem-practice-pre-midsem-problems.html#p-p5","l":"P5","s":"","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"endsem","c":"Endsem practice: pre-midsem problems","q":"<p id=\"p-p5\"><strong>P5.</strong> CFS with sched_latency = 48 ms and min_granularity = 6 ms. A and B have nice 0 (weight 1024), and C has nice −5 (weight 3121). (a) Give each process's time slice. [4] (b) Show how much each one's vruntime grows over one period. [3] (c) With 12 processes at nice 0, what slice does each get? [2] (d) Where does CFS put a process waking from a long sleep? [1]</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(a) Formula:</strong> <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.8444em;vertical-align:-0.15em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord text\"><span class=\"mord\">slice</span></span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.3361em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"katex-sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\" style=\"margin-right:0.0315em;\">k</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:2.0436em;vertical-align:-0.936em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mopen nulldelimiter\"></span><span class=\"mfrac\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:1.1076em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.314em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mop op-symbol small-op\" style=\"position:relative;top:0em;\">∑</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.1667em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0269em;\">w</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.3117em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:-0.0269em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"katex-sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\">i</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.23em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"frac-line\" style=\"border-bottom-width:0.04em;\"></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.677em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0269em;\">w</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.3361em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:-0.0269em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"katex-sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\" style=\"margin-right:0.0315em;\">k</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.936em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mclose nulldelimiter\"></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">×</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:1.0044em;vertical-align:-0.31em;\"></span><span class=\"mord text\"><span class=\"mord\">sched_latency</span></span></span></span></span>. Total weight = 1024 + 1024 + 3121 = 5169. A and B: 48 × 1024 / 5169 = <strong>9.51 ms</strong> each. C: 48 × 3121 / 5169 = <strong>28.98 ms</strong>. (They add up to 48 ms.)</p>\n<p><strong>(b) Formula:</strong> <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">Δ</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0359em;\">v</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0278em;\">r</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">u</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">n</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">t</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">im</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">e</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:2.1574em;vertical-align:-0.836em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mopen nulldelimiter\"></span><span class=\"mfrac\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:1.3214em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.314em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0269em;\">w</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.3361em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:-0.0269em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"katex-sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\" style=\"margin-right:0.0315em;\">k</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.23em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"frac-line\" style=\"border-bottom-width:0.04em;\"></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.677em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord\">1024</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.836em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mclose nulldelimiter\"></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">×</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.6679em;\"></span><span class=\"mord text\"><span class=\"mord\">runtime</span></span></span></span></span>. A and B: 9.51 × 1024 / 1024 = <strong>9.51</strong>. C: 28.98 × 1024 / 3121 = <strong>9.51</strong>. <strong>They are equal.</strong> A higher-weight process gets a longer slice but its vruntime grows more slowly per ms, so when CFS keeps all vruntimes level (always running the one with the <strong>lowest vruntime</strong>), the heavier process receives proportionally more CPU.</p>\n<p><strong>(c)</strong> 48 / 12 = 4 ms, which is below min_granularity, so each process gets <strong>6 ms</strong> (the round then takes 12 × 6 = 72 ms).</p>\n<p><strong>(d)</strong> Its vruntime is set to the <strong>minimum vruntime in the red-black tree</strong>, so it neither monopolises the CPU (with a tiny vruntime) nor is penalised for sleeping.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/endsem/endsem-practice-pre-midsem-problems#p-p6","u":"osn/endsem/endsem-practice-pre-midsem-problems.html#p-p6","l":"P6","s":"","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"endsem","c":"Endsem practice: pre-midsem problems","q":"<p id=\"p-p6\"><strong>P6.</strong> Multiprocessor scheduling (L07). (a) What is cache affinity, and how does single-queue scheduling (SQMS) hurt it? [3] (b) Give two scalability problems with SQMS. [2] (c) Under MQMS, A is alone on CPU 0, while B and D share CPU 1. What is the problem, and how is it fixed? [3] (d) Why is cache coherence needed, and how does bus snooping provide it? [2]</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(a)</strong> <strong>Cache affinity:</strong> a process run again on the <strong>same CPU</strong> finds some of its data still in that CPU's cache (and TLB), so it runs faster. SQMS puts all jobs in one queue and each CPU takes whatever job is next, so a job <strong>bounces between CPUs</strong> and finds a cold cache each time.</p>\n<p><strong>(b)</strong> (1) The single queue needs a <strong>lock</strong>, and as the number of CPUs grows the lock becomes <strong>heavily contended</strong> (CPUs spend more time waiting for it), limiting scalability. (2) Poor affinity, as in (a); fixing it needs extra mechanisms that make it more complex.</p>\n<p><strong>(c)</strong> <strong>Load imbalance:</strong> A gets a whole CPU, while B and D get only 50% each. <strong>Fix: migration.</strong> The OS moves jobs between queues, for example by <strong>work stealing</strong>: a CPU with a short queue occasionally <strong>looks at another queue</strong> and steals a job. Looking too often costs overhead; too rarely leaves the imbalance.</p>\n<p><strong>(d)</strong> Each CPU caches memory, so a write on one CPU can leave <strong>stale copies</strong> in the other caches. With <strong>bus snooping</strong>, each cache <strong>watches the shared bus</strong>, and when it sees a write to an address it holds, it <strong>invalidates</strong> (or updates) its own copy.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/endsem/endsem-practice-pre-midsem-problems#p-p7","u":"osn/endsem/endsem-practice-pre-midsem-problems.html#p-p7","l":"P7","s":"","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"endsem","c":"Endsem practice: pre-midsem problems","q":"<p id=\"p-p7\"><strong>P7.</strong> Network layers and devices (L08). (a) Name the TCP/IP layers, the data unit at each, and the address each uses. [4] (b) Which header does a hub, a switch and a router each look at? [3] (c) A host sends a 1000-byte HTTP message over TCP/IP and Ethernet. Show the encapsulation, assuming 20-byte TCP and IP headers and an 18-byte Ethernet header and trailer. [3]</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(a)</strong></p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Layer</th>\n<th>Data unit</th>\n<th>Address used</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Application</td>\n<td>message</td>\n<td>(hostname, port in the app)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Transport</td>\n<td>segment (TCP) / datagram (UDP)</td>\n<td><strong>port number</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Network</td>\n<td>packet (datagram)</td>\n<td><strong>IP address</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Link</td>\n<td>frame</td>\n<td><strong>MAC address</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Physical</td>\n<td>bits</td>\n<td>–</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>(b)</strong> A <strong>hub</strong> works at layer 1 and reads <strong>no header</strong>: it repeats the bits to every port. A <strong>switch</strong> works at layer 2: it reads the <strong>MAC address</strong>, learns which port leads to which MAC, and forwards the frame only to the right port. A <strong>router</strong> works at layer 3: it reads the <strong>IP address</strong> and forwards the packet towards the destination network using its routing table.</p>\n<p><strong>(c)</strong> Each layer wraps what it receives from above: 1000 B of data + 20 B TCP header = <strong>1020 B segment</strong>; + 20 B IP header = <strong>1040 B packet</strong>; + 18 B Ethernet header and trailer = <strong>1058 B frame</strong>. The receiver strips the headers in reverse order (<strong>decapsulation</strong>).</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/endsem/endsem-practice-pre-midsem-problems#p-p8","u":"osn/endsem/endsem-practice-pre-midsem-problems.html#p-p8","l":"P8","s":"","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"endsem","c":"Endsem practice: pre-midsem problems","q":"<p id=\"p-p8\"><strong>P8.</strong> TCP (L09). The client ISN is 1000 and the server ISN is 5000. (a) Give the seq and ack numbers of the three-way handshake. [3] (b) The client then sends 3 segments of 100 bytes. Give their seq numbers. The second segment is lost. What ACKs does the server send, and when does fast retransmit fire? [4] (c) Describe the 4-way close, and explain why TIME_WAIT exists. [3]</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(a)</strong> SYN uses one sequence number.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Segment</th>\n<th>seq</th>\n<th>ack</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1. Client → server: SYN</td>\n<td>1000</td>\n<td>–</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2. Server → client: SYN-ACK</td>\n<td>5000</td>\n<td>1001</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3. Client → server: ACK</td>\n<td>1001</td>\n<td>5001</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>(b)</strong> Data segments: seq = <strong>1001, 1101, 1201</strong> (each 100 bytes). ACK numbers are <strong>cumulative</strong> and name the <strong>next byte expected</strong>. The server ACKs <strong>1101</strong> after segment 1. Segment 2 (seq 1101) is lost. When segment 3 arrives out of order, the server can only ACK what it has in order, so it sends <strong>ACK 1101 again</strong> (a <strong>duplicate ACK</strong>). <strong>Fast retransmit</strong> fires when the sender receives <strong>three duplicate ACKs</strong> for 1101 (that is, the original plus 3 duplicates, which needs more segments arriving after the gap): the sender then retransmits segment 2 <strong>without waiting for the timeout</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>(c)</strong> <strong>Close:</strong> the side that closes first sends <strong>FIN</strong>; the other side replies <strong>ACK</strong>; later the other side sends its own <strong>FIN</strong>; the first side replies <strong>ACK</strong>. The side that sent the last ACK then enters <strong>TIME_WAIT</strong> for 2 × MSL (maximum segment lifetime). <strong>Why:</strong> (1) if the final ACK is lost, the other side will retransmit its FIN, and the waiting side can still answer; (2) it lets <strong>old delayed segments</strong> from this connection expire, so they cannot be mistaken for part of a <strong>new connection</strong> reusing the same ports.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/endsem/endsem-practice-pre-midsem-problems#p-p9","u":"osn/endsem/endsem-practice-pre-midsem-problems.html#p-p9","l":"P9","s":"","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"endsem","c":"Endsem practice: pre-midsem problems","q":"<p id=\"p-p9\"><strong>P9.</strong> TCP timers and congestion control. (a) EstimatedRTT = 80 ms and DevRTT = 8 ms. A SampleRTT of 120 ms arrives. Find the new EstimatedRTT, DevRTT and TimeoutInterval, with α = 0.125 and β = 0.25. [4] (b) ssthresh = 8 and cwnd starts at 1. List cwnd for rounds 1 to 8. Three duplicate ACKs arrive at the end of round 8. Give rounds 9 to 11. Then there is a timeout at the end of round 11. Give rounds 12 to 15. [6]</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(a)</strong> (K&amp;R order: update EstimatedRTT first, then DevRTT using the new estimate.)</p>\n<ul>\n<li>EstimatedRTT = 0.875 × 80 + 0.125 × 120 = 70 + 15 = <strong>85 ms</strong>.</li>\n<li>DevRTT = 0.75 × 8 + 0.25 × |120 − 85| = 6 + 8.75 = <strong>14.75 ms</strong>.</li>\n<li>TimeoutInterval = EstimatedRTT + 4 × DevRTT = 85 + 59 = <strong>144 ms</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>(b)</strong> <strong>Slow start</strong> doubles cwnd each round until it reaches ssthresh; then <strong>congestion avoidance</strong> adds 1 MSS per round. A loss by <strong>3 duplicate ACKs</strong> sets ssthresh = cwnd / 2 and cwnd = ssthresh (fast recovery). A <strong>timeout</strong> sets ssthresh = cwnd / 2 and cwnd = 1 (back to slow start).</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Round</th>\n<th>1</th>\n<th>2</th>\n<th>3</th>\n<th>4</th>\n<th>5</th>\n<th>6</th>\n<th>7</th>\n<th>8</th>\n<th>9</th>\n<th>10</th>\n<th>11</th>\n<th>12</th>\n<th>13</th>\n<th>14</th>\n<th>15</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>cwnd</td>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>8</td>\n<td>9</td>\n<td>10</td>\n<td>11</td>\n<td>12</td>\n<td>6</td>\n<td>7</td>\n<td>8</td>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>5</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>Rounds 1–4: slow start (1, 2, 4, 8); cwnd reaches ssthresh = 8 at round 4.</li>\n<li>Rounds 5–8: congestion avoidance (+1 per round): 9, 10, 11, 12.</li>\n<li>End of round 8, <strong>3 duplicate ACKs</strong>: ssthresh = 12 / 2 = <strong>6</strong>, cwnd = <strong>6</strong> (round 9), then 7, 8 (rounds 10, 11).</li>\n<li>End of round 11, <strong>timeout</strong>: ssthresh = 8 / 2 = <strong>4</strong>, cwnd = <strong>1</strong> (round 12). Slow start: 1, 2, 4 (reaches ssthresh = 4), then congestion avoidance: 5.</li>\n</ul>\n"},{"id":"osn/endsem/endsem-practice-pre-midsem-problems#p-p10","u":"osn/endsem/endsem-practice-pre-midsem-problems.html#p-p10","l":"P10","s":"","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"endsem","c":"Endsem practice: pre-midsem problems","q":"<p id=\"p-p10\"><strong>P10.</strong> Checksums and sockets (L09). (a) Compute the UDP checksum of the 16-bit words 0x1234, 0xF00F and 0x0F0F. [4] (b) List the socket calls for a TCP server and a TCP client, and say which ones block. [4] (c) Why does accept() return a new socket? [2]</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(a) Method:</strong> add the words with <strong>end-around carry</strong> (any carry out of bit 16 is added back into the low bits), then take the <strong>ones' complement</strong>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>0x1234 + 0xF00F = 0x10243. The carry (1) wraps around: 0x0243 + 1 = <strong>0x0244</strong>.</li>\n<li>0x0244 + 0x0F0F = <strong>0x1153</strong> (no carry).</li>\n<li>Ones' complement: 0xFFFF − 0x1153 = <strong>0xEEAC</strong>. This is the checksum.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><em>Check at the receiver:</em> 0x1153 + 0xEEAC = 0xFFFF, so no error is detected.</p>\n<p><strong>(b)</strong> <strong>Server:</strong> <code>socket()</code>, <code>bind()</code>, <code>listen()</code>, <code>accept()</code> (<strong>blocks</strong> until a client connects), <code>recv()</code> (<strong>blocks</strong> until data arrives), <code>send()</code>, <code>close()</code>. <strong>Client:</strong> <code>socket()</code>, <code>connect()</code> (<strong>blocks</strong> during the handshake), <code>send()</code>, <code>recv()</code> (<strong>blocks</strong>), <code>close()</code>. UDP has no listen, accept or connect: it uses <code>sendto()</code> and <code>recvfrom()</code> (which blocks). A blocking call puts the process to <strong>sleep</strong>, so it is a <strong>context-switch point</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>(c)</strong> The <strong>listening socket</strong> stays open to accept further connections. Each accepted connection gets its <strong>own new socket</strong>, identified by the <strong>4-tuple</strong> (source IP, source port, destination IP, destination port). That is how one server on port 80 can serve many clients at the same time: every connection has its own socket and state.</p>\n"},{"id":"osn/endsem/endsem-practice-pre-midsem-problems#p-p11","u":"osn/endsem/endsem-practice-pre-midsem-problems.html#p-p11","l":"P11","s":"","tags":["Other"],"hard":false,"d":"endsem","c":"Endsem practice: pre-midsem problems","q":"<p id=\"p-p11\"><strong>P11.</strong> Process states (L02 s12-15). P0 needs 2 units of CPU, then issues an I/O that takes 3 units, then needs 1 more unit of CPU. P1 needs 4 units of CPU. P0 starts first. The OS switches to another process when the running one blocks, and a process that finishes its I/O becomes ready but does not preempt. (a) Give each process's state at times 1 to 7. [5] (b) How long would it take if the OS ran P1 only after P0 finished? [2] (c) Draw the state diagram, and say which transitions the OS triggers and which come from events. [3]</p>\n","a":"<p><em>Solution.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(a)</strong></p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Time unit</th>\n<th>P0</th>\n<th>P1</th>\n<th>Note</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>Running</td>\n<td>Ready</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>Running</td>\n<td>Ready</td>\n<td>P0 issues its I/O at the end of this unit</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Blocked</td>\n<td>Running</td>\n<td>OS switches to P1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>Blocked</td>\n<td>Running</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>Blocked</td>\n<td>Running</td>\n<td>P0's I/O completes at the end of this unit</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6</td>\n<td>Ready</td>\n<td>Running</td>\n<td>P1 finishes (its 4th unit); P0 is ready but does not preempt</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>7</td>\n<td>Running</td>\n<td>Done</td>\n<td>P0 runs its last unit and finishes</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>The CPU is busy for all 7 units, because P1's computation <strong>overlaps</strong> P0's I/O.</p>\n<p><strong>(b)</strong> If P1 ran only after P0 finished: P0 takes 2 + 3 (CPU idle during I/O) + 1 = 6 units, then P1 takes 4: <strong>10 units</strong>. Overlapping I/O with another process's computation saves 3 units.</p>\n<p><strong>(c) Transitions:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Ready → Running:</strong> the OS <strong>schedules</strong> the process.</li>\n<li><strong>Running → Ready:</strong> the OS <strong>deschedules</strong> it (for example, timer interrupt, or yield).</li>\n<li><strong>Running → Blocked:</strong> the process starts <strong>I/O</strong> (its own system call).</li>\n<li><strong>Blocked → Ready:</strong> the <strong>I/O completes</strong> (an interrupt).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>There is <strong>never a direct Blocked → Running</strong> edge: after the I/O completes the process must wait in the ready queue until the scheduler picks it.</p>\n"}];